window.YANBIAN_MOCK_EXAMS = [{"id": "CET6-1", "level": "CET6", "set": 1, "year": 2026, "month": 6, "title": "六级 2026年6月 第1套", "audio": "audio/mock/cet6_2026_1.mp3", "minutes": 40, "questions": [{"id": "CET6-1-Q1", "type": "listening", "no": 1, "q": "", "opts": ["How to run one's business successfully", "How to make progress in one's career", "Why few people can fully display their capabilities", "Why young people seldom get the expected promotion"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[1]男士提到，今天，我们邀请了职业发展顾问露西·威廉姆斯来分享如何在职场中取得成功。(B)项是对录音中how to get ahead at work的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项中的\"经营企业\"在录音中未被提及，故排除；(C)项中的\"充分发挥自己的能力\"在录音中未被提及，故排除；(D)项是依据录音中Many of us never seem to get promoted设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是我们中的许多人似乎从未得到晋升，而并非特指年轻人很少获得晋升，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q2", "type": "listening", "no": 2, "q": "", "opts": ["To mix and interact with peers within the organization", "To restrict their connections exclusively to colleagues", "To limit communication only to their superiors", "To underestimate their role in the company"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[2]女士提到，人们所犯的一个错误是只和上司沟通。(C)项是对录音中to limit communication only to their bosses的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项中\"同龄人\"在录音中未提及，故排除；(B)项是根据录音中limit communication only to their bosses设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是只和老板交流，而不是只和同事交流，故排除；(D)项是根据录音中underestimate your colleagues设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是不要低估同事是很重要的，而不是低估他们在公司中的作用，且这并不是女士提到的人们犯的一个错误，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q3", "type": "listening", "no": 3, "q": "", "opts": ["Put in quality time", "Connect with managers", "Make greater efforts", "Demonstrate final results"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[3]女士提到，与其只是投入大量时间，不如确保你投入的是高质量的时间，而非单纯追求时间总量的增加。(A)项是对录音中putting in quality time的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] 录音中女士说的是，只和他们的上级交流是人们犯的一个错误，女士并未建议和管理者们沟通，故排除(B)项；(C)项是根据录音中trying to take on too much work设置的干扰项，但录音中女士说的是不建议承担过多的工作，故排除；(D)项是根据录音中demonstrated by the results设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是这些努力可通过取得的结果轻松证明，而不是女士建议展示最终的结果，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q4", "type": "listening", "no": 4, "q": "", "opts": ["To show their genius", "To gain due respect", "To guard against humiliation", "To avoid appearing arrogant"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[4]男士提到，但不言而喻，你不应该表现得像无所不知一样。否则，你会显得傲慢。(D)项是对录音中you'll appear arrogant的概括转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项是根据录音中show your genius设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是要谦虚，以后有机会展示天赋，并不是不应该表现得像无所不知一样的原因，且这句话并不是男士说的，故排除；(B)项是根据录音中gain their respect设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是通过帮助他人从而赢得尊重，这不是不应该表现得像无所不知的原因，故排除；(C)项\"为了防止遭受羞辱\"在录音中未被提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q5", "type": "listening", "no": 5, "q": "", "opts": ["Decide how to divide up the presentation tasks", "Investigate the available background research", "Complete the project design together", "Find a suitable topic for their article"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[5]女士提到，她认为他们首先需要决定如何分配演示任务。(A)项是对录音中divide up the presentation responsibilities的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (B)项是根据录音中background research设置的干扰项，但录音中男士提到他已经花了一个月完成了背景研究，由此可知，首先需要做的并不是调查背景研究，故排除；(C)项是根据录音中project design设置的干扰项，但\"项目设计\"只是对话者提到的演示内容展示的一个部分，而不是他们需要首先做的事情，故排除；(D)项中的\"找合适的题目\"在录音中未被提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q6", "type": "listening", "no": 6, "q": "", "opts": ["He wants to devote his time to other portions", "He believes the woman is better able to handle it", "He claims to have done most of the work already", "He finds the woman more meticulous about details"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[6]男士提到，你意思是让我这个承担了大部分工作任务的人，再承担大部分的演示汇报？背景研究那么细致，花了一个多月的时间呢。(C)项是对录音中the one who did the bulk of the task的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] \"将时间投入到其他部分\"在录音中未被提及，故排除(A)项；\"女士能更好地处理背景部分\"在录音中未被提及，故排除(B)项；(D)项是根据录音中meticulous一词设置的干扰项，但录音中提到的是背景研究是非常细致的，并不是女士更加细致，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q7", "type": "listening", "no": 7, "q": "", "opts": ["Questioning", "Complaining", "Arguing", "Worrying"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[7]女士提到，请别再抱怨了。(B)项是对录音中moaning的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)、(C)、(D)项在录音中均未被提及，故均排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q8", "type": "listening", "no": 8, "q": "", "opts": ["Toavoid sounding monotonous to the listeners", "Tomake the division of labor more reasonable", "To keep himself from being overburdened", "To bring their capabilities fully into play"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[8]男士提到，这可使我们交替呈现内容，从而避免让观众觉得内容单调乏味。(A)项是对录音中to avoid sounding monotonous to the audience的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] 二人实现分工均衡是整场对话的整体诉求，而非分配引言和结论这两个部分的直接目的，故排除(B)项；(C)、(D)项在录音中均未被提及，故均排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q9", "type": "listening", "no": 9, "q": "", "opts": ["Alter others'perceptions of the wearer", "Win others'admiration for the wearer", "Enhance the wearer's confidence", "Change the wearer's appearance"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[9]提到，它还可能改变他人对你的看法，这种影响可能是更好的，也可能是更坏的。(A)项是对录音中alter others' perceptions of you的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (B)项是根据录音中gain others' trust and admiration设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是对于女性来说，了解化妆如何影响他人对她的看法可能特别有用，而不是化妆本身的作用，故排除；(C)项中的\"增加自信\"在录音中未被提及，故排除；(D)项是根据change your appearance设置的干扰项，但这并不是苏格兰斯特灵大学的研究结果表明的化妆的作用，(D)项不符合题意，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q10", "type": "listening", "no": 10, "q": "", "opts": ["As being a greater threat", "As being of higher prestige", "As being more dominant", "As being more attractive"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[10]提到，研究显示，男性和女性均认为使用化妆品修饰后的面部更具吸引力。(D)项是对录音中as being more attractive的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项、(C)项是根据录音中being dominant and a potential threat设置的干扰项，但这仅是女性的看法，并不是男性和女性都持有此看法，故均排除；(B)项是根据as being higher in prestige设置的干扰项，但这仅为男性的观点，并非男女双方都持有此观点，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q11", "type": "listening", "no": 11, "q": "", "opts": ["It might influence her way of introducing herself", "It might impact her dressing style for the occasion", "It might affect her decision on applying cosmetics", "It might sway her view on the interviewers'questions"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[11]提到，在求职面试中，了解招聘委员会由男性还是女性组成，可能会影响女性候选人是否化妆的决定。(C)项是对录音中might influence a female candidate's decision about wearing makeup的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项、(B)项在录音中均未被提及，故均排除；(D)项是根据录音中interviewers' actions设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是面试官对化妆者的看法会影响其行为，并不是影响化妆者对面试官提问的看法，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q12", "type": "listening", "no": 12, "q": "", "opts": ["Those involving no self-recognition", "Those forcing us to clench our teeth", "Those resisting various temptations", "Those requiring no willpower at all"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[12]提到，你可能曾看到过一些广告，大肆宣传那些完全无需任何意志力即可改变习惯的方法。(D)项是对录音中methods...that require absolutely no willpower whatsoever的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项中的\"不涉及自我认知\"在录音中未被提及，故排除；(B)项和(C)项中提到的咬紧牙关和抵抗诱惑均为大部分人以为的有意志力的体现，并不是广告所宣扬的方法，故均排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q14", "type": "listening", "no": 14, "q": "", "opts": ["It equips us with an expert skill", "It exerts an enduring force on us", "It leads to a burning goal", "It begins with a bright idea"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[14]提到，令人惊讶的是，意志力往往始于一个想法——一个绝妙的想法，某种能够改善自身生活或帮助他人的方法。(D)项是对录音中begins with和a bright idea的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项是根据录音中an expert on willpower skills设置的干扰项，但录音中提到的是沃尔特·米切尔博士是意志力技能方面的专家，而不是意志力让人惊叹的内容，故排除；(B)项中的\"持久的影响\"在录音中未被提及，故排除；(C)项是根据录音中a burning goal设置的干扰项，但这是沃尔特·米切尔博士给\"一个绝妙的想法\"的命名，而不是令人惊叹的内容，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q15", "type": "listening", "no": 15, "q": "", "opts": ["Any cherished goal", "Any positive mindset", "Any personal instinct", "Any valued role model"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[15]提到，什么样的绝妙想法和激励因素能带来有效的意志力？事实上，任何你高度重视的目标都至关重要。(A)项是对录音中any goal that you value的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (B)项、(C)项在录音中均未被提及，故均排除；(D)项是根据录音中a role model设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是带来意志力的目标中包含成为子女的榜样，而不是榜样才能带来有效的意志力，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q16", "type": "listening", "no": 16, "q": "", "opts": ["It has long been criticized as unclear", "It has long been ignored by the public", "It has caused much panic among women", "It has resulted in different understandings"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[16]提到，部分原因在于，联邦政府于2004年首次发布的相关建议长期以来一直被批评为表述模糊。(A)项是对录音中has long been criticized as vague的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (B)项中的\"长期被公众忽视\"在录音中未提及，故排除；(C)项是根据录音中worrying和anxiety设置的干扰项，虽然录音提到这给孕妇带来了担忧，但这属于建议带来的客观后果，而不是讲话者针对建议的评价，故排除；录音中未提及\"不同的理解\"，(D)项属于无中生有，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q18", "type": "listening", "no": 18, "q": "", "opts": ["The impact of the FDA's analysis", "Multiple factors including mercury", "Critics' conclusions", "The prices of seafood"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[18]提到，当人们讨论孕妇与海产品时，汞问题尤为值得关注。这并非导致焦虑的唯一因素。(B)项是对录音中mercury和not the only factor的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项是根据录音中FDA和analysis设置的干扰项，但录音提到FDA的分析是为了制定建议，并没有说人们担心这个分析本身，故排除；录音中并未提及公众对批评者的结论感到担忧，(C)项属于无中生有，故排除；(D)项中的\"价格\"在录音中未被提及，故排除(D)项。\n\nRecording Two", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q19", "type": "listening", "no": 19, "q": "", "opts": ["They will suffer a breakdown soon", "They will be losing both joy and life", "They will be,putting themselves and family at risk", "They will make things worse for people around them"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[19]提到，因此，你不仅正在扼杀自己的快乐，甚至可能正在杀掉你自己。(B)项是对录音中killing your joy和killing yourself的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项中的\"很快崩溃\"在录音中并未提及，故排除；(C)项中的\"使家人面临风险\"在录音中未提及，故排除；(D)项是根据录音中the worse you're making things for yourself设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是使你自己的处境变得更糟，而不是周围的人，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q20", "type": "listening", "no": 20, "q": "", "opts": ["Spend 14 days for vacation every year", "Aimfor three separate vacations yearly", "Space out their vacations evenly instead of taking one long vacation", "Use all their days off for a long summer break to achieve full-on calm"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[20]提到，上述研究还建议你均匀分布假期，而非一次性用完所有假期天数。其中，That above-mentioned study指的是前文提到的9-year-long study published in Psychosomatic Medicine，(C)项是对录音中space out your vacations evenly instead of using up all of your days at once的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项是根据录音中14 days of paid leave设置的干扰项，但录音中是说如果你有14天的带薪假，而这只是一个假设的例子，并非研究强制要求的标准天数，故排除；(B)项是根据录音中的three separate 8-day vacations设置的干扰项，但录音说的是，如果你刚好有14天带薪年假，那差不多可以分成三个8天的假期，这只是特定条件下的推算结果，不是普遍适用的建议，故排除；(D)项是根据录音中的full-on calm和use all your days to take one long summer break设置的干扰项，但录音说的是，尽管把所有假期攒起来然后一次性休完长假很诱人，但整体目标应该是达到长度", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q21", "type": "listening", "no": 21, "q": "", "opts": ["Expecting to have more vacations in a year will boost one's work efficiency", "Anticipating more vacations would reduce one's feelings of despair at work", "Taking more than three vacations yearly would prolong one's excitement", "Planning multiple breaks throughout the year will add to one's happiness"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[21]提到，休假频率至关重要，因为全年安排多次假期意味着你总会有值得期待的事情。制定计划以及随之而来的期待感往往能让我们更快乐。(D)项是对录音中planning multiple breaks throughout the year和make us happier的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项中的\"提高工作效率\"在录音中未被提及，故排除；(B)项在录音中未被提及，故排除；录音中仅提到全年多次休息能提升幸福感，并未强调为\"三次以上\"，故排除(C)项。\n\nRecording Three", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q22", "type": "listening", "no": 22, "q": "", "opts": ["Surveying when drivers are caught in theheat of arguments", "Designing devices to prevent drivers from being distracted", "Testing models for monitoring drivers easily getting tired", "Developing sensor-based systems for autonomous vehicles"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[22]提到，参与欧盟770亿欧元研究计划的研究人员正在开发基于传感器的系统，用于监测驾驶员是否分心、焦虑或疲劳。(D)项是对录音中developing sensor-based systems的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项是根据原文中caught in the heat of an argument设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是新系统能够应对的一种驾驶状态，并不是说研究人员去调查人们吵架的时间点，故排除；(B)项是根据原文中driver is distracted设置的干扰项，但录音中说这些传感器系统是用来监测驾驶员是否分心或疲劳的，而不是用来防止他们分心的，故排除；(C)项中的\"容易疲劳\"在录音中未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q23", "type": "listening", "no": 23, "q": "", "opts": ["Make sure the autonomous vehicle operates in the simplest manner", "Ensure the transition from the driver to the vehicle is safely made", "Alleviate the driver's pressure as much as possible", "Record the driver's state as accurately as possible"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[23]提到，她表示，目标是真正确保从驾驶员到车辆的过渡过程以安全方式进行，从而能够考虑驾驶员的状态。(B)项是对录音中make sure that the transition from the driver to the vehicle is done in a safe way的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项中的\"最简单的方式\"在录音中未提及，故排除；录音中提到系统会考虑驾驶员在疲劳、压力或焦虑下的状态，目的是防止把控制权交给这种状态下的他们，而不是(C)项中提到的为了\"减轻\"他们的压力，故排除；(D)项是根据录音中driver's state设置的干扰项，录音中说的是使用传感器来检测驾驶员的状态，但这只是手段，并不是该项目要实现的最终目标，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q24", "type": "listening", "no": 24, "q": "", "opts": ["Driver fatigue", "Dreadful traffic", "Drunk driving", "Driving time"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[24]提到，驾驶员疲劳被认为是导致欧盟范围内多达五分之一交通事故的原因。(A)项是对录音中driver fatigue的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (B)项中的\"糟糕的交通状况\"在录音中未提及，故排除；(C)项中的\"酒后驾驶\"在录音中未提及，故排除；录音中虽然提到早上6点发生事故的概率是上午10点的20倍，但这只是说明疲劳高发的时间段，并不是说(D)项中的\"驾驶时间\"导致了五分之一的事故，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q25", "type": "listening", "no": 25, "q": "", "opts": ["There has been a strong debate on the feasibility of Level 3 cars", "There have been few remarkable advances in auto technology", "There has been a rise in attention-grabbing accidents recently", "There have been few feasible ways to shift public sentiment"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[25]提到，随着近期引人关注的交通事故频发，如今驾驶员普遍认为自动驾驶汽车的安全性低于以往水平。(C)项是对录音中with an increase in attention-grabbing accidents recently的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项是根据录音中strong debate和Level 3 car设置的干扰项，录音中说的是专家提到对三级汽车的安全性有激烈辩论，但这只是背景信息，并非导致公众觉得\"自动驾驶汽车比过去不安全\"的直接原因，故排除；录音中明确提到技术取得了进步(advances)，而不是(B)项中的\"没有显著的进步\"，故排除；(D)项是根据录音中some way to go in shifting public sentiment设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是改变公众情绪方面还有很长的路要走，并非是说\"可行的方法很少\"，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q46", "type": "reading", "no": 46, "q": "What does the author believe regarding social media?", "opts": ["To stop children using it is unrealistic and inadvisable", "It helps both adults and children to organise their lives", "It poses problems to both parents and children", "A world without it is really hard to imagine"], "answer": 0, "why": "根据题干中的关键词语for和social media可定位至首段。\n\n答案为(A)。首段首句总述作者的观点：儿童使用社交媒体是一个问题，但并未作具体阐述，因此应继续往下读，第3句提到，但凭空设想一个青少年被阻止使用周围成年人用来安排生活的平台和消息系统的世界，既不现实也不可取。(A)项是对该句信息的同义转述，故为答案。其中stop...using对应原文中的are prevented from accessing；unrealistic and inadvisable对应neither practical nor desirable。\n\n[干扰项排除] 首段第1至3句提到，儿童使用社交媒体是一个问题，但成年人会利用社交媒体安排自己的生活，这说明社交媒体对儿童是有害的，但对成年人是有益的，(B)、(C)两项均与原文不符，故排除；原文提到阻止青少年使用社交媒体\"既不现实也不可取\"，并非说一个没有社交媒体的世界真的很难想象，(D)项属于主观臆断，故排除。", "passage": "Children's use of social media is a problem. That doesn't mean it should be stopped. While younger children should not be on platforms where 13 is the minimum age, it is neither practical nor desirable to imagine a world in which teenagers are prevented from accessing the platforms and messaging systems that the adults around them use to organise their lives.\n\nBut the pleasure and stimulation that people of all ages find on their screens and smartphones must not blind us to the harm that spending time in this way can cause. The finding that the alarmingly high rate of depression in British teenage girls is closely correlated to time spent on social media is extremely concerning. Research the same extent. More than a third of depressed girls have experienced online harassment. About half of affected girls suffered disrupted sleep, compared with 20% of depressed boys.\n\nAlthough the research does not prove that social media causes depression, the importance of the finding that there is a connection should not be underestimated. Schools have done important work in communicating key messages about the risks of online abuse. But when it comes to broader questions about “\"screen time\", many professionals as well as parents are confused and uncertain.\n\nThe health secretary, Matt Hancock, wants official guidelines on time and age limits. This idea has the virtue of simplicity,though some experts question its effects and call for a greater degree of differentiation, arguing that time online by itself is not a bad thing and it all depends on what you are doing. Also due this year is information commissioner Elizabeth Denham's Age Appropriate Design Code, covering the use of children's personal data. Both initiatives are welcome. But the government is playing catch-up. Statutory regulation of the digital media giants is long overdue. The failure to protect children from data harvesting and targeted advertising thus far is as shocking as the lack of concern shown by the companies themselves with regard to children's psychological health.\n\nOf course, social media companies are not the source of all our ills, and can offer a too-easy target for politicians keen to divert attention from inadequately resourced schools and health services. There are other reasons for the rising incidence of depression in young people, with increasing pressure to succeed in education and in life more broadly-to be famous,beautiful, rich-often cited as a source of unhappiness or anxiety which can,if aggravated by other factors,and not managed, tip into ill health. Safeguarding means more than the avoidance of physical threat."}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q47", "type": "reading", "no": 47, "q": "What do we learn from the passage about British teenage girls?", "opts": ["The more frequently they interact on social media, themore they experience harassment", "The more time they spend on social media, the more likely they suffer from depression", "Most of them are engaged on social media for at least three hours a day", "They spend half as many hours on social media as British teenage boys"], "answer": 1, "why": "根据题干中的关键词语British teenage girls可定位至第2段第2句。\n\n答案为(B)。第2段第2句提到，英国女孩的抑郁症发病率高得惊人，且这与她们使用社交媒体的时间密切相关，这一发现极其令人担忧。(B)项是对该句信息的同义转述，故为答案。其中The more time they spend on social media, the more likely they suffer from depression对应原文中的alarmingly high rate of depression...is closely correlated to time spent on social media。\n\n[干扰项排除] 第2段第4句提到超过三分之一的抑郁女孩都经历过网络骚扰，但未提及互动频率与骚扰之间是否成正比，(A)项属于无中生有，故排除；第2段第3句提到每天使用社交媒体至少三小时的英国女孩的比例是\"two in five girls(五分之二，即40%)\"，未达到\"Most(大多数，通常超过50%)\"，(C)项与原文不符，故排除；第2段第3句明确表述\"达到同样时长的男孩只有女孩的一半\"，即女孩在社交媒体上花费的时间更多，而非更少，(D)项与原文相反，故排除。", "passage": "Children's use of social media is a problem. That doesn't mean it should be stopped. While younger children should not be on platforms where 13 is the minimum age, it is neither practical nor desirable to imagine a world in which teenagers are prevented from accessing the platforms and messaging systems that the adults around them use to organise their lives.\n\nBut the pleasure and stimulation that people of all ages find on their screens and smartphones must not blind us to the harm that spending time in this way can cause. The finding that the alarmingly high rate of depression in British teenage girls is closely correlated to time spent on social media is extremely concerning. Research the same extent. More than a third of depressed girls have experienced online harassment. About half of affected girls suffered disrupted sleep, compared with 20% of depressed boys.\n\nAlthough the research does not prove that social media causes depression, the importance of the finding that there is a connection should not be underestimated. Schools have done important work in communicating key messages about the risks of online abuse. But when it comes to broader questions about “\"screen time\", many professionals as well as parents are confused and uncertain.\n\nThe health secretary, Matt Hancock, wants official guidelines on time and age limits. This idea has the virtue of simplicity,though some experts question its effects and call for a greater degree of differentiation, arguing that time online by itself is not a bad thing and it all depends on what you are doing. Also due this year is information commissioner Elizabeth Denham's Age Appropriate Design Code, covering the use of children's personal data. Both initiatives are welcome. But the government is playing catch-up. Statutory regulation of the digital media giants is long overdue. The failure to protect children from data harvesting and targeted advertising thus far is as shocking as the lack of concern shown by the companies themselves with regard to children's psychological health.\n\nOf course, social media companies are not the source of all our ills, and can offer a too-easy target for politicians keen to divert attention from inadequately resourced schools and health services. There are other reasons for the rising incidence of depression in young people, with increasing pressure to succeed in education and in life more broadly-to be famous,beautiful, rich-often cited as a source of unhappiness or anxiety which can,if aggravated by other factors,and not managed, tip into ill health. Safeguarding means more than the avoidance of physical threat."}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q48", "type": "reading", "no": 48, "q": "What is the author's view on the research finding about British teenage girls?", "opts": ["Its implications about online abuse are obvious", "Itskey message calls for immediate attention", "Its importance should not be undervalued", "Its uncertainty should not go unnoticed"], "answer": 2, "why": "根据题干中的关键词语the research findings和British girls可定位至第3段。\n\n答案为(C)。第3段第1句提到，尽管该研究并未证实社交媒体会导致抑郁症，但不应低估发现二者存在关联这一结果的重要性。(C)项是该句信息的同义转述，故为答案。其中importance为原词复现；undervalued对应原文中的underestimated。\n\n[干扰项排除] 第3段第2句仅提到学校在传达网络欺凌风险的关键信息方面已开展重要工作，但并未提及其启示是\"明显的\"，(A)项属于无中生有，故排除；作者强调的是重要性，而非关注其关键信息或不确定性，(B)、(D)两项与原文不符，故排除。", "passage": "Children's use of social media is a problem. That doesn't mean it should be stopped. While younger children should not be on platforms where 13 is the minimum age, it is neither practical nor desirable to imagine a world in which teenagers are prevented from accessing the platforms and messaging systems that the adults around them use to organise their lives.\n\nBut the pleasure and stimulation that people of all ages find on their screens and smartphones must not blind us to the harm that spending time in this way can cause. The finding that the alarmingly high rate of depression in British teenage girls is closely correlated to time spent on social media is extremely concerning. Research the same extent. More than a third of depressed girls have experienced online harassment. About half of affected girls suffered disrupted sleep, compared with 20% of depressed boys.\n\nAlthough the research does not prove that social media causes depression, the importance of the finding that there is a connection should not be underestimated. Schools have done important work in communicating key messages about the risks of online abuse. But when it comes to broader questions about “\"screen time\", many professionals as well as parents are confused and uncertain.\n\nThe health secretary, Matt Hancock, wants official guidelines on time and age limits. This idea has the virtue of simplicity,though some experts question its effects and call for a greater degree of differentiation, arguing that time online by itself is not a bad thing and it all depends on what you are doing. Also due this year is information commissioner Elizabeth Denham's Age Appropriate Design Code, covering the use of children's personal data. Both initiatives are welcome. But the government is playing catch-up. Statutory regulation of the digital media giants is long overdue. The failure to protect children from data harvesting and targeted advertising thus far is as shocking as the lack of concern shown by the companies themselves with regard to children's psychological health.\n\nOf course, social media companies are not the source of all our ills, and can offer a too-easy target for politicians keen to divert attention from inadequately resourced schools and health services. There are other reasons for the rising incidence of depression in young people, with increasing pressure to succeed in education and in life more broadly-to be famous,beautiful, rich-often cited as a source of unhappiness or anxiety which can,if aggravated by other factors,and not managed, tip into ill health. Safeguarding means more than the avoidance of physical threat."}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q49", "type": "reading", "no": 49, "q": "What do experts thinkof health secretary Matt Hancock's idea?", "opts": ["Its implementation requires prompt action", "It is a welcome initiative but is long overdue", "Its success depends on how it is implemented", "It is too simplistic to produce the desired effect"], "answer": 3, "why": "根据题干中的关键词语experts和Matt Hancock可定位至第4段第1、2句。\n\n答案为(D)。第4段第1、2句提到，卫生大臣马特·汉考克希望出台关于时间与年龄限制的官方指导方针。这一想法具有简洁性的优点，尽管一些专家质疑其效果，并呼吁进行更大程度的差异化。由此可知，这些专家质疑了汉考克的想法并呼吁其他做法，即表明专家们认为该想法\"过于简单化\"，不足以解决问题。(D)项是对上述信息的同义转述，故为答案。其中simplistic为原文中simplicity的形容词形式；too...to produce the desired effect对应question its effects。\n\n[干扰项排除] 根据第4段第2句可知，专家质疑的是指导方针本身的效果，而非实施速度和实施方式，(A)、(C)两项均与原文不符，故排除；(B)项根据第4段第4句中的\"Both initiatives are welcome(这两项举措都值得欢迎)\"和第6句中的\"long overdue(早已刻不容缓)\"设置的干扰，但这是作者对法定监管的看法，并非专家对汉考克想法的评价，故排除。", "passage": "Children's use of social media is a problem. That doesn't mean it should be stopped. While younger children should not be on platforms where 13 is the minimum age, it is neither practical nor desirable to imagine a world in which teenagers are prevented from accessing the platforms and messaging systems that the adults around them use to organise their lives.\n\nBut the pleasure and stimulation that people of all ages find on their screens and smartphones must not blind us to the harm that spending time in this way can cause. The finding that the alarmingly high rate of depression in British teenage girls is closely correlated to time spent on social media is extremely concerning. Research the same extent. More than a third of depressed girls have experienced online harassment. About half of affected girls suffered disrupted sleep, compared with 20% of depressed boys.\n\nAlthough the research does not prove that social media causes depression, the importance of the finding that there is a connection should not be underestimated. Schools have done important work in communicating key messages about the risks of online abuse. But when it comes to broader questions about “\"screen time\", many professionals as well as parents are confused and uncertain.\n\nThe health secretary, Matt Hancock, wants official guidelines on time and age limits. This idea has the virtue of simplicity,though some experts question its effects and call for a greater degree of differentiation, arguing that time online by itself is not a bad thing and it all depends on what you are doing. Also due this year is information commissioner Elizabeth Denham's Age Appropriate Design Code, covering the use of children's personal data. Both initiatives are welcome. But the government is playing catch-up. Statutory regulation of the digital media giants is long overdue. The failure to protect children from data harvesting and targeted advertising thus far is as shocking as the lack of concern shown by the companies themselves with regard to children's psychological health.\n\nOf course, social media companies are not the source of all our ills, and can offer a too-easy target for politicians keen to divert attention from inadequately resourced schools and health services. There are other reasons for the rising incidence of depression in young people, with increasing pressure to succeed in education and in life more broadly-to be famous,beautiful, rich-often cited as a source of unhappiness or anxiety which can,if aggravated by other factors,and not managed, tip into ill health. Safeguarding means more than the avoidance of physical threat."}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q51", "type": "reading", "no": 51, "q": "What do welearn about the G-20's agreement on curbing the use of tax havens?", "opts": ["It has aggravated the problem in non-G-20 countries", "It has taken a toll on countries such as Cayman Islands", "It has failed to achieve the expected outcome", "It has failed to result in any long-term plan"], "answer": 2, "why": "根据题干中的关键词语the G-20's agreement和curbing the use of tax havens可定位至第1-2段。\n\n答案为(C)。第1段第1句提到，大约十年前，由全球最大经济体组成的二十国集团(G20)一致同意，严厉打击跨国企业滥用避税天堂进行避税的行为。第2段则进一步指出然而避税天堂并没有受到遏制……问题反而愈演愈烈。(C)项是对上述信息的概括总结，其中failed to achieve the expected outcome对应原文中的instead of reining in the use of tax havens和the problem has only gotten worse，故为答案。\n\n[干扰项排除] 原文未提到\"它加剧了非二十国集团国家面临的问题\"，故排除(A)项；(B)项是依据文中countries such as the Bahamas and Cayman Islands设置的干扰，但文中说的是巴哈马和开曼群岛是实际税率极低甚至几乎为零的避税天堂，而不是该协议对开曼群岛等国家造成了冲击，故排除；(D)项是依据文中This resulted in a plan that aimed to curb practices设置的干扰，但文中说的是该协议促成了一项旨在遏制企业避税行为的计划，而不是未能形成任何长期性计划，故排除。", "passage": "About a decade ago, the G-20, a forum of the world's biggest economies, agreed to crack down on multinational corporations'abusiveuse of tax havens. This resulted in a plan that aimed to curb practices that shielded a large chunk of corporate profits from tax authorities.\n\nBut, instead of reining in the use of tax havens-countries such as the Bahamas and Cayman Islands with very low or no effective tax rates—the problem has only gotten worse.\n\nIn a new study, we measured the excessive profits reported in tax havens that cannot be explained by ordinary economic activity such as employees, factories and research in that country. Our findings show a striking pattern of artificial shifting of paper profits to tax havens by corporations, which has been relentless since the 1980s.\n\nWe found that the world's biggest multinational businesses shifted 37% of the profits—$969 billion—they earned in other countries to tax havens in 2019. The figure was less than 2% in the 1970s. The main reasons for the large increase were the growth of the tax avoidance industry in the 1980s and U.S. policies that made it easier to shift profits from high-tax countries to tax havens.\n\nWe estimate that the amount of corporate taxes lost as a result reached 10% of total corporate revenue in 2019, up from less than 0.1% in the 1970s. In 2019, the total government tax loss globally was $250 billion. U.S. multinational corporations alone accounted for about half of that, followed by the U.K. and Germany. How do policymakers fix this? So far, the world as a whole has been trying to solve this problem by cutting or scrapping corporate taxes in a very gradual way. In the past 40 years, the global effective corporate tax rate has fallen from 23% to 17%. At the same time, governments have relied more heavily on consumption taxes. But the root cause of profit-shifting is the incentives involved, such as generous or lenient corporate tax rates in other countries. If countries could agree on a global minimum corporate tax rateof, say, 20%, the problem of This type of mechanism is exactly what more than 130 countries signed onto in 2021, with implementation of a 15% minimum tax set to begin soon in the EU, U.K., Japan, Indonesia and many other countries. The U.S. has notably not been able to pass this legislation.\n\nOur research suggests implementing this type of tax reform is necessary in reversing the shift of ever-greater amounts of corporate profits to tax havens-instead of being taxed by the governments where they operate and create value."}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q52", "type": "reading", "no": 52, "q": "What mainly accounted for the large increase in the world's biggest multinational corporations' shift of profits to tax havens?", "opts": ["Fluctuating tax rates globally", "Lenient policies of the U.S", "Heavy taxes imposed on them", "Rapid growth of their business"], "answer": 1, "why": "根据题干中的关键词语mainly accounted for the large increase和shift of profits可定位至第4段最后一句。\n\n答案为(B)。第4段最后一句提到，大幅增长的主要原因是20世纪80年代避税产业的发展，以及美国出台的使企业更容易将利润从高税收国家转移到避税天堂的政策。(B)项是对该句信息的概括总结，选项中的lenient(宽松的)对应原文中的made it easier；policies of the U.S.对应U.S. policies。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项原文未提及，故排除；(C)项是依据文中high-tax countries设置的干扰，但文中说的是美国出台的政策使企业将利润从高税率国家转移至避税天堂更容易而不是因为企业承受高额税款才导致利润转移大幅增加，故排除；(D)项原文未提及，故排除。", "passage": "About a decade ago, the G-20, a forum of the world's biggest economies, agreed to crack down on multinational corporations'abusiveuse of tax havens. This resulted in a plan that aimed to curb practices that shielded a large chunk of corporate profits from tax authorities.\n\nBut, instead of reining in the use of tax havens-countries such as the Bahamas and Cayman Islands with very low or no effective tax rates—the problem has only gotten worse.\n\nIn a new study, we measured the excessive profits reported in tax havens that cannot be explained by ordinary economic activity such as employees, factories and research in that country. Our findings show a striking pattern of artificial shifting of paper profits to tax havens by corporations, which has been relentless since the 1980s.\n\nWe found that the world's biggest multinational businesses shifted 37% of the profits—$969 billion—they earned in other countries to tax havens in 2019. The figure was less than 2% in the 1970s. The main reasons for the large increase were the growth of the tax avoidance industry in the 1980s and U.S. policies that made it easier to shift profits from high-tax countries to tax havens.\n\nWe estimate that the amount of corporate taxes lost as a result reached 10% of total corporate revenue in 2019, up from less than 0.1% in the 1970s. In 2019, the total government tax loss globally was $250 billion. U.S. multinational corporations alone accounted for about half of that, followed by the U.K. and Germany. How do policymakers fix this? So far, the world as a whole has been trying to solve this problem by cutting or scrapping corporate taxes in a very gradual way. In the past 40 years, the global effective corporate tax rate has fallen from 23% to 17%. At the same time, governments have relied more heavily on consumption taxes. But the root cause of profit-shifting is the incentives involved, such as generous or lenient corporate tax rates in other countries. If countries could agree on a global minimum corporate tax rateof, say, 20%, the problem of This type of mechanism is exactly what more than 130 countries signed onto in 2021, with implementation of a 15% minimum tax set to begin soon in the EU, U.K., Japan, Indonesia and many other countries. The U.S. has notably not been able to pass this legislation.\n\nOur research suggests implementing this type of tax reform is necessary in reversing the shift of ever-greater amounts of corporate profits to tax havens-instead of being taxed by the governments where they operate and create value."}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q53", "type": "reading", "no": 53, "q": "What have policymakers been doing to prevent government tax loss?", "opts": ["Reducing corporate taxes step by step", "Scrapping consumption taxes gradually", "Making joint efforts to levy corporate taxes", "Adjusting global effective corporate tax rates"], "answer": 0, "why": "根据题干中的关键词语policymakers和prevent government tax loss可定位至第6段第1、2句。\n\n答案为(A)。第6段第1、2句提到，那么，政策制定者该如何解决这一问题呢？迄今为止，全球总体上一直在尝试通过逐步降低甚至取消企业税款来解决此问题。(A)项是对该处信息的同义转述。选项中的reducing corporate taxes对应原文中的cutting or scrapping corporate taxes；选项中的step by step对应in a very gradual way。\n\n[干扰项排除] (B)项是依据文中cutting or scrapping corporate taxes设置的干扰，但文中说的是逐步降低甚至取消企业税，而不是逐步取消消费税，故排除；(C)项原文未提及，故排除；(D)项是依据文中the global effective corporate tax rate has fallen from 23% to 17%设置的干扰，但文中说的是全球企业实际税率下降这一结果，而不是通过调整全球企业实际税率来解决税收流失问题，属于答非所问，故排除。", "passage": "About a decade ago, the G-20, a forum of the world's biggest economies, agreed to crack down on multinational corporations'abusiveuse of tax havens. This resulted in a plan that aimed to curb practices that shielded a large chunk of corporate profits from tax authorities.\n\nBut, instead of reining in the use of tax havens-countries such as the Bahamas and Cayman Islands with very low or no effective tax rates—the problem has only gotten worse.\n\nIn a new study, we measured the excessive profits reported in tax havens that cannot be explained by ordinary economic activity such as employees, factories and research in that country. Our findings show a striking pattern of artificial shifting of paper profits to tax havens by corporations, which has been relentless since the 1980s.\n\nWe found that the world's biggest multinational businesses shifted 37% of the profits—$969 billion—they earned in other countries to tax havens in 2019. The figure was less than 2% in the 1970s. The main reasons for the large increase were the growth of the tax avoidance industry in the 1980s and U.S. policies that made it easier to shift profits from high-tax countries to tax havens.\n\nWe estimate that the amount of corporate taxes lost as a result reached 10% of total corporate revenue in 2019, up from less than 0.1% in the 1970s. In 2019, the total government tax loss globally was $250 billion. U.S. multinational corporations alone accounted for about half of that, followed by the U.K. and Germany. How do policymakers fix this? So far, the world as a whole has been trying to solve this problem by cutting or scrapping corporate taxes in a very gradual way. In the past 40 years, the global effective corporate tax rate has fallen from 23% to 17%. At the same time, governments have relied more heavily on consumption taxes. But the root cause of profit-shifting is the incentives involved, such as generous or lenient corporate tax rates in other countries. If countries could agree on a global minimum corporate tax rateof, say, 20%, the problem of This type of mechanism is exactly what more than 130 countries signed onto in 2021, with implementation of a 15% minimum tax set to begin soon in the EU, U.K., Japan, Indonesia and many other countries. The U.S. has notably not been able to pass this legislation.\n\nOur research suggests implementing this type of tax reform is necessary in reversing the shift of ever-greater amounts of corporate profits to tax havens-instead of being taxed by the governments where they operate and create value."}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q54", "type": "reading", "no": 54, "q": "What does the author say would bring tax havens to an end?", "opts": ["The alignment of corporate taxes with consumption taxes", "The implementation of generous tax rates in all countries", "The agreement on the root cause of global profit-shifting", "The imposition of a global minimum corporate tax rate"], "answer": 3, "why": "根据题干中的关键词语bring tax havens to an end可定位至第7段最后一句。\n\n答案为(D)。第7段最后一句提到，如果各国能够就全球最低企业税率达成一致，例如设定为20%，那么利润转移问题将在很大程度上消失，因为避税天堂将根本不复存在。(D)项是对该句信息的同义转述，其中的a global minimum corporate tax rate为原词复现；imposition(实施)在此语境中对应原文中的agree on(达成一致)。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项原文未提及，故排除；(B)项是依据文中generous or lenient corporate tax rates设置的干扰，但文中说的是这些宽松税率正是利润转移的诱因，而不是解决办法，故排除；(C)项是依据agree on a global...和the root cause of profit-shifting设置的干扰，但文中说的是想要让避税天堂消失，需要就全球最低企业税率达成一致，而不是就问题的根本原因达成一致，故排除。", "passage": "About a decade ago, the G-20, a forum of the world's biggest economies, agreed to crack down on multinational corporations'abusiveuse of tax havens. This resulted in a plan that aimed to curb practices that shielded a large chunk of corporate profits from tax authorities.\n\nBut, instead of reining in the use of tax havens-countries such as the Bahamas and Cayman Islands with very low or no effective tax rates—the problem has only gotten worse.\n\nIn a new study, we measured the excessive profits reported in tax havens that cannot be explained by ordinary economic activity such as employees, factories and research in that country. Our findings show a striking pattern of artificial shifting of paper profits to tax havens by corporations, which has been relentless since the 1980s.\n\nWe found that the world's biggest multinational businesses shifted 37% of the profits—$969 billion—they earned in other countries to tax havens in 2019. The figure was less than 2% in the 1970s. The main reasons for the large increase were the growth of the tax avoidance industry in the 1980s and U.S. policies that made it easier to shift profits from high-tax countries to tax havens.\n\nWe estimate that the amount of corporate taxes lost as a result reached 10% of total corporate revenue in 2019, up from less than 0.1% in the 1970s. In 2019, the total government tax loss globally was $250 billion. U.S. multinational corporations alone accounted for about half of that, followed by the U.K. and Germany. How do policymakers fix this? So far, the world as a whole has been trying to solve this problem by cutting or scrapping corporate taxes in a very gradual way. In the past 40 years, the global effective corporate tax rate has fallen from 23% to 17%. At the same time, governments have relied more heavily on consumption taxes. But the root cause of profit-shifting is the incentives involved, such as generous or lenient corporate tax rates in other countries. If countries could agree on a global minimum corporate tax rateof, say, 20%, the problem of This type of mechanism is exactly what more than 130 countries signed onto in 2021, with implementation of a 15% minimum tax set to begin soon in the EU, U.K., Japan, Indonesia and many other countries. The U.S. has notably not been able to pass this legislation.\n\nOur research suggests implementing this type of tax reform is necessary in reversing the shift of ever-greater amounts of corporate profits to tax havens-instead of being taxed by the governments where they operate and create value."}, {"id": "CET6-1-Q55", "type": "reading", "no": 55, "q": "What could be expected of the global problem of profit-shifting in the near future?", "opts": ["It would be solved with more countries cutting tax rates", "It would end with governments awareness of its severity", "It would cease to exist with worldwide corporate support", "It would continue because of noncooperation from the U.S"], "answer": 3, "why": "根据题干中的关键词语profit-shifting和in the near future可定位至第8段。\n\n答案为(D)。第8段提到，这一机制正是130多个国家在2021年签署同意的——值得注意的是，美国至今仍未能通过这项立法。而前文第5段指出，美国跨国企业造成的税收流失约占全球总额的一半。此外，第6段指出，如果各国能够就全球最低企业税率达成一致，那么利润转移问题将在很大程度上消失。因此，在美国尚未参与这一税制改革的情况下，全球利润转移问题短期内难以得到彻底解决。(D)项是对文中信息的合理推断。\n\n[干扰项排除] (A)项是依据文中the world as a whole has been trying to solve this problem by cutting or scrapping corporate taxes设置的干扰，但文中说的是过去各国一直在尝试通过逐步降低甚至取消企业税款来解决此问题，而不是继续降低税率就能够解决问题，故排除；(B)项原文未提及，故排除；文中讨论的是各国政府推动全球最低企业税制度，而非企业的支持态度，故排除(C)项。", "passage": "About a decade ago, the G-20, a forum of the world's biggest economies, agreed to crack down on multinational corporations'abusiveuse of tax havens. This resulted in a plan that aimed to curb practices that shielded a large chunk of corporate profits from tax authorities.\n\nBut, instead of reining in the use of tax havens-countries such as the Bahamas and Cayman Islands with very low or no effective tax rates—the problem has only gotten worse.\n\nIn a new study, we measured the excessive profits reported in tax havens that cannot be explained by ordinary economic activity such as employees, factories and research in that country. Our findings show a striking pattern of artificial shifting of paper profits to tax havens by corporations, which has been relentless since the 1980s.\n\nWe found that the world's biggest multinational businesses shifted 37% of the profits—$969 billion—they earned in other countries to tax havens in 2019. The figure was less than 2% in the 1970s. The main reasons for the large increase were the growth of the tax avoidance industry in the 1980s and U.S. policies that made it easier to shift profits from high-tax countries to tax havens.\n\nWe estimate that the amount of corporate taxes lost as a result reached 10% of total corporate revenue in 2019, up from less than 0.1% in the 1970s. In 2019, the total government tax loss globally was $250 billion. U.S. multinational corporations alone accounted for about half of that, followed by the U.K. and Germany. How do policymakers fix this? So far, the world as a whole has been trying to solve this problem by cutting or scrapping corporate taxes in a very gradual way. In the past 40 years, the global effective corporate tax rate has fallen from 23% to 17%. At the same time, governments have relied more heavily on consumption taxes. But the root cause of profit-shifting is the incentives involved, such as generous or lenient corporate tax rates in other countries. If countries could agree on a global minimum corporate tax rateof, say, 20%, the problem of This type of mechanism is exactly what more than 130 countries signed onto in 2021, with implementation of a 15% minimum tax set to begin soon in the EU, U.K., Japan, Indonesia and many other countries. The U.S. has notably not been able to pass this legislation.\n\nOur research suggests implementing this type of tax reform is necessary in reversing the shift of ever-greater amounts of corporate profits to tax havens-instead of being taxed by the governments where they operate and create value."}]}, {"id": "CET6-2", "level": "CET6", "set": 2, "year": 2026, "month": 6, "title": "六级 2026年6月 第2套", "audio": "audio/mock/cet6_2026_2.mp3", "minutes": 40, "questions": [{"id": "CET6-2-Q1", "type": "listening", "no": 1, "q": "", "opts": ["Celebrate his 10th wedding anniversary", "Celebrate his promotion in Barcelona", "Give his wife a pleasant surprise", "Ask for leave to visit Barcelona"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[1-1]男士提到，下周是他结婚十周年纪念日，他向妻子简承诺会安排一次城市短途旅行，因为他每年都可以休年假。句[1-2]男士提到，所以他正在尝试预订巴塞罗那的行程。由此可知，男士打算去巴塞罗那庆祝他的十周年结婚纪念日。(A)项是对录音信息的概括转述，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】录音中并未提及男士获得晋升，(B)项无中生有，故排除；男士只提到计划带妻子出行庆祝结婚纪念日，并没有提到要给妻子惊喜，(C)项过度推断，故排除；(D)项是依据录音中 I'm trying to book something in Barcelona 设置的干扰，但录音中说的是男士可以\"利用年假去巴塞罗那\"，而不是\"请假前往巴塞罗那\"，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q2", "type": "listening", "no": 2, "q": "", "opts": ["He is unable to find some inexpensive accommodation", "There are few warm places to go at this time of the year", "There is so much for him to do before going on vacation", "He is afraid Barcelona may not be so fabulous as expected"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[2]男士提到，他还在忙着找住处，所有地方要么价格贵得离谱，要么已经全部订满，他开始感到有些慌乱了。由此可知，男士慌乱的原因是他无法找到便宜的住宿，(A)项是对录音信息的概括转述，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】(B)项是依据 it would be nice to celebrate somewhere warm 设置的干扰，但录音中只表明了男士选择温暖的巴塞罗那作为出行地点，不存在温暖的出行地很少的问题，故排除；(C)项和(D)项在录音中均未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q3", "type": "listening", "no": 3, "q": "", "opts": ["Rent a car to drive into the city and scenic spots so as to save the expenses on transportation", "Spend a couple of nights in a glamorous hotel and the rest of the week in inexpensive places", "Find all-inclusive accommodation to eliminate the need to pay for food and drinks", "Stay in a hotel away from the coast where the accommodation is less expensive"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[3]女士提到，如果打算待一整周，为什么不选在某个非常豪华的地方住几晚，然后把剩下的时间安排在距离海岸稍远一些、住宿更便宜的地方。(B)项是对录音信息的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】(A)项中的\"租车\"以及(C)项中的\"全包式住宿\"均是男士听完女士建议后，自己联想到的内容，并非女士给出的建议，故排除；(D)项是依据录音中 a bit further up the coast where the accommodation is cheaper 设置的干扰，但录音中女士建议的是在某个非常豪华的地方住几晚，然后把剩下的时间安排在距离海岸稍远一些、住宿更便宜的地方，而不只是\"选择远离海岸的酒店\"，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q4", "type": "listening", "no": 4, "q": "", "opts": ["She is sure to like the cathedral", "She is a romantic at heart", "She is no good at logistics", "She is scared of heights"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[4]男士提到，妻子简对高处感到极度恐惧。(D)项是对录音中 Jane is terrified of heights 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】录音中男士未提及\"她(妻子简)一定会喜欢大教堂\"，(A)项无中生有，故排除；(B)项是依据女士提到 That sounds so romantic 设置的干扰，但女士此处是感叹男士的计划听起来很浪漫，而不是\"她(妻子简)内心是个浪漫主义者\"，故排除；(C)项是依据男士提到的\"turning into a logistical nightmare\"设置的干扰，但录音中说的是，出行将\"变成一场事务安排的噩梦\"，而不是\"她(妻子简)不擅长事务安排\"，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q5", "type": "listening", "no": 5, "q": "", "opts": ["They often share each other's experiences", "They enjoy interacting with patients", "They always do things tricky", "They are both pharmacists"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[5-1]男士提到，他一直在处理常规工作，包括配药、与患者讨论其用药方案等。句[5-2]女士提到，她也一样。由此可知，两人都是药剂师，故(D)项为正确答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】录音中未提及二人经常互相分享彼此的经历，(A)项无中生有，故排除；(B)项是依据录音中 talking to patients 设置的干扰，但录音中男士说的是他一直在处理常规工作，其中包括与患者讨论其用药方案，而不是\"他们享受和患者交流\"，故排除；(C)项是依据录音中男士说 that's always tricky 设置的干扰，但录音中男士是感叹女士遇到的病例棘手，而不是\"他们总是处理棘手的问题\"，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q6", "type": "listening", "no": 6, "q": "", "opts": ["The doctor ended up admitting his mistake", "The patient consulted another doctor", "The patient actually took her advice", "The doctor double-checked with her"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[6]女士提到，她(患者)实际上遵循了她的建议。(C)项是对录音中 followed my advice 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】录音中未提及医生最终承认了自己的错误，医生只是\"调整了患者的用药方案\"，故排除(A)项；录音中未提及患者咨询了另一位医生，故排除(B)项；(D)项是依据录音中 double-check 设置的干扰，但录音中说的是女士建议患者向医生再次核对确认一下，而不是医生与她再次核对确认，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q7", "type": "listening", "no": 7, "q": "", "opts": ["A proposal for applying flu shots more effectively", "A new medication therapy management program", "A novel strategy to persuade stubborn patients", "A therapy for patients with similar conditions"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[7]男士提到，他一直在为患有慢性疾病的患者开发一项新的药物治疗管理方案。(B)项是对录音中 a new medication therapy management program 的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】(A)项是依据录音中 flu shots 设置的干扰，但录音中女士说的是\"接诊了许多前来接种流感疫苗的患者\"，而不是提出\"一项更高效接种流感疫苗的提案\"，故排除；(C)项是依据录音中 patient is too stubborn 设置的干扰，但录音中男士说的是\"患者过于固执\"，而不是提出\"一种劝说固执患者的新策略\"，故排除；录音中男士提到他\"一直在为患有慢性疾病的患者开发一项新的药物治疗管理方案\"，并不只是为\"患有相似病症\"的患者，(D)项与录音不符，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q8", "type": "listening", "no": 8, "q": "", "opts": ["Inability to send text messages", "Distrust in pharmaceutical experts", "Medication non-adherence", "Need for constant reminding"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[8]女士提到，不遵从医嘱用药是一个大问题，尤其对老年患者而言。(C)项是对录音中 Medication non-adherence 的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】(A)项是依据录音中 text message 设置的干扰，但录音中女士是在询问男士\"是否使用短信提醒\"，而不是说老年患者\"无法发送短信\"，故排除；录音中未提及老年患者\"对药学专家不信任\"，(B)项无中生有，故排除；(D)项是依据录音中 reminders 设置的干扰，但录音中女士是在询问男士\"是否使用短信提醒\"，而不是说老年患者的大问题在于\"需要不断提醒\"，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q9", "type": "listening", "no": 9, "q": "", "opts": ["Accomplish their goals", "View things positively", "Overstate their importance", "Dominate job interviews"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[9]提到，总体而言，有激情的人通常被视为积极的存在，并被认为更有可能实现其目标。(A)项是对录音中 achieve their goals 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】(B)项是依据录音中 in a positive light 设置的干扰，但录音中说的是有激情的人通常被视为积极的存在，而不是他们本身积极地看待事物，故排除；(C)项是依据录音中 the importance of passion been overstated 设置的干扰，但录音中说的是激情的重要性是否被夸大，而不是夸大有激情的人的重要性，故排除；录音中未提到有激情的人在求职面试中占据主导地位，(D)项过度推断，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q10", "type": "listening", "no": 10, "q": "", "opts": ["It reflects a strong sense of obligation to others", "It signifies a universal model of motivation", "It may differ in various cultures", "It may have been underestimated"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[10]提到，根据斯坦福大学的一项新研究，在某些文化中，激情可能没那么重要。由此可知，激情在一些文化中重要性较高，而在另一些文化中重要性较低，即激情在不同文化中的重要性可能有所不同，故选(C)项。\n\n【干扰项排除】录音中提到的是集体主义文化中的人往往将履行对他人责任置于个人利益之上，即这是集体主义文化人群的特点，而不是激情的重要性，故排除(A)项；录音中提到在西方的动机模式中，激情常被视为成就的关键，而不是激情代表一种普遍的动机模式，(B)项扩大范围，故排除；录音中提到人们夸大了激情的重要性，而不是低估，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q11", "type": "listening", "no": 11, "q": "", "opts": ["It was under-investigated", "It was more predictable", "It was taken for granted", "It was more subtle"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[11]提到，对于哥伦比亚或泰国等更具集体主义特征的社会而言，激情对(学业)表现的影响更为微妙。(D)项是对录音中 more subtle 的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】录音中未提到激情对学业成就影响的调查不够充分，(A)项无中生有，故排除；录音中提到，在更具集体主义特征的社会中，激情对(学业)表现的影响则较为微妙，由此可知，影响会更难预测，而不是更容易预测，故排除(B)项；录音未提到激情对学业成就的影响被视为理所当然，(C)项无中生有，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q13", "type": "listening", "no": 13, "q": "", "opts": ["Materials undergoing certain processes", "Materials exposed to various natural forces", "Materials impacted by any change of molecules", "Materials going through extremely radical transformations"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[13]提到，材料经历干燥、冷冻、起皱、扩散及反应等过程时，此类图案便开始小范围形成。随后这些变化会演化出人们肉眼可见的、更大范围的复杂图案。(A)项是对录音中 materials undergo processes like... 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】录音未提到接触各种自然力量的材料能形成更大范围的复杂图案，(B)项无中生有，故排除；录音开头提到各种自然界中的图案，本质上都源于原子和分子层面的微观变化，而不仅仅只是分子变化，故排除(C)项；录音未提到极端剧烈变化，(D)项无中生有，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q14", "type": "listening", "no": 14, "q": "", "opts": ["The beauty of molecular clustering", "The way for appreciating patterns", "The creation of patterns", "The factors affecting clusters"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[14]提出，想象一下冬季窗户上那些精致的冰晶图案。紧接着提问，是什么造就了这种图案？而后以窗户上的冰晶为例，具体讲解了自然界中的图案是如何一步步形成的。(C)项是对录音信息的概括转述，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】冰晶的例子不是展现分子聚类的美感，而是讲解图案形成的原理，(A)项偏离主旨，故排除；录音并未提到欣赏自然图案的方式，(B)项无中生有，故排除；录音只是提到湿度、温度等多种外部因素都会影响聚类的整体形态，但并非冰晶例子的说明对象，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q15", "type": "listening", "no": 15, "q": "", "opts": ["A pattern by accident", "Patterns for specific purposes", "Patterns with random imperfections", "A pattern with artificial intervention"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[15]提到，分子过程十分简单，图案也有可能只是偶然形成。(A)项是对录音中 a pattern by coincidence 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】录音中提到，科学家并不总能明确某种图案的形成目的，甚至无法确定其是否存在，(B)项与录音内容不符，故排除；录音中提到玻璃表面的随机缺陷是影响图案形成的外部因素，并非分子过程可能会产生存在随机缺陷的图案，(C)项与录音信息不符，故排除；录音未提及人工干预形成图案，(D)项无中生有，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q16", "type": "listening", "no": 16, "q": "", "opts": ["Eliminating all the.old habits at once", "Making a permanent lifestyle change", "Realizing all the life goals one has set", "Improving multiple areas of one's life"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[16]提到，做出永久性的生活方式改变真的非常困难。(B)项是录音中 Making a permanent lifestyle change 的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】(A)项是根据录音中 old habits 设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是，人性自然倾向于回到过去某个时候的旧习惯，而不是困难在于\"一次性消除所有旧习惯\"，故排除；录音中未提及\"实现所有生活目标\"，(C)项无中生有，故排除；(D)项是根据录音中 multiple areas of life 和 improve 设置的干扰项，录音中提到很多人希望改善生活的多个领域，但这并非真正的困难所在，演讲者认为真正的困难是\"生活方式的改变\"，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q17", "type": "listening", "no": 17, "q": "", "opts": ["Theyweremorelikelytoimplementaparticularbehavior", "They were more likely to make plans for their future", "They tended to be committed to a single goal", "They tended to focus on one thing at a time"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[17]提到，研究人员发现，与那些没有为未来行为制定计划的对照组相比，填写这句话的人实际去锻炼的可能性要高出两到三倍。心理学家将这些具体计划称为执行意图，因为它们陈述了你打算在何时、何地以及如何实施某项特定行为。(A)项是录音中 implement a particular behavior 的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】(B)项是根据录音中 make plans for their future 设置的干扰项，但录音中的逻辑是因为他们制定了具体的计划，所以他们更有可能去实施行为，而不是他们更有可能为未来制定计划，故排除；(C)项是根据录音中 a single goal 设置的干扰项，但录音说的是专注于单一目标的人更可能成功，而不是具有执行意图的个体只致力于单一目标，故排除；(D)项是根据录音中 focus on one thing at a time 设置的干扰项，但这是研究人员发现起作用的前提条件，而不是有执行意图的人一次只专注一件事，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q18", "type": "listening", "no": 18, "q": "", "opts": ["The ability to make the process automatic", "A practical timetable for implementation", "A lot of conscious effort to remember to do it", "Concentration on the benefits it will provide"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[18]提到，每次专注于一件事，还有一条基于科学的依据：当你开始培养新习惯时，需要耗费大量主观意志力才能记住去完成这件事。(C)项是录音中 a lot of conscious effort to remember to do it 的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】(A)项是根据录音中 the process is... automatic 设置的干扰项，但录音说的是\"自动化\"是习惯养成之后的结果，而不是\"开始新习惯\"所需要的条件，故排除；录音中未提及\"实用的执行时间表\"和\"专注于它将提供的好处\"，(B)项和(D)项无中生有，故均排除。\n\nRecording Two", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q19", "type": "listening", "no": 19, "q": "", "opts": ["It resembles that of Earth in many inconceivable ways", "It has a haze much thicker than previously believed", "It is 333.4°F cooler than earlier estimates", "It is far beyond any quick measurements. 11"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[19]提到，他们发现的一个较为奇特的现象是：冥王星大气层中的雾霾浓度远高于此前的预期。(B)项是录音中 the haze in Pluto's atmosphere was much thicker than previously thought 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】录音中未提到冥王星大气层与地球大气层相似，(A)项无中生有，故排除；(C)项是根据录音中 -333.4 degrees Fahrenheit 设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是零下333.4华氏度是冥王星大气的实际最高温度，而不是\"比之前估计的低333.4华氏度\"，故排除；(D)项是根据录音中 quick measurements 设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是探测器对大气层进行了快速测量，而不是\"超出了快速测量\"，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q20", "type": "listening", "no": 20, "q": "", "opts": ["The accumulation of solid particles in the air", "The distribution of gases in its atmosphere", "The clouds of particles reflecting sun rays", "The aggravated effects of climate change"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[20]提到，但在地球上，该行星的整体温度主要由大气层中气体的分布所决定。(B)项是录音中 the distribution of gases in its atmosphere 的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】(A)项是根据录音中 solid particles 设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是当固体颗粒悬浮于空气中时，我们也会看到朦胧的天空，而不是固体颗粒决定了地球的温度，故排除；(C)项是根据录音中 clouds of particles that reflect the Sun's rays 设置的干扰项，录音中虽然提及火山爆发会释放反射太阳光的粒子云，但紧接着强调我们的星球并不由这个过程主导，故排除；(D)项是根据录音中 effects of climate change 设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是有人提议模仿火山现象来缓解气候变化的影响，而未提到气候变化决定了地球的温度，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q21", "type": "listening", "no": 21, "q": "", "opts": ["If spectrums of light can be better detected by telescope", "If Pluto's hazy atmosphere can be more easily observed", "If Pluto's hazy atmosphere scatters strong sunlight", "If the light from the telescope confirms their idea"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[21]提到，研究人员只能静待观测，看望远镜观测到的光线是否能验证他们这一创新构想。(D)项是录音中 if the light from the telescope validates their bright idea 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】(A)项是根据录音中 detect different spectrums of light 设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是望远镜本身具备观测光谱的能力，并不是研究人员需要等待观察的结果，故排除；录音中未提及\"冥王星多雾的大气层是否能被更容易地观测到\"，(B)项无中生有，故排除；(C)项是根据录音中 it scatters sunlight 设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是冥王星大气层会散射阳光，这是他们理论模型中的既定前提，而不是等待验证的最终结果，故排除。\n\nRecording Three", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q22", "type": "listening", "no": 22, "q": "", "opts": ["Parents can play a decisive role in cultivating their children's musical talent", "Children are embarrassed by parents' failure to appreciate their music", "Parents can mould their 8- to 10-year-old children's music taste", "Children aged 8 to 10 are old enough to produce poetic lyrics"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[22]提到，研究人员得出结论：父母影响其8至10岁子女音乐品味的窗口期很短。(C)项是录音中 Parents... influence their eight-to-ten-year-old children's music taste 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】录音中提到父母可以影响孩子们的音乐品味，而非\"孩子们的音乐天赋\"，故排除(A)项；(B)项是根据录音中 be embarrassed by their mother and father's preferences 设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是孩子们还没有开始因为父母的偏好而感到尴尬，而不是因为未能欣赏而感到尴尬，故排除；(D)项是根据录音中 old enough to appreciate poetic lyrics 设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是孩子的年龄足够大，能够\"欣赏\"富有诗意的歌词，而不是\"创作\"，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q23", "type": "listening", "no": 23, "q": "", "opts": ["They startto show their own musical preference", "They tend to be more influenced by their peers", "They tend to gravitate away from their homes", "They are more open to new genres of music"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[23]提到，在青春期，孩子们往往会远离父母的选择，并且更容易受到同龄人的影响。(B)项是录音中 children... are more influenced by their peers 的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】(A)项中的\"开始展现自己的音乐偏好\"在录音中未提及，故排除；(C)项是根据录音中 gravitate away from their parents' choices 设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是远离父母的选择，而不是远离家，故排除；(D)项是根据录音中 have an openness to new music 设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是10岁前的孩子对音乐保持开放态度，而不是青春期的孩子，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q24", "type": "listening", "no": 24, "q": "", "opts": ["Enabling children to be receptivetomusicexperts'suggestions", "Helping children develop the ability to manage their emotions", "Familia rising children with different schools of music", "Introducing children to a variety of musical styles"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[24]提到，埃格曼博士声称，向孩子们介绍各种各样的音乐风格是日后培养良好品味的关键。(D)项是录音中 introducing children to a variety of musical styles 的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】(A)项是根据录音中 musical suggestions 设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是孩子们对父母的音乐建议接受度高，并未提及音乐专家的建议，故排除；(B)项是根据录音中 the ability to manage their emotions 设置的干扰项，但录音中说培养管理情绪的能力是拥有广泛音乐品味的益处，而不是培养品味的关键，故排除；(C)项是根据录音 familiarization 和 lots of different types of music 设置的干扰项，但这只是用来解释为什么接触多种音乐风格有效的科学依据，真正的核心行动依然是\"向孩子们介绍多种音乐风格\"，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q25", "type": "listening", "no": 25, "q": "", "opts": ["By enhancing children's appreciation of their music", "By pushing their music hard enough upon children", "By attending their concerts and live music events with children", "By introducing their music to children as early as possible"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[25]提到，他们还可以通过共同参加音乐会和现场音乐活动，帮助孩子们形成对艺术家及乐队的积极印象。(C)项是录音中 by going to concerts and live music events together 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n【干扰项排除】(A)项中的\"增强孩子们对音乐的欣赏能力\"在录音中未提及，故排除；(B)项是根据录音中 push their favourite music too hard on their children 设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是父母不要把最喜欢的音乐太强硬地塞给孩子，而不是大力推销，故排除；(D)项是根据录音 introducing children to a variety of musical styles and before this critical period 设置的干扰项，但录音中说的是在\"关键期前\"让孩子们接触多种多样的音乐，而不是\"尽早\"，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q46", "type": "reading", "no": 46, "q": "What plays an important role in determining how much self-discipline people experience?", "opts": ["Their belief about the nature of motivation", "Their use of internal energy for persisting", "Their conception of self-discipline", "Their ability to resist temptation"], "answer": 2, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语 determining 和 how much self-discipline people experience 可定位至第3段第1句。\n【答案】答案为(C)。第3段第1句提到，你的答案很重要，因为它决定了你将经历多少自律。由于本段首句提到了\"答案对于决定你经历多少自律很重要\"，因此需阅读上文了解这个答案是关于什么问题的。第2段第1句提到，你如何看待自己自律的本质。由此可知，第3段第1句中的\"your answer（你的答案）\"正是指人们对自律的看法。(C)项\"他们对自律的认知\"是对该信息的概括总结。\n\n【干扰项排除】原文强调对\"自律\"的看法，而非\"动力本质\"，(A)项偷换概念，故排除；第2段第2句提到，自律是否靠内部能量运转，但这只是对自律的一种猜想，并非决定因素，故排除(B)项；根据第1段第2句可知，抵制诱惑的能力是自律的表现，而非决定经历多少自律的因素，(D)项因果倒置，故排除。", "passage": "What does self-discipline look like at work? Sometimes, it's the ability to resist temptation, such as distractions to avoid a particular task. Other times it's the ability to persist with a task when your energy and motivation seem low.\n\nWhat do you believe about the nature of your own self-discipline? Is it something that runs on internal energy that is spent with use? Or is it something that is bolstered as you apply it and persist? Your answer matters, as it determines how much self-discipline you experience. For years, research in psychology pointed to self-control as a resource that is depleted with use and needs to be restored through breaks, rest, or sleep. More recently,however,research points to the importance of mindset. If you view self-discipline as an expendable resource, your willpower tends to fade with use. If you believe that exercising your self-control strengthens and fuels it, that is more likely to be your experience as you resist temptation or push yourself to get something done that is not appealing.\n\nIf you don't already view self-discipline as a force that feeds itself, what might you do? Reflect on times that you had initial difficulty resisting temptation, getting started on something,or sticking with it, but pushed ahead and accomplished the task or met the goal. That's the kind of experience we're talking about with this particular belief about self-discipline, and you've already had such an experience, so you've shown that it's possible. When you feel your willpower and motivation fading, remind yourself of those previous experiences and imagine the same happening now.\n\nWhat if your experiences have always seemed to indicate that your self-control diminishes with use, and you need breaks to recharge? There's nothing wrong with that as long as you can afford to take those breaks. If not, remind yourself that not everyone experiences self-discipline as a depletable resource and that your experience might be different if you focus on gathering more energy at this point rather than focusing on how difficult it is to continue or the need for a break.\n\nAlso, if you know someone who is a role model of self-discipline, picture that person in your situation, persisting in the task. Regardless of how you do it, as you push forward to continue to accomplish progress, be sure to mentally reinforce yourself by taking pride in your ability to exert more willpower as you persist. The benefits of exerting willpower are cumulative. Your self-discipline is akin to a muscle, and each time you resist temptation or persist in a task despite a lack of motivation, you strengthen that muscle, leading to less effort and resistance in similar instances in the future."}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q47", "type": "reading", "no": 47, "q": "What did years of research suggest about self-discipline?", "opts": ["It is a depletable resource", "It is bolstered through use", "Its strength is dependent on willpower", "Its exercise is an exhausting experience"], "answer": 0, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语 years of research 可定位至第3段第2句。\n【答案】答案为(A)。第3段第2句指出，多年来，心理学研究指出，自我控制是一种随使用而消耗的资源，需要通过休息、放松或睡眠来恢复。(A)项\"它是一种可消耗的资源\"是对该句信息的同义转述，其中 depletable 对应原文中的 depleted。\n\n【干扰项排除】由第2段第3句可知，自律是否可以通过应用而得到强化，只是对自律本质的一种猜想，不属于\"多年来的研究\"中的内容，故排除(B)项；第3段第4句指出，如果你将自律视为一种可消耗的资源，那么你的意志力往往会在使用过程中逐渐消退。由此可知，意志力是自律作用的结果，而非决定因素，(C)项颠倒因果，故排除；第3段第2句指出，自律需要恢复，但并未说运用自律\"令人筋疲力尽\"，(D)项过度推断，故排除。", "passage": "What does self-discipline look like at work? Sometimes, it's the ability to resist temptation, such as distractions to avoid a particular task. Other times it's the ability to persist with a task when your energy and motivation seem low.\n\nWhat do you believe about the nature of your own self-discipline? Is it something that runs on internal energy that is spent with use? Or is it something that is bolstered as you apply it and persist? Your answer matters, as it determines how much self-discipline you experience. For years, research in psychology pointed to self-control as a resource that is depleted with use and needs to be restored through breaks, rest, or sleep. More recently,however,research points to the importance of mindset. If you view self-discipline as an expendable resource, your willpower tends to fade with use. If you believe that exercising your self-control strengthens and fuels it, that is more likely to be your experience as you resist temptation or push yourself to get something done that is not appealing.\n\nIf you don't already view self-discipline as a force that feeds itself, what might you do? Reflect on times that you had initial difficulty resisting temptation, getting started on something,or sticking with it, but pushed ahead and accomplished the task or met the goal. That's the kind of experience we're talking about with this particular belief about self-discipline, and you've already had such an experience, so you've shown that it's possible. When you feel your willpower and motivation fading, remind yourself of those previous experiences and imagine the same happening now.\n\nWhat if your experiences have always seemed to indicate that your self-control diminishes with use, and you need breaks to recharge? There's nothing wrong with that as long as you can afford to take those breaks. If not, remind yourself that not everyone experiences self-discipline as a depletable resource and that your experience might be different if you focus on gathering more energy at this point rather than focusing on how difficult it is to continue or the need for a break.\n\nAlso, if you know someone who is a role model of self-discipline, picture that person in your situation, persisting in the task. Regardless of how you do it, as you push forward to continue to accomplish progress, be sure to mentally reinforce yourself by taking pride in your ability to exert more willpower as you persist. The benefits of exerting willpower are cumulative. Your self-discipline is akin to a muscle, and each time you resist temptation or persist in a task despite a lack of motivation, you strengthen that muscle, leading to less effort and resistance in similar instances in the future."}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q48", "type": "reading", "no": 48, "q": "Why does the author ask us to reflect on our experience of meeting the goal despite initial difficulty?", "opts": ["To show the urgency of abandoning outdated beliefs about self-control", "To show the feasibility of viewing self-discipline as a force fueling itself", "To support the argument for persistence in workplace accomplishments", "To add evidence to consistent findings in previous psychological research"], "answer": 1, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语 reflect on、meeting the goal 和 initial difficulty 可定位至第4段第2句。\n【答案】答案为(B)。第4段第2句提到，回想一下那些你最初难以抵制诱惑、难以开始做某事或难以坚持下去，但仍然坚持前进并完成任务或达成目标的时刻。第3句进一步指出回想这些经历的原因：这就是我们所说的关于自律的这种特定信念所涉及的体验，而你已然拥有这样的经历，所以你已经证明了这是可行的。再根据第4段第1句\"如果你还不认为自律是一种能自我滋养的力量，你可以怎么做？\"可知，第3句中 it's possible（这是可行的）中的 it 指代的就是\"自律可自我滋养\"这个观念，回想这类经历的目的正是为了证明这一观念是可行的。(B)项\"为了展示将自律视为自我强化力量的可行性\"是对该信息的概括总结，其中 feasibility 对应原文中的 possible；a force fueling itself 对应 a force that feeds itself。\n\n【干扰项排除】原文未提及摒弃过时观念的\"紧迫性\"，(A)项属于无中生有，故排除；虽然全文首句涉及职场相关表述(at work)，但回想经历与在职场成就中保持毅力并无关联点，(C)项偏离核心，故排除；作者让读者反思个人经历，是为了改变读者的心态，而非为先前心理学研究增添证据，(D)项与原文目的不符，故排除。", "passage": "What does self-discipline look like at work? Sometimes, it's the ability to resist temptation, such as distractions to avoid a particular task. Other times it's the ability to persist with a task when your energy and motivation seem low.\n\nWhat do you believe about the nature of your own self-discipline? Is it something that runs on internal energy that is spent with use? Or is it something that is bolstered as you apply it and persist? Your answer matters, as it determines how much self-discipline you experience. For years, research in psychology pointed to self-control as a resource that is depleted with use and needs to be restored through breaks, rest, or sleep. More recently,however,research points to the importance of mindset. If you view self-discipline as an expendable resource, your willpower tends to fade with use. If you believe that exercising your self-control strengthens and fuels it, that is more likely to be your experience as you resist temptation or push yourself to get something done that is not appealing.\n\nIf you don't already view self-discipline as a force that feeds itself, what might you do? Reflect on times that you had initial difficulty resisting temptation, getting started on something,or sticking with it, but pushed ahead and accomplished the task or met the goal. That's the kind of experience we're talking about with this particular belief about self-discipline, and you've already had such an experience, so you've shown that it's possible. When you feel your willpower and motivation fading, remind yourself of those previous experiences and imagine the same happening now.\n\nWhat if your experiences have always seemed to indicate that your self-control diminishes with use, and you need breaks to recharge? There's nothing wrong with that as long as you can afford to take those breaks. If not, remind yourself that not everyone experiences self-discipline as a depletable resource and that your experience might be different if you focus on gathering more energy at this point rather than focusing on how difficult it is to continue or the need for a break.\n\nAlso, if you know someone who is a role model of self-discipline, picture that person in your situation, persisting in the task. Regardless of how you do it, as you push forward to continue to accomplish progress, be sure to mentally reinforce yourself by taking pride in your ability to exert more willpower as you persist. The benefits of exerting willpower are cumulative. Your self-discipline is akin to a muscle, and each time you resist temptation or persist in a task despite a lack of motivation, you strengthen that muscle, leading to less effort and resistance in similar instances in the future."}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q49", "type": "reading", "no": 49, "q": "What are we advised to do if we cannot afford to take breaks to recharge?", "opts": ["Figure out what has gone wrong", "Focus on the hardest part of our task", "Exert ourselves to go on with the work", "Gather new energy from people around"], "answer": 2, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语 cannot afford to take breaks to recharge 可定位至第5段。\n【答案】答案为(C)。第5段第1、2句提到，如果能抽出时间休息以此恢复精力，那没什么问题。第3句指出，如果不能，那就提醒自己，并非每个人都将自律视为一种可消耗的资源，如果你此刻专注于积累更多能量……你的体验可能会有所不同。第6段第1句紧接着指出，如果你认识某个在自律方面堪称榜样的人，想象那个人处于你的处境，坚持完成任务。由此可知，如果你无法休息，那么唯一的选择就是坚持完成任务、继续前进。(C)项是对原文信息的同义转述，其中 go on with 对应原文中的 persisting in；work 对应 task。\n\n【干扰项排除】原文旨在强调心态的重要性，建议改变心态而非查明问题所在，(A)项背离主旨，故排除；根据第5段第3句可知，原文建议不要纠结于继续下去会遇到的困难(rather than focusing on how difficult it is to continue)，(B)项与原文相反，故排除；第5段第3句虽然提到积累更多能量，但未明确能量来自\"周围的人\"，第6段第1句提到想象榜样，而\"榜样\"不等同于\"周围的人\"，(D)项主观臆断，故排除。", "passage": "What does self-discipline look like at work? Sometimes, it's the ability to resist temptation, such as distractions to avoid a particular task. Other times it's the ability to persist with a task when your energy and motivation seem low.\n\nWhat do you believe about the nature of your own self-discipline? Is it something that runs on internal energy that is spent with use? Or is it something that is bolstered as you apply it and persist? Your answer matters, as it determines how much self-discipline you experience. For years, research in psychology pointed to self-control as a resource that is depleted with use and needs to be restored through breaks, rest, or sleep. More recently,however,research points to the importance of mindset. If you view self-discipline as an expendable resource, your willpower tends to fade with use. If you believe that exercising your self-control strengthens and fuels it, that is more likely to be your experience as you resist temptation or push yourself to get something done that is not appealing.\n\nIf you don't already view self-discipline as a force that feeds itself, what might you do? Reflect on times that you had initial difficulty resisting temptation, getting started on something,or sticking with it, but pushed ahead and accomplished the task or met the goal. That's the kind of experience we're talking about with this particular belief about self-discipline, and you've already had such an experience, so you've shown that it's possible. When you feel your willpower and motivation fading, remind yourself of those previous experiences and imagine the same happening now.\n\nWhat if your experiences have always seemed to indicate that your self-control diminishes with use, and you need breaks to recharge? There's nothing wrong with that as long as you can afford to take those breaks. If not, remind yourself that not everyone experiences self-discipline as a depletable resource and that your experience might be different if you focus on gathering more energy at this point rather than focusing on how difficult it is to continue or the need for a break.\n\nAlso, if you know someone who is a role model of self-discipline, picture that person in your situation, persisting in the task. Regardless of how you do it, as you push forward to continue to accomplish progress, be sure to mentally reinforce yourself by taking pride in your ability to exert more willpower as you persist. The benefits of exerting willpower are cumulative. Your self-discipline is akin to a muscle, and each time you resist temptation or persist in a task despite a lack of motivation, you strengthen that muscle, leading to less effort and resistance in similar instances in the future."}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q50", "type": "reading", "no": 50, "q": "Why does the author compare our self-discipline to a muscle?", "opts": ["The more it is applied, the more resistance it encounters", "The more it is exerted, the more proud we become", "The more it is enforced, the stronger our willpower proves", "The more it is exercised, the more strengthened it gets"], "answer": 3, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语 muscle 可定位至全文最后一句。\n【答案】答案为(D)。全文最后一句指出，你的自律就像一块肌肉，每次你抵制诱惑或在缺乏动力时坚持一项任务，你都在强化那块肌肉，从而在未来类似情境中减少所需的努力和阻力。结合常识及文章可知，肌肉的特性是越锻炼越强壮，作者将自律比作肌肉，准确凸显了\"锻炼自律\"与\"自律增强\"之间的正向关联。(D)项\"锻炼得越多，它就越强\"准确概括了这一类比的核心逻辑，其中 strengthened 对应原文中的 strengthen。\n\n【干扰项排除】根据末段末句可知，增强自律可以减少未来任务所需的努力和阻力，(A)项与原文相反，故排除；第6段第2句指出，一定要为自身在坚持过程中展现出的更强意志力而感到自豪，但这是\"reinforce yourself（强化自己）\"的方式，而非将自律比作肌肉的目的，(B)项混淆了手段与目的，故排除；末段末句强调的是\"强化自律\"，而非证明\"意志力有多强\"，(C)项重心偏移，故排除。", "passage": "What does self-discipline look like at work? Sometimes, it's the ability to resist temptation, such as distractions to avoid a particular task. Other times it's the ability to persist with a task when your energy and motivation seem low.\n\nWhat do you believe about the nature of your own self-discipline? Is it something that runs on internal energy that is spent with use? Or is it something that is bolstered as you apply it and persist? Your answer matters, as it determines how much self-discipline you experience. For years, research in psychology pointed to self-control as a resource that is depleted with use and needs to be restored through breaks, rest, or sleep. More recently,however,research points to the importance of mindset. If you view self-discipline as an expendable resource, your willpower tends to fade with use. If you believe that exercising your self-control strengthens and fuels it, that is more likely to be your experience as you resist temptation or push yourself to get something done that is not appealing.\n\nIf you don't already view self-discipline as a force that feeds itself, what might you do? Reflect on times that you had initial difficulty resisting temptation, getting started on something,or sticking with it, but pushed ahead and accomplished the task or met the goal. That's the kind of experience we're talking about with this particular belief about self-discipline, and you've already had such an experience, so you've shown that it's possible. When you feel your willpower and motivation fading, remind yourself of those previous experiences and imagine the same happening now.\n\nWhat if your experiences have always seemed to indicate that your self-control diminishes with use, and you need breaks to recharge? There's nothing wrong with that as long as you can afford to take those breaks. If not, remind yourself that not everyone experiences self-discipline as a depletable resource and that your experience might be different if you focus on gathering more energy at this point rather than focusing on how difficult it is to continue or the need for a break.\n\nAlso, if you know someone who is a role model of self-discipline, picture that person in your situation, persisting in the task. Regardless of how you do it, as you push forward to continue to accomplish progress, be sure to mentally reinforce yourself by taking pride in your ability to exert more willpower as you persist. The benefits of exerting willpower are cumulative. Your self-discipline is akin to a muscle, and each time you resist temptation or persist in a task despite a lack of motivation, you strengthen that muscle, leading to less effort and resistance in similar instances in the future."}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q51", "type": "reading", "no": 51, "q": "What do around half of parents ofteensdoaccordingtoa new study?", "opts": ["They set their kids'phones to filter out obscene information", "They try to alleviate the spread of explicit scenes on the internet", "They feel concerned about.their kids'viewing indecent content online", "They strive to prevent their kids from spending excessive screen time"], "answer": 2, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语 half of parents of teens 和 a new study 可定位到第2段第1句。\n【答案】答案为(C)。第2段第1句提到，一项新的研究显示，约有一半的青少年家长担心自己的孩子在网上接触到露骨内容。(C)项是对该句信息的同义转述。选项中的 kids' viewing 是对原文中的 be exposed to 的主动被动转换；选项中的 indecent content online 对应原文中的 explicit content online。\n\n【干扰项排除】(A)项是依据文中 changing the settings in their kids' phone 设置的干扰，但文中提到的\"修改孩子手机中的相关设置以及对相关内容进行监控\"是作者提出的建议，而非研究发现中父母已经采取的行动，故排除(A)项；(B)项是依据文中 there are ways to alleviate the spread of explicit content 设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"有办法减少此类内容的影响\"，属于建议而非家长的实际做法，故排除；新研究关注的是\"担心接触露骨内容\"，而非\"防止屏幕使用时间过长\"，故排除(D)项。", "passage": "Smartphones have always posed challenges for parents of teens. From social media apps to excessive screen time, the digital world is as threatening as the physical.\n\nA new study shows that about half of parents of teens are worried about their teens being exposed to explicit content online. This is a valid concern, of course. But there are ways to alleviate the spread of explicit content, from changing the settings in their kids' phone to monitoring such content with apps like Bark. Just as alarming, if not more so, is that the amount of time teens spend on social media can lead to anxiety and depression.\n\nExcessive screentime has detrimental effects,fora lot of obvious reasons. Constant media consumption inhibits healthy activities associated with childhood, like spending time outside, exercising, talking to friends and family. If it interferes with sleep, that's even worse.\n\nAmple research shows too much smartphone use produces significant anxiety, depression, and feelings of isolation in teens. Teens log on to social media or spend time online trying to make connections, but end up feeling more alone and sad than ever. Faceless communication not only produces feelings of isolation and loneliness but increases instances of online bullying and susceptibility to criminal conducts. Though I've banned most social media, I've still battled smartphone time with my own kids. A lot of the timeIwishI could throwmykids'smartphonesintothenearestlake. The convenience of communication- when they need a ride home from school after practice,for instance-often feels like it isn't worth the battle to keep their screen time low and productive, and continue to encourage“real-life\"activities outside and with friends and family. I understand the addiction and often tell them I'm not sure how I would have handled having a smartphone as a teen. As an adult, I too, struggle with overuse.\n\nIn a recent TV interview to promote a new film about a mother and daughter navigating social media use, actress Kate Winslet expressed her view on teens and social media-and her concern mirrors the trends.\"I do definitely feel that the world of social media is frightening to parents because we don't really know what's there,\" she said.\"We don't know how our children are using it. We don't know the effects that it's really having on them.\"\n\nI don't think the U.S. government should regulate social media and teen smartphone use, but it is a vital topic for parents to understand and take seriously. Unfortunately, now, parents don't just have to worry about real-world dangers, but all the ones sneaking online and the effects they have on teenagers and their mental health."}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q52", "type": "reading", "no": 52, "q": "What does a large amount of research show about teens'excessive smartphone use?", "opts": ["It allows them less time for meaningful activities", "It renders them more lonely and low-spirited", "It gives them less timefor social interactions", "It makes them more alert to online bullying"], "answer": 1, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语 numerous studies 和 heavy smartphone use 可定位到第5段第1句。\n【答案】答案为(B)。第5段第1句提到，大量研究表明，过度使用智能手机会使青少年产生明显的焦虑、抑郁以及孤独感。(B)项是对该句信息的同义转述。\n\n【干扰项排除】由第4段可知，(A)项是过度使用屏幕(excessive screen time)的影响，而非\"大量研究\"关于青少年过度使用智能手机的核心发现，故排除；(C)项是依据文中 talking to friends and family 设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"过度使用屏幕会妨碍与朋友和家人的交流等活动\"，而不是\"大量研究表明其会减少社交互动时间\"，故排除；(D)项是依据文中 increases instances of online bullying 设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"会增加网络霸凌事件的发生概率\"，而不是\"使青少年对网络霸凌更加警觉\"，故排除。", "passage": "Smartphones have always posed challenges for parents of teens. From social media apps to excessive screen time, the digital world is as threatening as the physical.\n\nA new study shows that about half of parents of teens are worried about their teens being exposed to explicit content online. This is a valid concern, of course. But there are ways to alleviate the spread of explicit content, from changing the settings in their kids' phone to monitoring such content with apps like Bark. Just as alarming, if not more so, is that the amount of time teens spend on social media can lead to anxiety and depression.\n\nExcessive screentime has detrimental effects,fora lot of obvious reasons. Constant media consumption inhibits healthy activities associated with childhood, like spending time outside, exercising, talking to friends and family. If it interferes with sleep, that's even worse.\n\nAmple research shows too much smartphone use produces significant anxiety, depression, and feelings of isolation in teens. Teens log on to social media or spend time online trying to make connections, but end up feeling more alone and sad than ever. Faceless communication not only produces feelings of isolation and loneliness but increases instances of online bullying and susceptibility to criminal conducts. Though I've banned most social media, I've still battled smartphone time with my own kids. A lot of the timeIwishI could throwmykids'smartphonesintothenearestlake. The convenience of communication- when they need a ride home from school after practice,for instance-often feels like it isn't worth the battle to keep their screen time low and productive, and continue to encourage“real-life\"activities outside and with friends and family. I understand the addiction and often tell them I'm not sure how I would have handled having a smartphone as a teen. As an adult, I too, struggle with overuse.\n\nIn a recent TV interview to promote a new film about a mother and daughter navigating social media use, actress Kate Winslet expressed her view on teens and social media-and her concern mirrors the trends.\"I do definitely feel that the world of social media is frightening to parents because we don't really know what's there,\" she said.\"We don't know how our children are using it. We don't know the effects that it's really having on them.\"\n\nI don't think the U.S. government should regulate social media and teen smartphone use, but it is a vital topic for parents to understand and take seriously. Unfortunately, now, parents don't just have to worry about real-world dangers, but all the ones sneaking online and the effects they have on teenagers and their mental health."}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q53", "type": "reading", "no": 53, "q": "What do we learn about the author's feelings regarding smartphones and screen time?", "opts": ["They are deep-seated", "They are instinctive", "They are unique", "They are mixed. 17"], "answer": 3, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语 the author's feelings 和 smartphones and screen time 可定位到第6段。\n【答案】答案为(D)。第6段第2、3句提到，很多时候，我甚至希望能把他们的智能手机直接扔进最近的湖里。然而，通信便利——比如，当他们训练结束后需要从学校搭车回家时——常常让我觉得并不值得为此付出那么大的代价。第6段第4、5句提到作者理解这种\"手机成瘾\"的吸引力，也常常告诉孩子们：如果自己在青少年时期就拥有智能手机，也不知道自己会如何应对……与过度使用手机的问题作斗争。由此可见，作者既看到手机的便利，也担忧其负面影响，其态度是复杂的。(D)项是对作者态度的准确概括。\n\n【干扰项排除】(A)项、(B)项和(C)项均不是作者对智能手机和屏幕使用时间的态度，故排除。", "passage": "Smartphones have always posed challenges for parents of teens. From social media apps to excessive screen time, the digital world is as threatening as the physical.\n\nA new study shows that about half of parents of teens are worried about their teens being exposed to explicit content online. This is a valid concern, of course. But there are ways to alleviate the spread of explicit content, from changing the settings in their kids' phone to monitoring such content with apps like Bark. Just as alarming, if not more so, is that the amount of time teens spend on social media can lead to anxiety and depression.\n\nExcessive screentime has detrimental effects,fora lot of obvious reasons. Constant media consumption inhibits healthy activities associated with childhood, like spending time outside, exercising, talking to friends and family. If it interferes with sleep, that's even worse.\n\nAmple research shows too much smartphone use produces significant anxiety, depression, and feelings of isolation in teens. Teens log on to social media or spend time online trying to make connections, but end up feeling more alone and sad than ever. Faceless communication not only produces feelings of isolation and loneliness but increases instances of online bullying and susceptibility to criminal conducts. Though I've banned most social media, I've still battled smartphone time with my own kids. A lot of the timeIwishI could throwmykids'smartphonesintothenearestlake. The convenience of communication- when they need a ride home from school after practice,for instance-often feels like it isn't worth the battle to keep their screen time low and productive, and continue to encourage“real-life\"activities outside and with friends and family. I understand the addiction and often tell them I'm not sure how I would have handled having a smartphone as a teen. As an adult, I too, struggle with overuse.\n\nIn a recent TV interview to promote a new film about a mother and daughter navigating social media use, actress Kate Winslet expressed her view on teens and social media-and her concern mirrors the trends.\"I do definitely feel that the world of social media is frightening to parents because we don't really know what's there,\" she said.\"We don't know how our children are using it. We don't know the effects that it's really having on them.\"\n\nI don't think the U.S. government should regulate social media and teen smartphone use, but it is a vital topic for parents to understand and take seriously. Unfortunately, now, parents don't just have to worry about real-world dangers, but all the ones sneaking online and the effects they have on teenagers and their mental health."}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q54", "type": "reading", "no": 54, "q": "What does actress Kate Winslet think about the world of social media?", "opts": ["It poses increasing challenges to parents trying to ensure internet safety", "It scares parents because they don't really know what is on the screen", "It is a sphere difficult for parents and their teens to navigate", "It is full of uncertainties frightful to parents and their teens"], "answer": 1, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语 Kate Winslet thinks 和 the world of social media 可定位到第7段。\n【答案】答案为(B)。第7段第2句提到，她表示：\"我确实觉得社交媒体世界让家长感到害怕，因为我们根本不知道那里究竟有什么。\"(B)项是对该句信息的同义转述。选项中的 scares parents 对应原文中的 frightening to parents；don't really know 是原文复现。\n\n【干扰项排除】(A)项原文未提及，故排除；(C)项是依据文中 a mother and daughter navigating social media use 设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"电影讲述母女共同应对社交媒体使用问题\"，这是电影内容，而非凯特本人对社交媒体世界的评价，故排除；(D)项是依据文中 We don't know how our children 设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"家长不了解孩子如何使用社交媒体以及其影响\"，并未说\"充满了让青少年恐惧的不确定性\"，故排除。", "passage": "Smartphones have always posed challenges for parents of teens. From social media apps to excessive screen time, the digital world is as threatening as the physical.\n\nA new study shows that about half of parents of teens are worried about their teens being exposed to explicit content online. This is a valid concern, of course. But there are ways to alleviate the spread of explicit content, from changing the settings in their kids' phone to monitoring such content with apps like Bark. Just as alarming, if not more so, is that the amount of time teens spend on social media can lead to anxiety and depression.\n\nExcessive screentime has detrimental effects,fora lot of obvious reasons. Constant media consumption inhibits healthy activities associated with childhood, like spending time outside, exercising, talking to friends and family. If it interferes with sleep, that's even worse.\n\nAmple research shows too much smartphone use produces significant anxiety, depression, and feelings of isolation in teens. Teens log on to social media or spend time online trying to make connections, but end up feeling more alone and sad than ever. Faceless communication not only produces feelings of isolation and loneliness but increases instances of online bullying and susceptibility to criminal conducts. Though I've banned most social media, I've still battled smartphone time with my own kids. A lot of the timeIwishI could throwmykids'smartphonesintothenearestlake. The convenience of communication- when they need a ride home from school after practice,for instance-often feels like it isn't worth the battle to keep their screen time low and productive, and continue to encourage“real-life\"activities outside and with friends and family. I understand the addiction and often tell them I'm not sure how I would have handled having a smartphone as a teen. As an adult, I too, struggle with overuse.\n\nIn a recent TV interview to promote a new film about a mother and daughter navigating social media use, actress Kate Winslet expressed her view on teens and social media-and her concern mirrors the trends.\"I do definitely feel that the world of social media is frightening to parents because we don't really know what's there,\" she said.\"We don't know how our children are using it. We don't know the effects that it's really having on them.\"\n\nI don't think the U.S. government should regulate social media and teen smartphone use, but it is a vital topic for parents to understand and take seriously. Unfortunately, now, parents don't just have to worry about real-world dangers, but all the ones sneaking online and the effects they have on teenagers and their mental health."}, {"id": "CET6-2-Q55", "type": "reading", "no": 55, "q": "What message does the author try to convey in the last paragraph?", "opts": ["Parents have to be concerned about dangers to their teens in both real and virtual worlds", "Governments have responsibilities in regulating social media and teens'smartphoneuse", "Parents should provide guidance to their teens in the use of social media", "Online dangers are as serious as problems with teenagers' mental health"], "answer": 0, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语 convey 和 the last paragraph 可定位到最后一段。\n【答案】答案为(A)。最后一段最后一句提到，遗憾的是，如今家长们不仅要担心现实世界中的各种危险，还必须担心所有悄然侵入网络的风险，以及它们对青少年以及青少年心理健康所造成的影响。(A)项是对该句信息的同义转述。\n\n【干扰项排除】(B)项与原文相反，原文明确提到\"不认为美国政府应该对社交媒体和青少年智能手机的使用进行监管\"，故排除；(C)项是依据文中 parents... understand and take seriously 设置的干扰，但文中强调的是家长应重视现实和网络中的各种风险，而不是强调\"提供社交媒体使用指导\"，故排除；(D)项是依据文中 the effects they have on teenagers and their mental health 设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"会影响青少年以及青少年心理健康\"，而不是\"网络危险与心理健康问题同样严重\"，故排除。", "passage": "Smartphones have always posed challenges for parents of teens. From social media apps to excessive screen time, the digital world is as threatening as the physical.\n\nA new study shows that about half of parents of teens are worried about their teens being exposed to explicit content online. This is a valid concern, of course. But there are ways to alleviate the spread of explicit content, from changing the settings in their kids' phone to monitoring such content with apps like Bark. Just as alarming, if not more so, is that the amount of time teens spend on social media can lead to anxiety and depression.\n\nExcessive screentime has detrimental effects,fora lot of obvious reasons. Constant media consumption inhibits healthy activities associated with childhood, like spending time outside, exercising, talking to friends and family. If it interferes with sleep, that's even worse.\n\nAmple research shows too much smartphone use produces significant anxiety, depression, and feelings of isolation in teens. Teens log on to social media or spend time online trying to make connections, but end up feeling more alone and sad than ever. Faceless communication not only produces feelings of isolation and loneliness but increases instances of online bullying and susceptibility to criminal conducts. Though I've banned most social media, I've still battled smartphone time with my own kids. A lot of the timeIwishI could throwmykids'smartphonesintothenearestlake. The convenience of communication- when they need a ride home from school after practice,for instance-often feels like it isn't worth the battle to keep their screen time low and productive, and continue to encourage“real-life\"activities outside and with friends and family. I understand the addiction and often tell them I'm not sure how I would have handled having a smartphone as a teen. As an adult, I too, struggle with overuse.\n\nIn a recent TV interview to promote a new film about a mother and daughter navigating social media use, actress Kate Winslet expressed her view on teens and social media-and her concern mirrors the trends.\"I do definitely feel that the world of social media is frightening to parents because we don't really know what's there,\" she said.\"We don't know how our children are using it. We don't know the effects that it's really having on them.\"\n\nI don't think the U.S. government should regulate social media and teen smartphone use, but it is a vital topic for parents to understand and take seriously. Unfortunately, now, parents don't just have to worry about real-world dangers, but all the ones sneaking online and the effects they have on teenagers and their mental health."}]}, {"id": "CET6-3", "level": "CET6", "set": 3, "year": 2026, "month": 6, "title": "六级 2026年6月 第3套", "audio": "", "minutes": 15, "questions": [{"id": "CET6-3-Q46", "type": "reading", "no": 46, "q": "Why have three artists brought a lawsuit against Stability AI, DeviantArt, and Midjourney?", "opts": ["They claim that the use of the Stable Diffusion tool is a violation of law", "They claim these companies infringed their intellectual property rights", "They want to stop the use of all AI tools in the creation of art works", "They want to prevent artists from being compelled to rely on AI tools"], "answer": 1, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语brought a lawsuit against和Stability AI, DeviantArt, and Midjourney可定位至首段。\n【答案】答案为(B)。首段提到，三位艺术家已对Stability AI、DeviantArt 和Midjourney 三家公司提起诉讼，指控它们文本生成图像的人工智能工具侵犯了数千名艺术家的著作权。(B)项是对该句信息的同义转述，故为答案。其中claim 对应原文中的alleging；infringed 和rights 为原词复现。\n【干扰项排除】第2段提到使用Stable Diffusion 工具训练模型的行为存在问题，但重点不是该工具的使用，而是训练数据侵权，(A)项避重就轻，故排除；根据第4段可知，此次诉讼还旨在阻止艺术家被取代，并非希望停止在艺术创作中使用所有人工智能工具，(C)项表述绝对，故排除；原文未提及\"防止艺术家被迫依赖人工智能工具\"，(D)项无中生有，故排除。", "passage": "Three artists have brought a lawsuit against Stability AI, DeviantArt, and Midjourney, alleging that the text- to-image AI tools have infringed the rights of thousands of artists.\n\nThe lawsuit claims that the Stable Diffusion tool used by Stability AI, DeviantArt, and Midjourney was trained on billions of copyrighted images downloaded from the internet and used by the companies “without compensation or consent from the artists.\n\nThe artists are represented by The Joseph Saveri Law Firm and Matthew Butterick..\n\n“If Stable Diffusion and similar products are allowed to continue to operate as they do now, the foreseeable result is they will replace the very artists whose stolen works power these AI products with whom they are competing,the law firm wrote in a press release, <4AI image products are not just an infringement of artists' rights; they will eliminate 'artist' as a viable career path. In addition to obtaining compensation for the wrongful conduct, this lawsuit seeks to prevent that outcome and ensure these products follow the same rules as any other new technology that involves the use of massive amounts of intellectual property. If streaming music can be accomplished within the law, so can AI products.\n\nSince text-to-image generators have increased in popularity in the past year, many artists have been vocalizing their opposition to AI art after seeing their own distorted signatures appear in AI art and finding out that their work was used to train the tools without their permission.\n\nNot only are many artists furious at the possible copyright violations of AI art, but they have also been frustrated by how AI tools are able to sidestep the labor and art processes they are required to go through. As a result, many artists have been trying to ban AI art from art-sharing platforms.\n\nAs AI innovation continues at a rapid speed, it seems that artists and lawmakers are trying to put a brake on further developments before if too late.\n\nMany tech enthusiasts are still rallying around the promise of AI tools to create detailed images cheaply and quickly- anonymous tech supporters have already published a response to Butterick and Joseph Saveri's lawsuit on a website, using the “fair use“ clause as the basis for why copyrighted images could be used by the tool. Fair use, a legal doctrine that allows for a limited usage of copyrighted materials without permission by the original creators for purposes such as teaching and news sharing, is what a lot of generative model creators claim that their training data is covered by. However, since fair use applies differently to each case, if hard to make a blanket statement about all works in a dataset as being applicable.\n\n•2 0 2 6 年6 月六级真题（第三套）•"}, {"id": "CET6-3-Q47", "type": "reading", "no": 47, "q": "What will happen if Stable Diffusion and similar products are allowed to continue to operate as they do now, according to the law firm?", "opts": ["Intellectual property laws will be hard to enforce", "Fewer artists will invest talent in artistic creation", "Artists will lose their means of subsistence", "Copied works will flood the art market"], "answer": 2, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语Stable Diffusion, continue to operate和according to the law firm可定位至第4段第1句。\n【答案】答案为(C)。第4段第1句提到，该律师事务所在新闻稿中写道：\"如果允许Stable Diffusion 及类似产品继续像现在这样运作，可预见的结果是，它们将取代正为这些人工智能产品提供被盗作品并与其竞争的艺术家。\"第2句继续指出，人工智能图像产品……还将消灭\"艺术家\"这一可行的职业道路。由此可知，艺术家将无法谋生，失去生存手段。(C)项准确概括了原文信息，故为答案。其中lose their means of subsistence对应原文中的replace the very artists和eliminate the 'artist' as a viable career path。\n【干扰项排除】原文未提及知识产权法律难以执行的问题，(A)项无中生有，故排除；根据第4段第1句可知，(A)将取代艺术家，也就是说将有更少的艺术家，甚至艺术家不复存在，而非更少的艺术家投入才华进行创作，(B)项语义偏移，故排除；原文未讨论\"复制品将充斥艺术市场\"，诉讼关注的是侵权和职业替代，(D)项无中生有，故排除。", "passage": "Three artists have brought a lawsuit against Stability AI, DeviantArt, and Midjourney, alleging that the text- to-image AI tools have infringed the rights of thousands of artists.\n\nThe lawsuit claims that the Stable Diffusion tool used by Stability AI, DeviantArt, and Midjourney was trained on billions of copyrighted images downloaded from the internet and used by the companies “without compensation or consent from the artists.\n\nThe artists are represented by The Joseph Saveri Law Firm and Matthew Butterick..\n\n“If Stable Diffusion and similar products are allowed to continue to operate as they do now, the foreseeable result is they will replace the very artists whose stolen works power these AI products with whom they are competing,the law firm wrote in a press release, <4AI image products are not just an infringement of artists' rights; they will eliminate 'artist' as a viable career path. In addition to obtaining compensation for the wrongful conduct, this lawsuit seeks to prevent that outcome and ensure these products follow the same rules as any other new technology that involves the use of massive amounts of intellectual property. If streaming music can be accomplished within the law, so can AI products.\n\nSince text-to-image generators have increased in popularity in the past year, many artists have been vocalizing their opposition to AI art after seeing their own distorted signatures appear in AI art and finding out that their work was used to train the tools without their permission.\n\nNot only are many artists furious at the possible copyright violations of AI art, but they have also been frustrated by how AI tools are able to sidestep the labor and art processes they are required to go through. As a result, many artists have been trying to ban AI art from art-sharing platforms.\n\nAs AI innovation continues at a rapid speed, it seems that artists and lawmakers are trying to put a brake on further developments before if too late.\n\nMany tech enthusiasts are still rallying around the promise of AI tools to create detailed images cheaply and quickly- anonymous tech supporters have already published a response to Butterick and Joseph Saveri's lawsuit on a website, using the “fair use“ clause as the basis for why copyrighted images could be used by the tool. Fair use, a legal doctrine that allows for a limited usage of copyrighted materials without permission by the original creators for purposes such as teaching and news sharing, is what a lot of generative model creators claim that their training data is covered by. However, since fair use applies differently to each case, if hard to make a blanket statement about all works in a dataset as being applicable.\n\n•2 0 2 6 年6 月六级真题（第三套）•"}, {"id": "CET6-3-Q48", "type": "reading", "no": 48, "q": "What makes artists furious aside from copyright infringement?", "opts": ["Art works produced with AI tools are not banned from art-sharing platforms", "Text-to-image generators have won such great popularity in the past year", "Their distorted signatures appear more and more frequently in AI art", "AI tools can bypass the considerable labor involved in creating art works"], "answer": 3, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语furious和aside from copyright infringement可定位至第6段。\n【答案】答案为(D)。第6段第1句提到，许多艺术家不仅对人工智能艺术可能涉及的版权侵权行为感到愤怒，更对其能够规避自身所需经历的劳动与艺术创作流程而感到沮丧。(D)项是对该句信息的同义转述，故为答案。其中bypass 对应原文中的sidestep；considerable labor 对应原文中的the labor and art processes。\n【干扰项排除】第6段最后一句提到，因此，许多艺术家一直在试图禁止人工智能艺术在艺术分享平台上的传播，这是许多艺术家感到愤怒的结果，而非原因，(A)项颠倒因果，故排除；第5段提到，文本生成图像工具越来越流行，这是背景信息，而非愤怒的原因，(B)项答非所问，故排除；第5段提到，许多艺术家在看到自己扭曲的签名出现在人工智能生成的艺术作品中后，纷纷公开反对人工智能艺术，这一现象属于版权侵权的具体表现(签名是作品的一部分)，有悖于题目\"aside from copyright infringement\"，故排除(C)项。", "passage": "Three artists have brought a lawsuit against Stability AI, DeviantArt, and Midjourney, alleging that the text- to-image AI tools have infringed the rights of thousands of artists.\n\nThe lawsuit claims that the Stable Diffusion tool used by Stability AI, DeviantArt, and Midjourney was trained on billions of copyrighted images downloaded from the internet and used by the companies “without compensation or consent from the artists.\n\nThe artists are represented by The Joseph Saveri Law Firm and Matthew Butterick..\n\n“If Stable Diffusion and similar products are allowed to continue to operate as they do now, the foreseeable result is they will replace the very artists whose stolen works power these AI products with whom they are competing,the law firm wrote in a press release, <4AI image products are not just an infringement of artists' rights; they will eliminate 'artist' as a viable career path. In addition to obtaining compensation for the wrongful conduct, this lawsuit seeks to prevent that outcome and ensure these products follow the same rules as any other new technology that involves the use of massive amounts of intellectual property. If streaming music can be accomplished within the law, so can AI products.\n\nSince text-to-image generators have increased in popularity in the past year, many artists have been vocalizing their opposition to AI art after seeing their own distorted signatures appear in AI art and finding out that their work was used to train the tools without their permission.\n\nNot only are many artists furious at the possible copyright violations of AI art, but they have also been frustrated by how AI tools are able to sidestep the labor and art processes they are required to go through. As a result, many artists have been trying to ban AI art from art-sharing platforms.\n\nAs AI innovation continues at a rapid speed, it seems that artists and lawmakers are trying to put a brake on further developments before if too late.\n\nMany tech enthusiasts are still rallying around the promise of AI tools to create detailed images cheaply and quickly- anonymous tech supporters have already published a response to Butterick and Joseph Saveri's lawsuit on a website, using the “fair use“ clause as the basis for why copyrighted images could be used by the tool. Fair use, a legal doctrine that allows for a limited usage of copyrighted materials without permission by the original creators for purposes such as teaching and news sharing, is what a lot of generative model creators claim that their training data is covered by. However, since fair use applies differently to each case, if hard to make a blanket statement about all works in a dataset as being applicable.\n\n•2 0 2 6 年6 月六级真题（第三套）•"}, {"id": "CET6-3-Q49", "type": "reading", "no": 49, "q": "What do we learn from the passage about many AI enthusiasts?", "opts": ["They look forward to AI tools creating good-quality art works", "They claim artists can use copyrighted materials just as teachers do", "They support Al's limited use of copyrighted images without permission", "They wish the t4fair use\" clause would be applicable to all works in a dataset"], "answer": 2, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语many AI enthusiasts可定位至第8段。\n【答案】答案为(C)。第8段提到，许多科技爱好者仍对人工智能工具能够廉价、快速地生成详细图像的前景充满期待……以\"合理使用\"条款作为该工具可使用受版权保护图像的依据。第9段第1句详细解释了\"合理使用\"条款：\"合理使用\"是一项法律原则，允许在无须原作者许可的情况下，对受版权保护的材料进行有限使用。由此可知，他们对人工智能工具持支持、拥护的态度，并明确主张可以在未经许可的情况下有限使用受版权保护的图像。(C)项是对该部分信息的合理概括，故为答案。其中support 对应原文中的rallying around；limited use 对应原文中的a limited usage；copyrighted images 和without permission 为原词复现。\n【干扰项排除】(A)项是依据第8段中的create detailed images设置的干扰，但这不等同于(A)项中的creating good-quality art works，且不是他们主张的核心，故排除；原文未提及艺术家与教师在使用受版权保护的材料方面的类比，(B)项无中生有，故排除；末段最后一句提到，很难对数据集里的所有作品一概而论，断言其均适用\"合理使用\"这一原则，这是作者的观点，而非人工智能爱好者的主张，(D)项偷换主体，故排除。", "passage": "Three artists have brought a lawsuit against Stability AI, DeviantArt, and Midjourney, alleging that the text- to-image AI tools have infringed the rights of thousands of artists.\n\nThe lawsuit claims that the Stable Diffusion tool used by Stability AI, DeviantArt, and Midjourney was trained on billions of copyrighted images downloaded from the internet and used by the companies “without compensation or consent from the artists.\n\nThe artists are represented by The Joseph Saveri Law Firm and Matthew Butterick..\n\n“If Stable Diffusion and similar products are allowed to continue to operate as they do now, the foreseeable result is they will replace the very artists whose stolen works power these AI products with whom they are competing,the law firm wrote in a press release, <4AI image products are not just an infringement of artists' rights; they will eliminate 'artist' as a viable career path. In addition to obtaining compensation for the wrongful conduct, this lawsuit seeks to prevent that outcome and ensure these products follow the same rules as any other new technology that involves the use of massive amounts of intellectual property. If streaming music can be accomplished within the law, so can AI products.\n\nSince text-to-image generators have increased in popularity in the past year, many artists have been vocalizing their opposition to AI art after seeing their own distorted signatures appear in AI art and finding out that their work was used to train the tools without their permission.\n\nNot only are many artists furious at the possible copyright violations of AI art, but they have also been frustrated by how AI tools are able to sidestep the labor and art processes they are required to go through. As a result, many artists have been trying to ban AI art from art-sharing platforms.\n\nAs AI innovation continues at a rapid speed, it seems that artists and lawmakers are trying to put a brake on further developments before if too late.\n\nMany tech enthusiasts are still rallying around the promise of AI tools to create detailed images cheaply and quickly- anonymous tech supporters have already published a response to Butterick and Joseph Saveri's lawsuit on a website, using the “fair use“ clause as the basis for why copyrighted images could be used by the tool. Fair use, a legal doctrine that allows for a limited usage of copyrighted materials without permission by the original creators for purposes such as teaching and news sharing, is what a lot of generative model creators claim that their training data is covered by. However, since fair use applies differently to each case, if hard to make a blanket statement about all works in a dataset as being applicable.\n\n•2 0 2 6 年6 月六级真题（第三套）•"}, {"id": "CET6-3-Q50", "type": "reading", "no": 50, "q": "What does the author think of the 4<fair use\" clause in copyright law?", "opts": ["It may determine the final verdict of the case", "It may not apply to the present legal case", "It does not cover the works of all artists", "It is subject to different interpretations"], "answer": 1, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语author和\"fair use\" term可定位至末段。\n【答案】答案为(B)。末段最后一句提到，然而，由于合理使用的适用情况因个案而异，因此很难对数据集里的所有作品一概而论，断言其均适用这一原则。由此可知，不是所有案件和作品都适用这一原则，(B)项\"它可能不适用于当前的法律案件\"是对该句信息的合理推断，故为答案。\n【干扰项排除】作者只是指出\"合理使用\"条款的适用情况因个案而异，未讨论其对本案判决结果的影响，(A)项无中生有，故排除；(C)项是依据末段最后一句中的\"it's hard to make a blanket statement about all works in a dataset as being applicable\"设置的干扰，\"很难对数据集中的所有作品一概而论\"并不等同于\"不涵盖所有艺术家的作品\"，(C)项表述绝对，故排除；末段作者强调的是个案适用性的差异，而非这一条款存在不同的解释，两者侧重点不同，故排除(D)项。", "passage": "Three artists have brought a lawsuit against Stability AI, DeviantArt, and Midjourney, alleging that the text- to-image AI tools have infringed the rights of thousands of artists.\n\nThe lawsuit claims that the Stable Diffusion tool used by Stability AI, DeviantArt, and Midjourney was trained on billions of copyrighted images downloaded from the internet and used by the companies “without compensation or consent from the artists.\n\nThe artists are represented by The Joseph Saveri Law Firm and Matthew Butterick..\n\n“If Stable Diffusion and similar products are allowed to continue to operate as they do now, the foreseeable result is they will replace the very artists whose stolen works power these AI products with whom they are competing,the law firm wrote in a press release, <4AI image products are not just an infringement of artists' rights; they will eliminate 'artist' as a viable career path. In addition to obtaining compensation for the wrongful conduct, this lawsuit seeks to prevent that outcome and ensure these products follow the same rules as any other new technology that involves the use of massive amounts of intellectual property. If streaming music can be accomplished within the law, so can AI products.\n\nSince text-to-image generators have increased in popularity in the past year, many artists have been vocalizing their opposition to AI art after seeing their own distorted signatures appear in AI art and finding out that their work was used to train the tools without their permission.\n\nNot only are many artists furious at the possible copyright violations of AI art, but they have also been frustrated by how AI tools are able to sidestep the labor and art processes they are required to go through. As a result, many artists have been trying to ban AI art from art-sharing platforms.\n\nAs AI innovation continues at a rapid speed, it seems that artists and lawmakers are trying to put a brake on further developments before if too late.\n\nMany tech enthusiasts are still rallying around the promise of AI tools to create detailed images cheaply and quickly- anonymous tech supporters have already published a response to Butterick and Joseph Saveri's lawsuit on a website, using the “fair use“ clause as the basis for why copyrighted images could be used by the tool. Fair use, a legal doctrine that allows for a limited usage of copyrighted materials without permission by the original creators for purposes such as teaching and news sharing, is what a lot of generative model creators claim that their training data is covered by. However, since fair use applies differently to each case, if hard to make a blanket statement about all works in a dataset as being applicable.\n\n•2 0 2 6 年6 月六级真题（第三套）•"}, {"id": "CET6-3-Q51", "type": "reading", "no": 51, "q": "What do we learn about the current electric supply system in Africa?", "opts": ["It curbs the economic development of the continent", "It mainly depends on the polluting diesel generators", "It is the primary cause of inequitable distribution of wealth", "It deprives many Africans of the opportunity to use computers"], "answer": 0, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语the current electric supply system可定位至第2段。\n【答案】答案为(A)。第2段第1、2句提到，当前的电力供应体系压制了像阿戈克和他的学生这类人群的经济才能。相反，我们应该释放他们的潜力，让非洲真正实现其经济发展潜能。由此可知，非洲电力供应体系压制了个体的经济才能，从而阻碍了整个非洲大陆的经济发展。(A)项是对此处信息的概括总结，故为答案。\n【干扰项排除】(B)项是依据文中fuel for toxic, polluting generators设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"由于供电不足，人们不得不依赖有毒且污染环境的柴油发电机作为替代方案\"，故排除；(C)项说的是\"能源获取机会的不平等\"，而不是\"电力供应体系是财富分配不均的主要原因\"，故排除(C)项；(D)项是依据文中teaching computer skills ... were canceled设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"教授计算机技能的课程被迫取消\"，故排除。", "passage": "On a warm October morning, Daniel Agok woke up to another disappointing day at his school in Wanyjok, South Sudan. The classes he had planned teaching computer skills to aspiring young professionals were canceled, due to an unreliable diesel generator and the absence of a municipal power grid in his area. Now, he would be losing frustrated customers as well as income. Agok is just one of the hundreds of millions of people in sub-Saharan Africa, many of whom are business owners, who still do not have dependable electricity. This inequitable access to energy continues to put their livelihood and future at risk.\n\nThe current system keeps a lid on the economic talents of people like Agok and his students. We must instead unleash their abilities and let Africa reach its true economic potential. That means wiring Africa with clean, dependable energy.\n\nHistorically, national governments on the continent have led electrification efforts, but fewer than half of the people surveyed say that the national grid is providing a reliable supply of electricity. This has led to expensive and harmful temporary “solutions.” In Nigeria alone experts estimate people spend $13 billion every year on fuel fbr toxic, polluting generators. That number will only grow as the continent's population hits 2.5 billion people by 2050. Deploying modern grid infrastructure with speed and at scale is essential to replacing these temporary solutions with reliable and affordable alternatives. The rapidly growing private power sector in Africa holds the key to achieving the speed and scale necessary to close the continent's energy access gap. Private companies today are building world-class infrastructure and driving forward economic growth with sustainable business models. In fact, many governments agree and have begun to embrace the private sector as partners in providing reliable power at scale. In South Africa, President Cyril Ramaphosa has streamlined licensing requirements and enlisted the private sector to play a key role. South Africa is just one example. Across the continent, the private sector has delivered power faster and more reliably, and governments are increasingly turning to private partners.\n\nWith the right funding and structure in place, the private sector will come together to provide this essential service. After all, electrifying sub-Saharan Africa is a $350 billion opportunity.\n\nToday, Daniel Agok is steadily growing his business in South Sudan. His town of Wanyjok was electrified by a private company, providing clean, reliable, and affordable power to his business and his neighbors. Agok now operates his growing computer skills training business smoothly. His classes are no longer affected by the smell and the constant noise of the diesel generator, and he saves money with more affordable power. His students are developing hardware and software skills."}, {"id": "CET6-3-Q52", "type": "reading", "no": 52, "q": "What do we learn from the passage about Africa's past electrification efforts?", "opts": ["They have failed to meet most Africans' need of a reliable power supply", "They have failed to build a national grid to provide electric power", "They have unleashed the true economic potential of the continent", "They have aimed at wiring Africa with clean, dependable energy"], "answer": 0, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语Africa's past electrification efforts可定位至第3段。\n【答案】答案为(A)。第3段提到，从历史上看，非洲各国政府一直主导电气化建设工作，但不到一半的受访者表示国家电网能够提供稳定可靠的电力供应。由此可知，过去的电气化建设收效甚微，超过半数民众无法获得稳定可靠的电力，(A)项是对该句信息的概括总结，故为答案。\n【干扰项排除】(B)项是依据文中the national grid设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"国家电网无法提供稳定可靠的电力供应\"，而不是\"未能建成国家电网\"，故排除；(C)项是依据文中let Africa reach its true economic potential设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"非洲尚未实现其真正的经济潜力\"，而不是\"释放了经济潜力\"，故排除；(D)项是依据文中wiring Africa with clean, dependable energy设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"未来应该为非洲铺设\"，而不是\"过去的电气化建设工作旨在实现这一目标\"，故排除。", "passage": "On a warm October morning, Daniel Agok woke up to another disappointing day at his school in Wanyjok, South Sudan. The classes he had planned teaching computer skills to aspiring young professionals were canceled, due to an unreliable diesel generator and the absence of a municipal power grid in his area. Now, he would be losing frustrated customers as well as income. Agok is just one of the hundreds of millions of people in sub-Saharan Africa, many of whom are business owners, who still do not have dependable electricity. This inequitable access to energy continues to put their livelihood and future at risk.\n\nThe current system keeps a lid on the economic talents of people like Agok and his students. We must instead unleash their abilities and let Africa reach its true economic potential. That means wiring Africa with clean, dependable energy.\n\nHistorically, national governments on the continent have led electrification efforts, but fewer than half of the people surveyed say that the national grid is providing a reliable supply of electricity. This has led to expensive and harmful temporary “solutions.” In Nigeria alone experts estimate people spend $13 billion every year on fuel fbr toxic, polluting generators. That number will only grow as the continent's population hits 2.5 billion people by 2050. Deploying modern grid infrastructure with speed and at scale is essential to replacing these temporary solutions with reliable and affordable alternatives. The rapidly growing private power sector in Africa holds the key to achieving the speed and scale necessary to close the continent's energy access gap. Private companies today are building world-class infrastructure and driving forward economic growth with sustainable business models. In fact, many governments agree and have begun to embrace the private sector as partners in providing reliable power at scale. In South Africa, President Cyril Ramaphosa has streamlined licensing requirements and enlisted the private sector to play a key role. South Africa is just one example. Across the continent, the private sector has delivered power faster and more reliably, and governments are increasingly turning to private partners.\n\nWith the right funding and structure in place, the private sector will come together to provide this essential service. After all, electrifying sub-Saharan Africa is a $350 billion opportunity.\n\nToday, Daniel Agok is steadily growing his business in South Sudan. His town of Wanyjok was electrified by a private company, providing clean, reliable, and affordable power to his business and his neighbors. Agok now operates his growing computer skills training business smoothly. His classes are no longer affected by the smell and the constant noise of the diesel generator, and he saves money with more affordable power. His students are developing hardware and software skills."}, {"id": "CET6-3-Q53", "type": "reading", "no": 53, "q": "What is essential to tackling the power supply shortage in Africa?", "opts": ["Replacing temporary solutions with long-term strategies", "Accelerating economic growth with sustainable policies", "Restructuring national power generation companies", "Putting in place a large-scale modem power grid speedily"], "answer": 3, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语crucial和tackling the power supply shortage可定位至第4段最后一句。\n【答案】答案为(D)。第4段最后一句提到，快速、大规模地部署现代化电网基础设施，是用可靠且负担得起的替代方案取代这些临时措施的关键。(D)项是对该句信息的同义转述，故为答案。其中Putting in place对应原文中的Deploying；modern power grid对应原文中的modern grid infrastructure。\n【干扰项排除】原文只提到要取代这些临时措施，但没有说\"用长期战略取代\"是解决问题的关键，故排除(A)项；(B)项是依据文中driving forward economic growth with sustainable business models设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"私营企业通过可持续的商业模式推动经济增长\"，这并非\"解决电力短缺的关键\"，故排除；文中没有提到\"重组国家电力生产企业\"的相关内容，故排除(C)项。", "passage": "On a warm October morning, Daniel Agok woke up to another disappointing day at his school in Wanyjok, South Sudan. The classes he had planned teaching computer skills to aspiring young professionals were canceled, due to an unreliable diesel generator and the absence of a municipal power grid in his area. Now, he would be losing frustrated customers as well as income. Agok is just one of the hundreds of millions of people in sub-Saharan Africa, many of whom are business owners, who still do not have dependable electricity. This inequitable access to energy continues to put their livelihood and future at risk.\n\nThe current system keeps a lid on the economic talents of people like Agok and his students. We must instead unleash their abilities and let Africa reach its true economic potential. That means wiring Africa with clean, dependable energy.\n\nHistorically, national governments on the continent have led electrification efforts, but fewer than half of the people surveyed say that the national grid is providing a reliable supply of electricity. This has led to expensive and harmful temporary “solutions.” In Nigeria alone experts estimate people spend $13 billion every year on fuel fbr toxic, polluting generators. That number will only grow as the continent's population hits 2.5 billion people by 2050. Deploying modern grid infrastructure with speed and at scale is essential to replacing these temporary solutions with reliable and affordable alternatives. The rapidly growing private power sector in Africa holds the key to achieving the speed and scale necessary to close the continent's energy access gap. Private companies today are building world-class infrastructure and driving forward economic growth with sustainable business models. In fact, many governments agree and have begun to embrace the private sector as partners in providing reliable power at scale. In South Africa, President Cyril Ramaphosa has streamlined licensing requirements and enlisted the private sector to play a key role. South Africa is just one example. Across the continent, the private sector has delivered power faster and more reliably, and governments are increasingly turning to private partners.\n\nWith the right funding and structure in place, the private sector will come together to provide this essential service. After all, electrifying sub-Saharan Africa is a $350 billion opportunity.\n\nToday, Daniel Agok is steadily growing his business in South Sudan. His town of Wanyjok was electrified by a private company, providing clean, reliable, and affordable power to his business and his neighbors. Agok now operates his growing computer skills training business smoothly. His classes are no longer affected by the smell and the constant noise of the diesel generator, and he saves money with more affordable power. His students are developing hardware and software skills."}, {"id": "CET6-3-Q54", "type": "reading", "no": 54, "q": "What are many governments in Africa doing to solve the electricity supply problem?", "opts": ["Focusing on building world-class infrastructures", "Relying on the private sector for scale economy", "Launching sustainable business models", "Cooperating with private companies"], "answer": 3, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语many governments in Africa和supply problem可定位至第5段第3句。\n【答案】答案为(D)。第5段第3句提到，事实上，许多政府对此表示认同，并已开始将私营部门视为实现大规模可靠供电的合作伙伴。(D)项是对该句信息的同义转述，故为答案。其中Cooperating with private companies对应原文中的embrace the private sector as partners。\n【干扰项排除】(A)项是依据文中Private companies today are building world-class infrastructure设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"私营企业正在建设世界一流的基础设施\"，而不是\"非洲国家政府\"，故排除；(B)项是依据文中providing reliable power at scale设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"政府将私营部门视为合作伙伴，以实现大规模可靠供电\"，而不是\"依靠私营部门实现规模经济\"，故排除；(C)项是依据文中driving forward economic growth with sustainable business models设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"私营企业通过可持续的商业模式推动经济增长\"，而不是\"政府正在推出可持续的商业模式\"，故排除。", "passage": "On a warm October morning, Daniel Agok woke up to another disappointing day at his school in Wanyjok, South Sudan. The classes he had planned teaching computer skills to aspiring young professionals were canceled, due to an unreliable diesel generator and the absence of a municipal power grid in his area. Now, he would be losing frustrated customers as well as income. Agok is just one of the hundreds of millions of people in sub-Saharan Africa, many of whom are business owners, who still do not have dependable electricity. This inequitable access to energy continues to put their livelihood and future at risk.\n\nThe current system keeps a lid on the economic talents of people like Agok and his students. We must instead unleash their abilities and let Africa reach its true economic potential. That means wiring Africa with clean, dependable energy.\n\nHistorically, national governments on the continent have led electrification efforts, but fewer than half of the people surveyed say that the national grid is providing a reliable supply of electricity. This has led to expensive and harmful temporary “solutions.” In Nigeria alone experts estimate people spend $13 billion every year on fuel fbr toxic, polluting generators. That number will only grow as the continent's population hits 2.5 billion people by 2050. Deploying modern grid infrastructure with speed and at scale is essential to replacing these temporary solutions with reliable and affordable alternatives. The rapidly growing private power sector in Africa holds the key to achieving the speed and scale necessary to close the continent's energy access gap. Private companies today are building world-class infrastructure and driving forward economic growth with sustainable business models. In fact, many governments agree and have begun to embrace the private sector as partners in providing reliable power at scale. In South Africa, President Cyril Ramaphosa has streamlined licensing requirements and enlisted the private sector to play a key role. South Africa is just one example. Across the continent, the private sector has delivered power faster and more reliably, and governments are increasingly turning to private partners.\n\nWith the right funding and structure in place, the private sector will come together to provide this essential service. After all, electrifying sub-Saharan Africa is a $350 billion opportunity.\n\nToday, Daniel Agok is steadily growing his business in South Sudan. His town of Wanyjok was electrified by a private company, providing clean, reliable, and affordable power to his business and his neighbors. Agok now operates his growing computer skills training business smoothly. His classes are no longer affected by the smell and the constant noise of the diesel generator, and he saves money with more affordable power. His students are developing hardware and software skills."}, {"id": "CET6-3-Q55", "type": "reading", "no": 55, "q": "Why will private companies be motivated to join the effort of electrifying sub-Saharan Africa?", "opts": ["There is big money to be made", "They have enough funding in place", "South Africa has set a successful example", "Governments are turning to them for help"], "answer": 0, "why": "【定位】根据题干中的关键词语private companies和sub-Saharan Africa可定位至第7段。\n【答案】答案为(A)。第7段提到，只要具备适当的资金支持和制度框架，私营部门就会携手提供这项至关重要的服务。毕竟，为撒哈拉以南的非洲实现全面电气化，是一个价值3500 亿美元的机遇。(A)项是对该处信息的同义转述，故为答案。其中big money to be made对应原文中的a $350 billion opportunity。\n【干扰项排除】(B)项是依据文中With the right funding and structure in place设置的干扰，但文中说的是参与服务项目的条件，并不是说私营企业\"已经拥有充足资金\"，故排除；(C)项是依据文中South Africa is just one example设置的干扰，但文中提到南非只是私营部门参与电力建设的一个案例，并非\"因为南非树立了成功典范才参与\"，故排除；(D)项是依据文中governments are increasingly turning to private partners设置的干扰，但这是合作趋势，不是动力，故排除。", "passage": "On a warm October morning, Daniel Agok woke up to another disappointing day at his school in Wanyjok, South Sudan. The classes he had planned teaching computer skills to aspiring young professionals were canceled, due to an unreliable diesel generator and the absence of a municipal power grid in his area. Now, he would be losing frustrated customers as well as income. Agok is just one of the hundreds of millions of people in sub-Saharan Africa, many of whom are business owners, who still do not have dependable electricity. This inequitable access to energy continues to put their livelihood and future at risk.\n\nThe current system keeps a lid on the economic talents of people like Agok and his students. We must instead unleash their abilities and let Africa reach its true economic potential. That means wiring Africa with clean, dependable energy.\n\nHistorically, national governments on the continent have led electrification efforts, but fewer than half of the people surveyed say that the national grid is providing a reliable supply of electricity. This has led to expensive and harmful temporary “solutions.” In Nigeria alone experts estimate people spend $13 billion every year on fuel fbr toxic, polluting generators. That number will only grow as the continent's population hits 2.5 billion people by 2050. Deploying modern grid infrastructure with speed and at scale is essential to replacing these temporary solutions with reliable and affordable alternatives. The rapidly growing private power sector in Africa holds the key to achieving the speed and scale necessary to close the continent's energy access gap. Private companies today are building world-class infrastructure and driving forward economic growth with sustainable business models. In fact, many governments agree and have begun to embrace the private sector as partners in providing reliable power at scale. In South Africa, President Cyril Ramaphosa has streamlined licensing requirements and enlisted the private sector to play a key role. South Africa is just one example. Across the continent, the private sector has delivered power faster and more reliably, and governments are increasingly turning to private partners.\n\nWith the right funding and structure in place, the private sector will come together to provide this essential service. After all, electrifying sub-Saharan Africa is a $350 billion opportunity.\n\nToday, Daniel Agok is steadily growing his business in South Sudan. His town of Wanyjok was electrified by a private company, providing clean, reliable, and affordable power to his business and his neighbors. Agok now operates his growing computer skills training business smoothly. His classes are no longer affected by the smell and the constant noise of the diesel generator, and he saves money with more affordable power. His students are developing hardware and software skills."}]}, {"id": "CET4-1", "level": "CET4", "set": 1, "year": 2026, "month": 6, "title": "四级 2026年6月 第1套", "audio": "audio/mock/cet4_2026_1.mp3", "minutes": 40, "questions": [{"id": "CET4-1-Q1", "type": "listening", "no": 1, "q": "", "opts": ["He had bought meals with his online food ordering account", "His son had used his cell phone to pay for playing a game", "His son had ordered food from several restaurants", "He had dined out at a famous five-star restaurant"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[1]提到，上周末，一名男子6岁的儿子从多家餐厅线上点了一堆食物，导致他收到了一张1,000美元的账单。(C)项是对录音中his 6-year-old son ordered a virtual buffet of food from several restaurants的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：录音中是他的儿子在线上订了食物，而不是他自己，故排除(A)项；录音中表明他儿子付的是食物费用，而非“游戏费用”，故排除(B)项；录音中未提及在五星级餐厅用餐，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q2", "type": "listening", "no": 2, "q": "", "opts": ["Teach his son a lesson", "Change his password", "Ask his neighbors for help", "Buy a bigger refrigerator"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[2]提到，这位父亲打算更改自己的密码。(B)项是对录音中change的词复现，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：(A)项在录音中未提及，故排除；(C)项是依据录音中的neighbors设置的干扰，但录音中说的是邀请一些邻居来吃饭，而不是向邻居寻求帮助，故排除；(D)项是依据录音中的refrigerators设置的干扰，但录音中提到的是大部分食物都存进了家里的冰箱里，而非要买一个更大的冰箱，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q3", "type": "listening", "no": 3, "q": "", "opts": ["They examined how visitors affected animal behaviour in zoos", "They studied when the best time to feed animals in public was", "They studied how animals adapted themselves to life in zoos", "They examined what behaviours indicated animal boredom"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[3]提到，他们研究了游客如何影响动物园内250多种动物的行为。(A)项是对录音中They examined how visitors affected the behaviour of more than 250 species in zoos的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：录音中仅提到在投喂时段动物行为的变化，并未研究投喂的最佳时间，故排除(B)项；录音仅提到部分动物物种很可能已经习惯了人类的存在，但并未提到专家研究了动物如何适应动物园生活，故排除(C)项；录音仅提到重复性行为通常代表动物无聊，但并未提到专家研究了哪些行为表明动物感到无聊，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q4", "type": "listening", "no": 4, "q": "", "opts": ["They socialise with zookeepers as well as with each other", "They show good adaptability to living with humans", "They have been kept as pets by humans", "They have been born and raised in zoos"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[4]提到，部分动物物种是在动物园中出生并长大的，因此很可能已经习惯了人类的存在。(D)项是对录音中have been born and raised in zoos的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：录音仅提到在公众喂食时段，大象之间的社交互动显著增加，但并未提到与动物饲养员进行社交，且这也并非部分动物物种习惯人类存在的原因，故排除(A)项；“良好的适应能力”是动物习惯人类存在的表现，并非其原因，故排除(B)项；录音未提到动物被人类作为宠物饲养，故排除(C)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q5", "type": "listening", "no": 5, "q": "", "opts": ["Adjusting it", "Suspending it", "Reevaluating it", "Expanding it"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[5]提到，丹佛市正暂停其深受欢迎的电动自行车激励项目。(B)项是对录音中suspending的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：录音提到，市政府随后调整了该项目，限制每月申请人数，周一，市政府宣布暂停该项目，因此调整只是项目暂停前的临时操作，并非丹佛市的核心举措，故排除(A)项；(C)项和(D)项在录音中未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q6", "type": "listening", "no": 6, "q": "", "opts": ["They could buy an electric bike for $400 in April", "They could buy their first electric bike at a discount", "They could enjoy free after-sale service for their purchase", "They could get $1,200 from government funds if they qualified"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[6]提到，符合特定收入条件的居民可申领1,200美元补助。(D)项是对录音中Residents who met certain income qualifications could qualify for $1,200的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：400美元是政府补贴金额，不是电动自行车的售价，且四月份是项目上线的时间，并非指在四月份才能低价买车，故排除(A)项；录音未提到人们可以以折扣价购入自己的第一辆电动自行车或享受免费售后服务，故排除(B)项和(C)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q7", "type": "listening", "no": 7, "q": "", "opts": ["Put 400 more electric bikes on its streets", "Increase the program funding by 8 percent", "Resume its electric bike incentive program", "Invest millions of dollars into the local economy"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[7]提到，该市预计将于明年年初恢复该项目。(C)项是对录音中resume the program的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：录音仅提到“已经投放了4400辆电动自行车”，但并未提到要“再投放400辆电动自行车”，故排除(A)项；(B)项在录音中未提及，故排除；录音提到，该计划“已经为当地经济注入860万美元”，但这是往年的成果，而不是明年的计划，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q8", "type": "listening", "no": 8, "q": "", "opts": ["The coin collection", "The primitive tools", "The ancient ornaments", "The 2,000-year-old clothes"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[8]男士提到，但他最喜欢的还是硬币收藏。(A)项是对录音中the coin collection 的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：录音中男士仅提到原始工具是展品之一，并未提到这是他最喜欢的展品，故排除(B)项;(C)项在录音中未提及，故排除;录音中提到，有些古钱币已经有两千多年的历史，而不是男士喜欢有两千年历史的衣物，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q9", "type": "listening", "no": 9, "q": "", "opts": ["It is charming with narrow but beautiful streets", "It attracts visitors from every corner of the world", "It has flowers displayed around the Trevi Fountain", "It is more attractive than the pictures in guidebooks"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[9]女士提到，街道狭窄却美丽，颇具魅力。(A)项中的charming 是对录音中charm 的同义转述，narrow but beautiful 是原词复现，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：录音中仅提到男士和女士都认为老城区游客太多了，但女士并未提到她认为老城区吸引着来自世界各地的游客，故排除(B)项;录音中提到摆放着鲜花的是带有西班牙阶梯的广场，而非特雷维喷泉，(C)项与录音内容不符，故排除;录音中并未对比实景和旅游指南中的图片，(D)项属于无中生有，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q10", "type": "listening", "no": 10, "q": "", "opts": ["It is covered with wild grass", "It is very famous and pretty", "It is located away from an old church", "It is surrounded by Spanish-style houses"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[10]男士提到，它非常著名且十分美丽。(B)项是对录音中It is very famous and pretty 的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：(A)项和(D)项在录音中未提及，故排除;录音中仅提到广场一侧有宽阔的台阶，可通向教堂，而非广场距离教堂很远，故排除(C)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q11", "type": "listening", "no": 11, "q": "", "opts": ["Ask Kathy what her plan is", "Tell Kathy Mike will join them", "Ask Kathy if she'd like to see the square", "Tell Kathy Mike would like to have a change"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。录音中男士提到自己明天打算去参观带有西班牙阶梯的广场，随后女士询问自己与凯茜是否能够同行，并在句[11]表示，要先问问凯茜，但自己相信她一定会同意的。由此可知，女士会先询问凯茜是否想参观这个广场，(C)项是对录音内容的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：女士首先要做的是询问凯茜是否想参观这个广场，并非询问凯茜的计划是什么，(A)项与录音内容不符，故排除;录音中是女士和凯茜想要加入迈克的行程，并非告知凯茜迈克会加入她们，故排除(B)项;(D)项在录音中未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q12", "type": "listening", "no": 12, "q": "", "opts": ["How to fit into the bigger picture", "How to get on with colleagues", "How to create a solid first impression", "How to succeed in a new job"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[12]男士提到，您能给我一些在新工作中取得成功的建议吗? (D)项是对录音中being successful at a new job 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：(A)项\"如何融入整体大局\"和(C)项\"如何给人留下良好的第一印象\"都是录音中女士提到的细节信息，并非男士想要获取建议的核心出发点，故均排除;(B)项在录音中未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q13", "type": "listening", "no": 13, "q": "", "opts": ["Get familiar with all the details of his job description", "Learn about the relations within the organization", "Recognize his own limitations and weaknesses", "Understand his role from various perspectives"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[13]女士提到，你首先要做的最重要的事是从多个角度理解自身的职责。(D)项是对录音中understand your role from multiple perspectives 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：录音中女士提到，你在工作中的任务并不仅限于职位描述中所列出的职责，故排除(A)项;录音中女士提到男士要思考自己的工作如何与组织内的其他成员产生关联，而不是了解组织内部的关系，故排除(B)项;(C)项在录音中未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q14", "type": "listening", "no": 14, "q": "", "opts": ["Learning history", "Meeting his boss", "Creating reports for other departments to use", "Leaving his coworkers in the present company"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[14]男士提到，他对与上司见面也略感不安。(B)项是对录音中meeting 和boss 的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：录音中提到，学习历史是了解公司业务结构的方法之一，并不是男士感到不安的事情，故排除(A)项;录音中男士提到，自己每月末制作的某些报告可能会被其他部门的团队使用，但这并不是男士感到不安的事，故排除(C)项;(D)项在录音中未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q15", "type": "listening", "no": 15, "q": "", "opts": ["Get toknow the decision-makers", "Acquire decision-making authority", "Promote its products and services", "Observe its overall management"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[15]女士提到，深入了解拥有决策权的人至关重要，这能帮你深入了解企业的运营方式及决策制定过程。(A)项是对录音中get to know the people who have decision-making authority 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：录音中女士建议男士要了解那些拥有决策权的人，而不是让男士获得决策权，故排除(B)项;(C)项和(D)项在录音中均未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q16", "type": "listening", "no": 16, "q": "", "opts": ["They do not tell marketers their feeling about a product", "They do not compare competing brands and prices", "They are not hesitant to contradict themselves", "They are not sure what they really like to buy"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[16]提到，许多消费者表示在评估购买时会比较多个竞争品牌及价格。然而，研究表明事实并非如此。消费者的实际想法或感受往往与其陈述内容相矛盾。(B)项是对录音内容的概括转述，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：(A)项和(D)项在录音中未提及，故排除;录音中仅提到消费者的实际想法或感受往往与其言辞相悖，而不是消费者会自相矛盾，故排除(C)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q17", "type": "listening", "no": 17, "q": "", "opts": ["Consumers are often attracted by new products", "Consumers tend to make decisions on the spot", "Consumers find it hard to resist temptation", "Consumers are actually driven by emotion"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[17]提到，情感才是真正驱动购买行为及整体决策的关键因素。优秀的营销人员始终牢记这一重要事实。(D)项是对录音中Emotion is what really drives the purchasing behaviors 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：(A)项、(B)项和(C)项在录音中均未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q18", "type": "listening", "no": 18, "q": "", "opts": ["Draw consumers' attention to the luxury goods symbolizing quality life", "Leave consumers the impression they are doing nothing manipulative", "Highlight the emotional response it will trigger in the consumer", "Remind consumers of the wide connections it can help to build"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[18]提到，作为营销人员，我们应重点关注产品的特点，同时也要推销生活方式与使用感受。关键在于突出消费者使用该产品时会产生的情感反应。(C)项是对录音中highlight the emotional response a consumer will have by using the product 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：(A)项在录音中未提及，故排除;录音末尾提到，帮助消费者达到理想的情绪状态本身并不具有操纵性，而不是要给消费者留下并未操纵的印象，故排除(B)项;录音中说的是通信设备帮助与亲友及更广泛的人群联系，而这并非是所有产品的作用，(D)项表述有误，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q19", "type": "listening", "no": 19, "q": "", "opts": ["They are taking in a lot of plastic", "They are polluting the environment", "They consume much processed food", "They make everything with plastics"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[19]提到，美国人正在摄入以及吸入大量塑料。(A)项是对录音中consuming and breathing in a lot of plastic 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：录音中仅提到塑料渗透到环境中，而不是美国人正在污染环境，(B)项属于张冠李戴，故排除;(C)项在录音中未提及，故排除;录音中提到，塑料被广泛用于制造我们周围几乎所有的物品，而非美国人用塑料制造一切，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q20", "type": "listening", "no": 20, "q": "", "opts": ["Beer, salts and added sugars", "Tap water and take-away food", "Food packages and plastic bags", "Air, bottled water and seafood"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[20]提到，数据显示，对美国人而言，空气、瓶装水和海鲜是吸人或摄入微塑料的最大来源。(D)项是对录音中air, bottled water and seafood 的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：录音中提到，添加糖所占比例较低，且盐类、自来水和啤酒的贡献量极小，(A)项和(B)项与录音内容相悖，故排除;(C)项在录音中均未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q22", "type": "listening", "no": 22, "q": "", "opts": ["They planned to adopt a pet animal", "They kept not only a dog but a cat", "They had at least one pet", "They liked cats more than dogs"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[22]提到，55%的受访者表示他们至少养了一只宠物。(C)项是对录音中they own at least one pet 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：录音中说的是55%的受访者表示他们至少养了一只宠物，并非打算领养一只宠物，(A)项与录音内容不符，故排除;录音中并未提到受访者同时养狗和猫，故排除(B)项;录音中并未比较受访者对猫狗的喜爱程度，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q23", "type": "listening", "no": 23, "q": "", "opts": ["A sense of pride", "A sense of duty", "A sense of purpose", "A sense of security"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[23]提到，近四分之三的受访者表示，他们的宠物提供了一种使命感。(C)项是对录音中a sense of purpose 的原词复现，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：(A)项、(B)项和(D)项在录音中均未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q24", "type": "listening", "no": 24, "q": "", "opts": ["A few problems with keeping big animals", "Some disadvantages related to pet ownership", "A few cases of getting attacked by pet animals", "Some conflicts with neighbors due to pet keeping"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[24]提到，然而，人们确实报告了饲养宠物的一些缺点。(B)项是对录音中a few disadvantages to pet ownership 的同义转述，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：录音中提到，饲养宠物存在一些缺点，但并未特指饲养大型动物，(A)项缩小范围，故排除;录音中提到，6%的养宠者表示，自己因为饲养宠物摔倒或者受伤，而非宠物主动袭击，故排除(C)项;(D)项在录音中未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q25", "type": "listening", "no": 25, "q": "", "opts": ["Reduce as many symptoms of depression as medical help", "Improve people's health as much as physical activities", "Lower older people's stress as effectively as exercising", "Bring many of the same benefits as human social support"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[25]提到，人类社会支持能带来诸多健康益处: 减轻压力、降低长期疾病的发病率等。研究还表明，与宠物互动可带来类似的诸多益处。(D)项是对录音信息的概括转述，故为答案。\n\n干扰项排除：录音中并未将与宠物互动的作用和医疗、体育、锻炼等内容进行对比，故排除(A)项、(B)项和(C)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q46", "type": "reading", "no": 46, "q": "What is the classic grocery store dilemma?", "opts": ["Whether or not one should eat organic food", "Whether or not one can stretch their wallet far", "Organic food costs more but it certainly tastes better", "One wants the best food but their budget is limited"], "answer": 3, "why": "根据题干中的关键词classic grocery store dilemma可定位到第1段第2句。\n答案为(D)。第1段第2句提到，这是杂货店里经典的两难困境。第3、4句对这个两难困境进行解释：一方面，家里的餐桌理应只摆上最好的食材。但另一方面，你的钱包却未必撑得住。(D)项是对第3、4句的同义转述，其中the best food对应原文中的only the best；budget is limited对应原文中的wallet can only stretch so far。\n干扰项排除：(A)项未体现原文\"想要好食材但预算有限\"的核心矛盾，故排除；(B)项仅片面强调经济压力，未涵盖\"追求优质食材\"这一方面，故排除；(C)项\"口感确实更好\"在文中未提及，属于无中生有，故排除。", "passage": "Is organic food worth the higher price? It's the classic grocery store dilemma. On the one hand, your family dinner table deserves only the best. On the other hand, your wallet can only stretch so far. Whether or not to buy organic is a personal decision, said Mike von Massow, professor of Food Agriculture. Science won't give a clear-cut answer to the question of whether organic food is “better.\" There are often trade-offs (权衡) and answers become less definitive depending on the product under consideration. One thing is definitive: Organic and conventionally grown food are equally nutritious. From a nutrition point of view, there is no difference between organic and traditionally grown products.\n\nOrganic products don't necessarily have fewer pesticides (杀虫剂) in fact, they may have more. There is also no big difference between organic and non-organic products from a food-safety perspective. Even organic growers use pesticides. Testing shows little if any pesticide remains on conventionally grown products. In fact, organic farmers often use larger quantities of pesticide because they can only use the natural variety of insect-killers. Chemical pesticides generally kill a much wider range of pests (害虫) than any single natural pesticide.\n\nThe use of animal waste in organic agriculture can also be a factor in the spread of bacteria like E. coli and salmonella. Over the past 10 years, Canada has seen more food-safety recalls for organic products, proportionally, than for conventional products.\n\nOn the whole, organic agriculture may have a smaller environmental footprint. That's mostly because the risk of groundwater contamination from organic farming is lower. Organic farmers never used neonics, the controversial pesticides that have been linked to shrinking bee populations worldwide. Traditional agriculture has been making “dramatic improvements\" to reduce its impact on the environment. It isn't a black-and-white picture, though.\n\nOrganic farming is much less efficient when it comes to land use, although yields vary considerably depending on the crop.\n\nOn the other hand, advocates of organic farming argue yields and efficiency could be significantly improved if the food industry devoted as much research and money to organic farming as it does conventional agriculture"}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q47", "type": "reading", "no": 47, "q": "What do we learn about organic food from science?", "opts": ["Whether it is any better remains uncertain", "Whether it is healthier is under consideration", "Whether it is more nutritious than conventional food is arguable", "Whether it is going to replace conventional food is still unclear"], "answer": 0, "why": "根据题干中的关键词organic food和science可定位到第3段第1句。\n答案为(A)。第3段第1句提到，科学无法对\"有机食品是否'更好'\"这个问题给出明确答案。(A)项是对该句的同义转述，其中Whether it is any better对应原文中的whether organic food is \"better\"；remains uncertain对应原文中的won't give a clear-cut answer。\n干扰项排除：原文说有机食品\"是否更健康\"这一问题没有确定结论，而不是\"还在考虑\"，(B)项与原文不符，故排除；(C)项与原文提到的\"有机种植与传统种植的食品营养成分相当\"矛盾，故排除；(D)项中的\"取代传统食品\"在原文中未提及，故排除。", "passage": "Is organic food worth the higher price? It's the classic grocery store dilemma. On the one hand, your family dinner table deserves only the best. On the other hand, your wallet can only stretch so far. Whether or not to buy organic is a personal decision, said Mike von Massow, professor of Food Agriculture. Science won't give a clear-cut answer to the question of whether organic food is “better.\" There are often trade-offs (权衡) and answers become less definitive depending on the product under consideration. One thing is definitive: Organic and conventionally grown food are equally nutritious. From a nutrition point of view, there is no difference between organic and traditionally grown products.\n\nOrganic products don't necessarily have fewer pesticides (杀虫剂) in fact, they may have more. There is also no big difference between organic and non-organic products from a food-safety perspective. Even organic growers use pesticides. Testing shows little if any pesticide remains on conventionally grown products. In fact, organic farmers often use larger quantities of pesticide because they can only use the natural variety of insect-killers. Chemical pesticides generally kill a much wider range of pests (害虫) than any single natural pesticide.\n\nThe use of animal waste in organic agriculture can also be a factor in the spread of bacteria like E. coli and salmonella. Over the past 10 years, Canada has seen more food-safety recalls for organic products, proportionally, than for conventional products.\n\nOn the whole, organic agriculture may have a smaller environmental footprint. That's mostly because the risk of groundwater contamination from organic farming is lower. Organic farmers never used neonics, the controversial pesticides that have been linked to shrinking bee populations worldwide. Traditional agriculture has been making “dramatic improvements\" to reduce its impact on the environment. It isn't a black-and-white picture, though.\n\nOrganic farming is much less efficient when it comes to land use, although yields vary considerably depending on the crop.\n\nOn the other hand, advocates of organic farming argue yields and efficiency could be significantly improved if the food industry devoted as much research and money to organic farming as it does conventional agriculture"}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q48", "type": "reading", "no": 48, "q": "What does the passage say about organic and non-organic products as far as food safety is concerned?", "opts": ["Their difference is the conventions used to test them", "They differ in how much pesticide remains on them", "One relies on pesticides whereas the other does not", "There is no substantial difference between the two"], "answer": 3, "why": "根据题干中的关键词organic and nonorganic produce和food safety可定位到第5段第2句。\n答案为(D)。第5段第2句提到，从食品安全角度来看，有机与非有机食品也没有明显差别。(D)项是对该句的同义转述，其中no substantial difference对应原文中的no big difference。\n干扰项排除：(A)项中的\"检测惯例\"在文中未提及，故排除；原文只提到传统食品上残留的杀虫剂很少，并未提有机食品的残留量，故排除(B)项；(C)项与原文中提到的两类食品都使用杀虫剂相矛盾，故排除。", "passage": "Is organic food worth the higher price? It's the classic grocery store dilemma. On the one hand, your family dinner table deserves only the best. On the other hand, your wallet can only stretch so far. Whether or not to buy organic is a personal decision, said Mike von Massow, professor of Food Agriculture. Science won't give a clear-cut answer to the question of whether organic food is “better.\" There are often trade-offs (权衡) and answers become less definitive depending on the product under consideration. One thing is definitive: Organic and conventionally grown food are equally nutritious. From a nutrition point of view, there is no difference between organic and traditionally grown products.\n\nOrganic products don't necessarily have fewer pesticides (杀虫剂) in fact, they may have more. There is also no big difference between organic and non-organic products from a food-safety perspective. Even organic growers use pesticides. Testing shows little if any pesticide remains on conventionally grown products. In fact, organic farmers often use larger quantities of pesticide because they can only use the natural variety of insect-killers. Chemical pesticides generally kill a much wider range of pests (害虫) than any single natural pesticide.\n\nThe use of animal waste in organic agriculture can also be a factor in the spread of bacteria like E. coli and salmonella. Over the past 10 years, Canada has seen more food-safety recalls for organic products, proportionally, than for conventional products.\n\nOn the whole, organic agriculture may have a smaller environmental footprint. That's mostly because the risk of groundwater contamination from organic farming is lower. Organic farmers never used neonics, the controversial pesticides that have been linked to shrinking bee populations worldwide. Traditional agriculture has been making “dramatic improvements\" to reduce its impact on the environment. It isn't a black-and-white picture, though.\n\nOrganic farming is much less efficient when it comes to land use, although yields vary considerably depending on the crop.\n\nOn the other hand, advocates of organic farming argue yields and efficiency could be significantly improved if the food industry devoted as much research and money to organic farming as it does conventional agriculture"}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q49", "type": "reading", "no": 49, "q": "What does the author say about organic farming?", "opts": ["It may be blamed for the shrinking bee populations in the world", "It minimizes the use of controversial pesticides in its operations", "It may impact the environment less than conventional farming", "It contaminates groundwater as much as conventional farming"], "answer": 2, "why": "根据题干中的关键词organic farming可定位到第8段第1句。\n答案为(C)。第8段第1句提到，总体而言，有机农业的环境足迹可能更小。(C)项是对该句的同义转述，其中impact the environment less对应原文中的have a smaller environmental footprint。\n干扰项排除：(A)项与原文\"有机农民不使用争议性杀虫剂，该杀虫剂被认为与全球蜜蜂数量减少有关\"矛盾，故排除；(B)项表述不准确，原文是\"从不使用\"争议性杀虫剂，而非减少使用，故排除；(D)项与原文\"有机农业造成地下水污染的风险更低\"矛盾，故排除。", "passage": "Is organic food worth the higher price? It's the classic grocery store dilemma. On the one hand, your family dinner table deserves only the best. On the other hand, your wallet can only stretch so far. Whether or not to buy organic is a personal decision, said Mike von Massow, professor of Food Agriculture. Science won't give a clear-cut answer to the question of whether organic food is “better.\" There are often trade-offs (权衡) and answers become less definitive depending on the product under consideration. One thing is definitive: Organic and conventionally grown food are equally nutritious. From a nutrition point of view, there is no difference between organic and traditionally grown products.\n\nOrganic products don't necessarily have fewer pesticides (杀虫剂) in fact, they may have more. There is also no big difference between organic and non-organic products from a food-safety perspective. Even organic growers use pesticides. Testing shows little if any pesticide remains on conventionally grown products. In fact, organic farmers often use larger quantities of pesticide because they can only use the natural variety of insect-killers. Chemical pesticides generally kill a much wider range of pests (害虫) than any single natural pesticide.\n\nThe use of animal waste in organic agriculture can also be a factor in the spread of bacteria like E. coli and salmonella. Over the past 10 years, Canada has seen more food-safety recalls for organic products, proportionally, than for conventional products.\n\nOn the whole, organic agriculture may have a smaller environmental footprint. That's mostly because the risk of groundwater contamination from organic farming is lower. Organic farmers never used neonics, the controversial pesticides that have been linked to shrinking bee populations worldwide. Traditional agriculture has been making “dramatic improvements\" to reduce its impact on the environment. It isn't a black-and-white picture, though.\n\nOrganic farming is much less efficient when it comes to land use, although yields vary considerably depending on the crop.\n\nOn the other hand, advocates of organic farming argue yields and efficiency could be significantly improved if the food industry devoted as much research and money to organic farming as it does conventional agriculture"}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q50", "type": "reading", "no": 50, "q": "What do advocates of organic farming argue about its yields and efficiency?", "opts": ["They would increase quickly with the emergence of new technologies", "They would improve remarkably with more research and investment", "They would be achieved along with enormous health benefits", "They would considerably lower the cost to the environment"], "answer": 1, "why": "根据题干中的关键词advocates和yields and efficiency可定位到最后一段。\n答案为(B)。最后一段提到，另一方面，有机农业的支持者认为，如果食品行业能像对待传统农业一样，为有机农业投入同等的研究与资金，其产量和效率会得到大幅提升。(B)项是对该句的同义转述，其中improve remarkably对应原文中的significantly improved；more research and investment对应原文中的devoted as much research and money。\n干扰项排除：原文仅提到\"投入研究和资金\"，未涉及新技术，故排除(A)项；原文并未提及健康效益，故排除(C)项；(D)项并非支持者对产量和效率的观点，属于答非所问，故排除。", "passage": "Is organic food worth the higher price? It's the classic grocery store dilemma. On the one hand, your family dinner table deserves only the best. On the other hand, your wallet can only stretch so far. Whether or not to buy organic is a personal decision, said Mike von Massow, professor of Food Agriculture. Science won't give a clear-cut answer to the question of whether organic food is “better.\" There are often trade-offs (权衡) and answers become less definitive depending on the product under consideration. One thing is definitive: Organic and conventionally grown food are equally nutritious. From a nutrition point of view, there is no difference between organic and traditionally grown products.\n\nOrganic products don't necessarily have fewer pesticides (杀虫剂) in fact, they may have more. There is also no big difference between organic and non-organic products from a food-safety perspective. Even organic growers use pesticides. Testing shows little if any pesticide remains on conventionally grown products. In fact, organic farmers often use larger quantities of pesticide because they can only use the natural variety of insect-killers. Chemical pesticides generally kill a much wider range of pests (害虫) than any single natural pesticide.\n\nThe use of animal waste in organic agriculture can also be a factor in the spread of bacteria like E. coli and salmonella. Over the past 10 years, Canada has seen more food-safety recalls for organic products, proportionally, than for conventional products.\n\nOn the whole, organic agriculture may have a smaller environmental footprint. That's mostly because the risk of groundwater contamination from organic farming is lower. Organic farmers never used neonics, the controversial pesticides that have been linked to shrinking bee populations worldwide. Traditional agriculture has been making “dramatic improvements\" to reduce its impact on the environment. It isn't a black-and-white picture, though.\n\nOrganic farming is much less efficient when it comes to land use, although yields vary considerably depending on the crop.\n\nOn the other hand, advocates of organic farming argue yields and efficiency could be significantly improved if the food industry devoted as much research and money to organic farming as it does conventional agriculture"}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q51", "type": "reading", "no": 51, "q": "What is the finding of one recent study about being selfish?", "opts": ["It may bring certain benefits in the short term", "It usually yields more disadvantages than advantages", "It serves individual interest rather than a collective's", "It may give way to cooperating in the long run"], "answer": 0, "why": "根据题干中的关键词one recent study和being selfish可定位到第3段第2、3句。\n答案为(A)。第3段第2、3句提到，近期一项研究测试了人们放下私利以实现更大集体利益的意愿。该研究发现，自私相较合作更有利。第4句进一步提到，这种益处可能十分短暂。(A)项是对第4句的同义转述。其中benefits为原词复现；in the short term对应原文中的be short-lived。\n干扰项排除：原文只说短期内自私更有利，而非\"通常弊大于利\"，故排除(B)项；原文未提及自私行为服务于个人利益，而非集体利益，故排除(C)项；(D)项是根据原文中的players who cooperated did better in the long run设置的干扰，但原文提到的是\"选择合作的参与者长远来看表现更好\"，并没有说自私行为会让位于(give way to)合作行为，(D)项属于答非所问，故排除。", "passage": "It is still controversial whether we are fundamentally generous or selfish and whether these tendencies are shaped by our genes or environment.\n\nSome evidence points to humans being born cooperative. At later stages in life we routinely work together to reach goals and help out in times of need.\n\nYet instances of selfish behavior are also plenty. One recent study tested people's willingness to set aside selfish interests to reach a greater good. It found that being selfish was more advantageous than cooperating. The benefit may be short-lived, however. Another study showed that players who cooperated did better in the long run.\n\nIt seems that human nature supports both pro-social and selfish traits (特征). Genetic studies have made some progress toward identifying their biological roots. By comparing identical twins, who share nearly 100 percent of their genes, and non-identical twins, who share about half, researchers have found overwhelming evidence for genetic effects on behaviors. In these twin studies, identical and non-identical twins are asked, for example, to split a sum of money with a peer. Such studies often also rely on careful psychological assessments and DNA analysis.\n\nOther work highlights specific genes as key players. My colleagues and I recently identified a gene linked to altruistic (利他 的) behavior and found that a particular variant of it was associated with more selfish behavior in preschoolers.\n\nAccording to evolutionary scientists, cooperative behavior may have evolved first among relatives to promote the continuation of their genetic line. As communities diversified, mutual support could have broadened to include individuals not linked by blood. Another possibility is that humans cooperate to gain some advantage, such as boosting reputation. Finally, evolutionary processes take place at the group level. Groups of highly cooperative individuals have higher chances of survival because they can work together to reach goals that are unattainable to less cooperative groups.\n\nYet almost no behavior is entirely genetic, even among identical twins. Culture, schooling and parenting are important determinants of cooperation. Thus, the degree to which we act cooperatively or selfishly is unique to each individual and depends on a variety of genetic and environmental influences."}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q52", "type": "reading", "no": 52, "q": "What have researchers found by studying identical and non-identical twins?", "opts": ["They are overwhelmingly different in the amounts of genes", "One's behavior is largely determined by their genetic traits", "One's biological roots can be identified by their genes", "They differ from each other in their behavior patterns"], "answer": 1, "why": "根据题干中的关键词identical twins和fraternal twins可定位到第4段。\n答案为(B)。第4段第3句提到，在双胞胎研究中，研究人员发现了压倒性的证据，证明基因会对行为产生影响。(B)项是对该句的同义转述，其中is largely determined by their genetic traits对应原文中的genetic effects。\n干扰项排除：(A)项是根据原文中share nearly 100 percent... about half设置的干扰，但原文提到的是双胞胎基因重合比例不同，并非基因数量差异巨大，故排除；(C)项是根据原文中的progress toward identifying their biological roots设置的干扰，这里只提到有进展，并未提及通过基因识别生物学根源，故排除；文中未提及双胞胎行为模式各不相同，故排除(D)项。", "passage": "It is still controversial whether we are fundamentally generous or selfish and whether these tendencies are shaped by our genes or environment.\n\nSome evidence points to humans being born cooperative. At later stages in life we routinely work together to reach goals and help out in times of need.\n\nYet instances of selfish behavior are also plenty. One recent study tested people's willingness to set aside selfish interests to reach a greater good. It found that being selfish was more advantageous than cooperating. The benefit may be short-lived, however. Another study showed that players who cooperated did better in the long run.\n\nIt seems that human nature supports both pro-social and selfish traits (特征). Genetic studies have made some progress toward identifying their biological roots. By comparing identical twins, who share nearly 100 percent of their genes, and non-identical twins, who share about half, researchers have found overwhelming evidence for genetic effects on behaviors. In these twin studies, identical and non-identical twins are asked, for example, to split a sum of money with a peer. Such studies often also rely on careful psychological assessments and DNA analysis.\n\nOther work highlights specific genes as key players. My colleagues and I recently identified a gene linked to altruistic (利他 的) behavior and found that a particular variant of it was associated with more selfish behavior in preschoolers.\n\nAccording to evolutionary scientists, cooperative behavior may have evolved first among relatives to promote the continuation of their genetic line. As communities diversified, mutual support could have broadened to include individuals not linked by blood. Another possibility is that humans cooperate to gain some advantage, such as boosting reputation. Finally, evolutionary processes take place at the group level. Groups of highly cooperative individuals have higher chances of survival because they can work together to reach goals that are unattainable to less cooperative groups.\n\nYet almost no behavior is entirely genetic, even among identical twins. Culture, schooling and parenting are important determinants of cooperation. Thus, the degree to which we act cooperatively or selfishly is unique to each individual and depends on a variety of genetic and environmental influences."}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q53", "type": "reading", "no": 53, "q": "What did the author and colleagues identify in their recent research?", "opts": ["A gene-related selfish behavior in preschool children", "Key factors that determine one's altruistic behavior", "A specific gene associated with altruistic behavior", "Roles played by genes in shaping one's character"], "answer": 2, "why": "根据题干中的关键词the author and colleagues identify可定位到第5段第2句。\n答案为(C)。第5段第2句提到，\"我\"和同事们近期发现了一种与利他行为相关的基因。(C)项是对该句的同义转述，其中gene和altruistic behavior为原词复现；associated with对应原文中的linked to。\n干扰项排除：(A)项是根据原文中selfish behavior in preschoolers设置的干扰，但原文提到的是该基因变体与学龄前儿童身上的自私行为存在关联，(A)项将现象当作研究发现，故排除；(B)项是根据原文中a gene linked to altruistic behavior设置的干扰，但原文只是提到与利他行为相关的基因，并未提及它是决定利他行为的关键因素，故排除；原文讲的是基因与行为存在关联，而不是基因在塑造人的性格中所起到的作用，故排除(D)项。", "passage": "It is still controversial whether we are fundamentally generous or selfish and whether these tendencies are shaped by our genes or environment.\n\nSome evidence points to humans being born cooperative. At later stages in life we routinely work together to reach goals and help out in times of need.\n\nYet instances of selfish behavior are also plenty. One recent study tested people's willingness to set aside selfish interests to reach a greater good. It found that being selfish was more advantageous than cooperating. The benefit may be short-lived, however. Another study showed that players who cooperated did better in the long run.\n\nIt seems that human nature supports both pro-social and selfish traits (特征). Genetic studies have made some progress toward identifying their biological roots. By comparing identical twins, who share nearly 100 percent of their genes, and non-identical twins, who share about half, researchers have found overwhelming evidence for genetic effects on behaviors. In these twin studies, identical and non-identical twins are asked, for example, to split a sum of money with a peer. Such studies often also rely on careful psychological assessments and DNA analysis.\n\nOther work highlights specific genes as key players. My colleagues and I recently identified a gene linked to altruistic (利他 的) behavior and found that a particular variant of it was associated with more selfish behavior in preschoolers.\n\nAccording to evolutionary scientists, cooperative behavior may have evolved first among relatives to promote the continuation of their genetic line. As communities diversified, mutual support could have broadened to include individuals not linked by blood. Another possibility is that humans cooperate to gain some advantage, such as boosting reputation. Finally, evolutionary processes take place at the group level. Groups of highly cooperative individuals have higher chances of survival because they can work together to reach goals that are unattainable to less cooperative groups.\n\nYet almost no behavior is entirely genetic, even among identical twins. Culture, schooling and parenting are important determinants of cooperation. Thus, the degree to which we act cooperatively or selfishly is unique to each individual and depends on a variety of genetic and environmental influences."}, {"id": "CET4-1-Q55", "type": "reading", "no": 55, "q": "What can we infer from the last paragraph?", "opts": ["Cooperative behavior is evident among identical twins", "One is born either cooperative or non-cooperative", "Genes determine one's tendency to cooperate", "Cooperative behavior may be cultivated"], "answer": 3, "why": "根据题干中的关键词the last paragraph可定位到最后一段。\n答案为(D)。最后一段指出，几乎没有行为是完全由基因决定的。文化、学校教育以及家庭教育，是合作行为的重要决定因素。由此可知，合作行为能够通过后天培养形成。(D)项是对该段内容的合理推断。\n干扰项排除：(A)项是根据原文中even among identical twins设置的干扰，但原文提到的是同卵双胞胎的行为也并非完全由基因决定，而不是其合作行为表现明显，故排除；(B)项与原文矛盾，文章最后一段明确指出合作行为取决于各类基因因素和环境因素的作用而非天生就固定为合作或不合作，故排除；原文明确说明合作倾向是基因与环境共同作用的结果，并非单纯由基因决定，(C)项表述片面，故排除。", "passage": "It is still controversial whether we are fundamentally generous or selfish and whether these tendencies are shaped by our genes or environment.\n\nSome evidence points to humans being born cooperative. At later stages in life we routinely work together to reach goals and help out in times of need.\n\nYet instances of selfish behavior are also plenty. One recent study tested people's willingness to set aside selfish interests to reach a greater good. It found that being selfish was more advantageous than cooperating. The benefit may be short-lived, however. Another study showed that players who cooperated did better in the long run.\n\nIt seems that human nature supports both pro-social and selfish traits (特征). Genetic studies have made some progress toward identifying their biological roots. By comparing identical twins, who share nearly 100 percent of their genes, and non-identical twins, who share about half, researchers have found overwhelming evidence for genetic effects on behaviors. In these twin studies, identical and non-identical twins are asked, for example, to split a sum of money with a peer. Such studies often also rely on careful psychological assessments and DNA analysis.\n\nOther work highlights specific genes as key players. My colleagues and I recently identified a gene linked to altruistic (利他 的) behavior and found that a particular variant of it was associated with more selfish behavior in preschoolers.\n\nAccording to evolutionary scientists, cooperative behavior may have evolved first among relatives to promote the continuation of their genetic line. As communities diversified, mutual support could have broadened to include individuals not linked by blood. Another possibility is that humans cooperate to gain some advantage, such as boosting reputation. Finally, evolutionary processes take place at the group level. Groups of highly cooperative individuals have higher chances of survival because they can work together to reach goals that are unattainable to less cooperative groups.\n\nYet almost no behavior is entirely genetic, even among identical twins. Culture, schooling and parenting are important determinants of cooperation. Thus, the degree to which we act cooperatively or selfishly is unique to each individual and depends on a variety of genetic and environmental influences."}]}, {"id": "CET4-2", "level": "CET4", "set": 2, "year": 2026, "month": 6, "title": "四级 2026年6月 第2套", "audio": "audio/mock/cet4_2026_2.mp3", "minutes": 40, "questions": [{"id": "CET4-2-Q1", "type": "listening", "no": 1, "q": "", "opts": ["She was born months after the end of World War I", "She has been living in her house throughout her life", "She suffered a great deal during the Second World War", "She is a 102-year-old woman living in Somerset Village"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[1]提到，南希·吉福德出生于第一次世界大战结束数月之后。(A)项对应录音内容，故为答案。其中was born months after the end of 为录音原词复现，World War I 对应录音中的the First World War。\n|干扰项排除|录音开头提到南希·吉福德正在出售她从两岁起就一直居住的房子，而非一生都住在这栋房子里，故排除(B)项；录音只提到南希·吉福德在这栋房子里经历了第二次世界大战(having lived in it through the Second World War)，但并未提及她在第二次世界大战期间遭受了很多苦难，(C)项属于无中生有，故排除；(D)项中的102年是南希·吉福德在房子里居住的时长，且录音开头点明她是104岁(a 104-year-old woman)，(D)项与录音内容不符，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q2", "type": "listening", "no": 2, "q": "", "opts": ["She has been persuaded by the estate agent", "She needs the money badly for her health", "She is leaving Glastonbury Village", "She is moving to a nursing home"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[2]提到，吉福德夫人打算卖掉房子，搬到附近格拉斯顿伯里的一家养老院，原因是她的身体状况不佳。(D)项是对录音中moving to a nursing home 的原词复现，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|录音未提及房产经纪人劝她卖房，故排除(A)项；录音只提到健康问题是她搬家的原因，但未提及她因为健康问题而急需用钱，(B)项属于过度解读，故排除；格拉斯顿伯里是养老院所在地，她是搬去这里，而非离开，(C)项与录音内容相悖，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q3", "type": "listening", "no": 3, "q": "", "opts": ["The navy's timely arrival", "His rich sailing experience", "The word HELP written on the boat", "His use of a mirror to send a signal"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[3]提到，这名47岁男子在船上写下了英文单词\"HELP\"，这成为他获救的关键。(C)项是对录音中wrote the word HELP in English on the boat 的同义转述，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|海军是最终实施救援的一方，但录音并未说海军及时抵达是男子获救的关键，故排除(A)项；录音未提及男子有丰富的航海经验，故排除(B)项；利用镜子发出信号是男子中途尝试的求救手段，但这只是辅助行为，不是他获救的关键，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q4", "type": "listening", "no": 4, "q": "", "opts": ["He lost all consciousness", "He was in good condition", "He was too distressed to pick himself up", "He entirely abandoned himself to despair"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[4]提到，他被发现时健康状况良好，但体重减轻了。(B)项是对录音中was found in good health 的同义转述，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|录音未提及他失去意识以及悲痛消沉，(A)项和(C)项属于无中生有，故均排除；录音提到男子尝试多种求救方法：在船上写下英文单词HELP (wrote the word \"HELP\" in English on the boat)、用布收集雨水(collected rainwater with a cloth)、生火发出求救信号(light a fire to send a distress signal)以及用镜子发出信号(signaled with a mirror)，这一系列自救行为表明他并非陷入绝望，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q5", "type": "listening", "no": 5, "q": "", "opts": ["Their efforts to provide social services have been ignored", "They have been criticized by the Denver school board", "They have taken various steps to get more funding", "Their enrollment has been on a noticeable decline"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[5]提到，丹佛公立学校董事会计划关闭几所小学和中学，以弥补因入学人数下降而导致的预算减少。由此可知，丹佛将要关闭的小学和中学的入学人数已出现下降趋势。(D)项是对录音中declining enrollment 的同义转述，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|录音提到的是预算减少意味着无法再提供同样的心理健康支持、社交和情感服务(the decreased budget means it cannot provide the same mental health supports, social and emotional services)，并非学校为提供社会服务所做的努力被忽视了，故排除(A)项；(B)项和(C)项在录音中均未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q6", "type": "listening", "no": 6, "q": "", "opts": ["Approve criteria for closing schools with decreased funding", "Enroll 3,000 fewer elementary and middle school students", "Decide which elementary and middle schools to close", "Alter its per-pupil funding policy on a rational basis"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[6]提到，该校区表示，在未来三年内，预计小学和中学的入学人数将减少3,000人。(B)项是对录音中enroll 3,000 fewer elementary and middle school students 的原词复现，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|录音中提到学校董事会通过了关闭学校的标准(The school board approved criteria to close schools)，这是已经发生了的事情，而非未来三年内会发生，(A)项时间范围错误，故排除；录音中明确提到具体关停学校名单会在未来几天公布(The specific schools will be announced in the coming days)，而不是未来三年才决定，故排除(C)项；录音中提到学区是按人头拨款，但录音并未提及要修改这一政策，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q7", "type": "listening", "no": 7, "q": "", "opts": ["To enable the district to remain competitive", "To avoid a negative impact on its economy", "To keep the district free of debt", "To obtain the expected funding"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。录音开头提到了丹佛校区计划关闭一些小学和中学，其后丹佛校区发言人在句[7]明确提到，为了让校区不陷入债务，这是必须采取的一步。必须采取的一步(a step that needs to be taken)即关闭一些学校。由此可知，(C)项是对录音中in order for the district to stay out of debt 的同义转述，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|\"让校区保持竞争力\"和\"当地经济\"在录音中均未提及，故排除(A)项和(B)项；录音表明生源减少会导致拨款变少，\"获取预期的经费\"与录音逻辑相反，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q8", "type": "listening", "no": 8, "q": "", "opts": ["To book tickets for a musical", "To enquire about the price of cats", "To pay for her afternoon flight to London", "To have her personal computer repaired"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。女士在句[8]中提到，她一直在试着预订伦敦音乐剧《猫》在5月28日晚上的门票，但电脑就是不接受她的银行卡付款。她已经试了三次了，但就是不能扣款。由此可知，女士订票失败，给男士打电话是为了预定一场音乐剧的门票。(A)项是对录音中to book tickets for the musical Cats 的同义转述，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|Cats是音乐剧的名称，不是动物猫咪，(B)项曲解了单词在录音中的含义，故排除；录音未提及女士飞往伦敦的航班，故排除(C)项；女士的电脑只是支付失败，录音中未提及找人修理她的个人电脑，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q9", "type": "listening", "no": 9, "q": "", "opts": ["He frequents the Lion Theatre", "He knows how to sell things well", "He is a computer science expert", "He is away on a business trip"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。女士在句[9]提到，这些事情通常都是她丈夫做的，但他现在在意大利出差，下周四才回来。(D)项是对录音中he is in Italy on a business trip 的同义转述，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|(A)项和(C)项在录音中均未提及，故排除；(B)项是根据录音中女士提到的And I know how quickly these things can sell out(而且我知道这些东西卖得有多快)设置的干扰，并非她的丈夫了解如何更好地卖东西，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q10", "type": "listening", "no": 10, "q": "", "opts": ["They are sold at a discount", "They are in low demand", "They are no longer available", "They are not too expensive"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[10]明确提到，前排的所有票都已经卖完了。(C)项是对录音内容的同义转述，故为答案。其中no longer available 对应录音中的sold out。\n|干扰项排除|录音未提及前排的票是否打折以及价格高低，故排除(A)项和(D)项；前排的票全部卖完，说明需求很高，(B)项表述与录音相反，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q11", "type": "listening", "no": 11, "q": "", "opts": ["Heights", "Circus", "Wild animals", "Dark nights"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。女士在句[11]中明确提到她害怕高处。(A)项是录音中heights 的原词复现，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|录音未提及马戏团、野生动物以及漆黑的夜晚，故排除(B)项、(C)项和(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q12", "type": "listening", "no": 12, "q": "", "opts": ["He finds it bothersome to reply", "He has switched off his phone", "He hasn't got the time to text back", "He doesn't know how to respond"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[12-1]中，女士问男士最近为什么一直不回她的短信。男士在句[12-2]中回答道：\"我差不多一周前就把手机关机了，从那以后就一直没再开机。\"由此可知，(B)项是对录音内容的同义转述，其中switched off 对应录音中的turned off，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|(A)项和(C)项在录音中均未提及，故排除；男士是没看到短信，而不是不知道如何回复，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q13", "type": "listening", "no": 13, "q": "", "opts": ["His lack of sufficient sleep at night", "His addiction to mobile phone games", "His excessive use of the mobile phone", "His high frequency of surfing the net"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。男士在句[13]中提到，他下载了一个应用，能记录他在手机上花了多少时间，然后他震惊地意识到，他平均每天使用手机长达八个小时。最糟糕的一天是他使用了十二个小时。由此可知，男士震惊地意识到他过度使用手机。(C)项是对录音内容的正确推断，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|录音未提及男士的夜间睡眠情况，故排除(A)项；录音中，男士明确否定自己沉迷于手机游戏，(B)项与录音内容相悖，故排除；让男士震惊的是他使用手机的时长，而非上网频率，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q14", "type": "listening", "no": 14, "q": "", "opts": ["Shopping online and paying for his purchases", "Communicating with friends on social media", "Exploring whatever content to his liking", "Sharing and posting pictures and videos"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[14]提到，男士花了太多时间在社交媒体上，给朋友发消息，以及评论别人的帖子。(B)项是对录音内容的同义转述，故为答案。其中Communicating with friends 对应录音中的messaging friends；on social media 为原词复现。\n|干扰项排除|网上购物和线上付款是女士的行为，与男士无关，(A)项混淆主体，故排除；(C)项和(D)项在录音中均未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q15", "type": "listening", "no": 15, "q": "", "opts": ["She prefers chatting with others online to meeting them", "There are few physical stores near where she lives", "Ordering meals online is a great convenience to her", "She needs to talk with her friends and family far away"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。女士在句[15]中说道：\"那对你来说挺好的。但我的朋友和家人都不住在这个城市，所以我们没法经常见面。我别无选择，只能和他们在线聊天。\"由此可知，女士需要使用手机与朋友和家人聊天。(D)项是对录音内容的概述，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|女士并未提到自己更喜欢线下见面还是在线上聊天，故排除(A)项；\"实体店\"和\"线上点餐\"在录音中均未提及，故排除(B)项和(C)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q16", "type": "listening", "no": 16, "q": "", "opts": ["It is now narrowing", "It keeps increasing", "It has remained huge", "It will soon disappear"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[16]提到，跳槽者与留职者之间的薪资增长差距正在收窄。(A)项是对录音中The gap...is now closing 的同义转述，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|录音明确提到薪资增长差距正在收窄，并非持续增大或一直很大，(B)项和(C)项与录音内容相悖，故均排除；录音中未提到该差距会很快消失，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q17", "type": "listening", "no": 17, "q": "", "opts": ["It is fluctuating between 5-7%", "It is slowing down for everyone", "It is faster for those who stay in their jobs", "It is inadequate for dealing with inflation. 11"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[17]提到，7月，这一差距扩大至2.6个百分点，但现如今所有人的薪资增长都在放缓，跳槽者在新岗位上能拿到的加薪幅度也缩水了。(B)项是对录音中wage growth is slowing down for everyone 的原词复现，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|录音中提到的5.4%、7.3%分别是留职者和跳槽者的年均加薪比例，并非薪资增长在5%-7%之间波动，故排除(A)项；录音提到跳槽者年均加薪7.3%，而留职者仅5.4%。因此，留职者薪资增速更慢，(C)项与录音内容相悖，故排除；(D)项在录音中未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q18", "type": "listening", "no": 18, "q": "", "opts": ["Booming", "Stabilizing", "Struggling", "Cooling"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[18]提到，劳动力市场可能正在降温。(D)项是对录音中cooling 的原词复现，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|(A)项、(B)项和(C)项在录音中均未提及，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q19", "type": "listening", "no": 19, "q": "", "opts": ["They have smaller amounts of nutrients than un processed diets", "Their contribution to calorie consumption is hard to determine", "They account for nearly half of the average American diet", "Their low price and convenience negatively impact health"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[19]提到，但一项新研究表示，这些食品(即深加工食品)低廉的价格和便捷性却是以健康为代价。(D)项是对录音中their low price and convenience come at a cost to health 的同义转述，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|录音未对比深加工食品与未加工食品的营养含量，故排除(A)项；录音提到，人们食用深加工食品时，每日摄入的热量会多出约500卡路里，因此，热量摄入可以量化判定，(B)项中hard to determine(难以判定)与录音内容不符，故排除；录音提到，深加工食品占普通美国人日常饮食的60% (sixty percent)，而非\"占普通美国人日常饮食的一半\"，故排除(C)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q20", "type": "listening", "no": 20, "q": "", "opts": ["A strong desire to overeat", "A lack of nutrients like fiber", "A higher risk of cancer and obesity", "A greater intake of sugar and salt"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[20]提到，其他研究已将其与更高的癌症和肥胖风险联系起来。(C)项是录音中a higher risk of cancer and obesity 的原词复现，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|(A)项、(B)项和(D)项所表述的内容均不属于题干中其他研究的结论，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q21", "type": "listening", "no": 21, "q": "", "opts": ["Eat more quickly", "Feel full sooner", "Chew thoroughly", "Swallow bit by bit"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[21]提到，研究还发现，食用深加工食品时，人们的进食速度更快。(A)项是对录音中people ate faster 的同义转述，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|录音提到，如果你吃得太快，肠道就没有足够的时间向大脑传递已经摄入足够热量、感到饱腹的信号。由此可知，人们在食用深加工食品时，大脑接收饱腹信号滞后，不会更早地感到饱，(B)项与录音信息相悖，故排除；录音提到，深加工食品通常质地较软，更容易咀嚼和吞咽，因此人们不会\"仔细咀嚼\"或\"小口慢咽\"，故排除(C)项和(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q22", "type": "listening", "no": 22, "q": "", "opts": ["By attributing them to current cultural influences", "By attributing them to conservative thinking", "In terms of new genetic findings", "In terms of personal preferences"], "answer": 1, "why": "答案为(B)。句[22]提到，女性在照顾孩子和做家务方面所花的时间仍比男性多。这通常被归因于落后的观念与保守的价值观。(B)项是对录音中attributed to...conservative values 的同义转述，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|(A)项和(C)项在录音中未提及，故均排除；个人喜好是这项新研究得出的结论，并非人们以往普遍持有的看法，故排除(D)项。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q23", "type": "listening", "no": 23, "q": "", "opts": ["Sharing with women the chores they see necessary", "Dividing housework that is demanding for women", "Performing household tasks that they enjoy", "Doing tasks that are physically challenging"], "answer": 2, "why": "答案为(C)。句[23]提到，总体而言，男性和女性都会主动多承担自己喜欢做的家务。举例来说，相比女性，男性更喜欢打理庭院和维护房屋，并倾向于希望对这些家务承担主要责任。(C)项是对录音中take more responsibility for tasks they liked 的同义转述，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|(A)项在录音中未提及，故排除；录音中只是举例男性偏爱庭院打理、房屋维护，不能等同于男性倾向于分担繁重的家务或是偏爱体力上具有挑战性的家务，(B)项和(D)项属于主观臆断，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q24", "type": "listening", "no": 24, "q": "", "opts": ["The large within-group differences", "The great similarities across groups", "The researchers' subjective biases", "The noticeable over generalizations"], "answer": 0, "why": "答案为(A)。句[24]提到，不过我们需要记住，如同任何关于群体差异的研究一样，各群体内部存在较大差异。(A)项是对录音中large within-group differences 的原词复现，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|录音中明确提到，群体内部存在较大差异，而非群体之间高度相似，(B)项与录音内容相悖，故排除；(C)项和(D)项在录音中未提及，故均排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q25", "type": "listening", "no": 25, "q": "", "opts": ["They seem to be universal", "They impact people's whole life", "They shape one's role in society", "They appear at an early age"], "answer": 3, "why": "答案为(D)。句[25]提到，作者指出以往的研究揭示了对待家庭和事业的态度存在性别差异。这种差异早在六岁时就出现了。(D)项是对录音中appear as early as six years old 的同义转述，故为答案。\n|干扰项排除|(A)项和(C)项在录音中未提及，故均排除；录音中只提到这种差异在六岁时就出现了，并未提及这种差异会影响人们的一生，(B)项属于过度延伸，故排除。", "passage": ""}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q46", "type": "reading", "no": 46, "q": "What do we learn from the passage about people who suffer from the so-called Impostor Phenomenon?", "opts": ["They systematically underestimate their chances of success", "They tend to attribute their achievements to good fortune", "They seem to live in constant fear of being deceived", "They generally rely on luck and chance for success"], "answer": 1, "why": "|定位|根据题干中的关键词people who suffer和Impostor Phenomenon可定位到第1段第2句。\n答案为(B)。第1段第2句提到，他们认为任何成功都归因于外界因素，或仅仅是运气和偶然。(B)项是对该句的同义转述，其中attribute their achievements to good fortune对应原文中的any success is due to external circumstances or just luck and chance。\n|干扰项排除|(A)项是根据原文中systematically underestimate themselves and their own performance设置的干扰，但原文说的是这类人系统地低估了自己和自身表现，而不是低估自己成功的概率，故排除；(C)项是根据原文中their \"deception\" will be exposed设置的干扰，但原文指的是害怕自己伪装的一面被揭穿，而不是害怕被他人欺骗，故排除；(D)项是根据原文中due to external circumstances or just luck and chance设置的干扰，但原文指的是他们主观上将成功归功于运气和偶然，而不是他们依靠运气和偶然获取成功，故排除。", "passage": "People who systematically underestimate themselves and their own performance suffer from the so-called Impostor Phenomenon. They think that any success is due to external circumstances or just luck and chance. It is common for people to question their abilities now and again. A healthy amount of reflection and self- doubt can protect a person from acting impulsively. However, there are people who are permanently plagued by a massive amount of self-doubt despite delivering a good performance. They think that all of their successes are not a product of their skill or hard work, instead they attribute their own successes to external circumstances, for example, to luck and chance, or believe that their performance is massively overestimated by others. Failures, on the other hand, are always internalised as the result of their own shortcomings.\n\nPsychologists from Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg examined the topic for the first time under real-life conditions. Seventy-six participants completed a range of intelligence tests and received positive feedback on them, regardless of their actual performance. They were then asked why they think they did so well. The study showed two things: First, the self-reported degree of Impostor Phenomenon is not related to actual measured intelligence or performance. Secondly, the test supported the assumption that people with a tendency to exhibit the Impostor Phenomenon devalue their objectively measured performance and attribute positive results to external causes such as luck and chance, but not to their own abilities. These results are also completely unrelated to age and gender.\n\nA permanent underestimation of one's own abilities is often accompanied by the fear that this supposed intellectual deception will be exposed sooner or later and that they will pay the price for this. The Impostor Phenomenon was first described in 1978 by US psychologists Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes. They observed that there is a particularly high number of successful women who do not think they are very intelligent. The Impostor Phenomenon is not defined as a mental illness. However, people who suffer from it show a higher vulnerability to depression."}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q47", "type": "reading", "no": 47, "q": "What do people with a healthy amount of reflection and self-doubt tend to do?", "opts": ["Credit all their successes to hard work", "Strive hard to perfect their skills", "Refrain from acting on impulse", "Deliver a better performance"], "answer": 2, "why": "|定位|根据题干中的关键词a healthy amount of reflection and self-doubt可定位到第2段第2句。\n答案为(C)。第2段第2句提到，适度的自我反思与自我怀疑能让人避免冲动行事。(C)项是对该句的同义转述，其中Refrain from acting on impulse对应原文中的protect a person from acting impulsively。\n|干扰项排除|(A)项和(B)项在原文中未提及，故均排除；(D)项是根据原文中delivering a good performance设置的干扰，但原文指的是有些人即使表现出色，也依旧深陷自我怀疑之中而不是适度反思的人会表现得更出色，故排除。", "passage": "People who systematically underestimate themselves and their own performance suffer from the so-called Impostor Phenomenon. They think that any success is due to external circumstances or just luck and chance. It is common for people to question their abilities now and again. A healthy amount of reflection and self- doubt can protect a person from acting impulsively. However, there are people who are permanently plagued by a massive amount of self-doubt despite delivering a good performance. They think that all of their successes are not a product of their skill or hard work, instead they attribute their own successes to external circumstances, for example, to luck and chance, or believe that their performance is massively overestimated by others. Failures, on the other hand, are always internalised as the result of their own shortcomings.\n\nPsychologists from Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg examined the topic for the first time under real-life conditions. Seventy-six participants completed a range of intelligence tests and received positive feedback on them, regardless of their actual performance. They were then asked why they think they did so well. The study showed two things: First, the self-reported degree of Impostor Phenomenon is not related to actual measured intelligence or performance. Secondly, the test supported the assumption that people with a tendency to exhibit the Impostor Phenomenon devalue their objectively measured performance and attribute positive results to external causes such as luck and chance, but not to their own abilities. These results are also completely unrelated to age and gender.\n\nA permanent underestimation of one's own abilities is often accompanied by the fear that this supposed intellectual deception will be exposed sooner or later and that they will pay the price for this. The Impostor Phenomenon was first described in 1978 by US psychologists Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes. They observed that there is a particularly high number of successful women who do not think they are very intelligent. The Impostor Phenomenon is not defined as a mental illness. However, people who suffer from it show a higher vulnerability to depression."}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q48", "type": "reading", "no": 48, "q": "What do Impostor Phenomenon sufferers often do according to psychologists from Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg?", "opts": ["Attribute their accomplishments to others", "Deny their actual measured intelligence", "Claim to have confidence in their own abilities", "Mark down their objectively rated performance"], "answer": 3, "why": "|定位|根据题干中的关键词Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg可定位到第4段第2句。\n答案为(D)。第4段第2句提到，其次，研究支持了这样一种假设，即有这类心理倾向的人，会贬低他们经过客观测评过的表现，把好成绩归功于运气和偶然等外部因素，而非自身能力。(D)项是对该句的同义转述，其中Mark down their objectively rated performance对应原文中的devalue their objectively measured performance。\n|干扰项排除|(A)项是根据原文中attribute positive results to external causes设置的干扰，但原文指的是将好成绩归功于运气等外界因素，而不是将成就归功于别人，故排除；(B)项是根据原文中actual measured intelligence设置的干扰，但原文表明该症状程度与实际测量的智力水平无关，而不是患者否认这一智力水平，故排除；(C)项与原文表达的患者对自己能力缺乏自信的核心内容相悖，故排除。", "passage": "People who systematically underestimate themselves and their own performance suffer from the so-called Impostor Phenomenon. They think that any success is due to external circumstances or just luck and chance. It is common for people to question their abilities now and again. A healthy amount of reflection and self- doubt can protect a person from acting impulsively. However, there are people who are permanently plagued by a massive amount of self-doubt despite delivering a good performance. They think that all of their successes are not a product of their skill or hard work, instead they attribute their own successes to external circumstances, for example, to luck and chance, or believe that their performance is massively overestimated by others. Failures, on the other hand, are always internalised as the result of their own shortcomings.\n\nPsychologists from Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg examined the topic for the first time under real-life conditions. Seventy-six participants completed a range of intelligence tests and received positive feedback on them, regardless of their actual performance. They were then asked why they think they did so well. The study showed two things: First, the self-reported degree of Impostor Phenomenon is not related to actual measured intelligence or performance. Secondly, the test supported the assumption that people with a tendency to exhibit the Impostor Phenomenon devalue their objectively measured performance and attribute positive results to external causes such as luck and chance, but not to their own abilities. These results are also completely unrelated to age and gender.\n\nA permanent underestimation of one's own abilities is often accompanied by the fear that this supposed intellectual deception will be exposed sooner or later and that they will pay the price for this. The Impostor Phenomenon was first described in 1978 by US psychologists Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes. They observed that there is a particularly high number of successful women who do not think they are very intelligent. The Impostor Phenomenon is not defined as a mental illness. However, people who suffer from it show a higher vulnerability to depression."}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q49", "type": "reading", "no": 49, "q": "What happens when a person permanently underestimates their own abilities?", "opts": ["They will focus their attention on external factors", "They will be fearful of potential consequences", "They will count on good fortune for success", "They will keep improving their own skills"], "answer": 1, "why": "|定位|根据题干中的关键词permanently underestimates their own abilities可定位到最后一段第1句。\n答案为(B)。最后一段第1句提到，长期低估自己能力的人，往往还会心存顾虑：害怕这种所谓的智力欺诈迟早会被戳穿，并为此付出代价。(B)项是对该句的同义转述，其中fearful of potential consequences对应原文中的fear that... they will pay the price for this。\n|干扰项排除|(A)项是根据原文中attribute their own successes to external circumstances设置的干扰，但原文指的是把成功归功于外部因素，而不是将注意力集中在外部因素上，故排除；(C)项是根据原文中due to external circumstances or just luck and chance设置的干扰，但原文指的是对成功的归因，而不是依靠运气获得成功，故排除；(D)项在原文中未提及，故排除。", "passage": "People who systematically underestimate themselves and their own performance suffer from the so-called Impostor Phenomenon. They think that any success is due to external circumstances or just luck and chance. It is common for people to question their abilities now and again. A healthy amount of reflection and self- doubt can protect a person from acting impulsively. However, there are people who are permanently plagued by a massive amount of self-doubt despite delivering a good performance. They think that all of their successes are not a product of their skill or hard work, instead they attribute their own successes to external circumstances, for example, to luck and chance, or believe that their performance is massively overestimated by others. Failures, on the other hand, are always internalised as the result of their own shortcomings.\n\nPsychologists from Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg examined the topic for the first time under real-life conditions. Seventy-six participants completed a range of intelligence tests and received positive feedback on them, regardless of their actual performance. They were then asked why they think they did so well. The study showed two things: First, the self-reported degree of Impostor Phenomenon is not related to actual measured intelligence or performance. Secondly, the test supported the assumption that people with a tendency to exhibit the Impostor Phenomenon devalue their objectively measured performance and attribute positive results to external causes such as luck and chance, but not to their own abilities. These results are also completely unrelated to age and gender.\n\nA permanent underestimation of one's own abilities is often accompanied by the fear that this supposed intellectual deception will be exposed sooner or later and that they will pay the price for this. The Impostor Phenomenon was first described in 1978 by US psychologists Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes. They observed that there is a particularly high number of successful women who do not think they are very intelligent. The Impostor Phenomenon is not defined as a mental illness. However, people who suffer from it show a higher vulnerability to depression."}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q51", "type": "reading", "no": 51, "q": "In what way is music important?", "opts": ["It is able to create novel and extraordinary emotions", "It has the power to unite people throughout the world", "It allows different cultural groups to sing in harmony", "It is the only language favored by all peoples of the world"], "answer": 1, "why": "|定位|根据题干中的关键词music important和题文同序原则可定位到第1段第3句。\n答案为(B)。第1段第3句提到，它在全球范围内都有着至关重要的作用，并且有能力让人们在平和融洽中凝聚在一起。(B)项是对该句的同义转述。其中the power to unite people对应原文中的the potential to bring people together；throughout the world对应原文中的across the globe。\n|干扰项排除|(A)项是根据原文中express or change emotions设置的干扰，但原文说的是音乐本质上用于\"抒发或改变情绪\"，而不是音乐能够\"创造新奇独特的情绪\"，故排除；(C)项是根据原文中musicians from different cultures设置的干扰，但原文只是说演绎、表演，而不是齐声合唱，故排除；(D)项是根据原文中Music is a universal language设置的干扰，但原文指的是音乐是\"通用语言\"，而不是说它是全世界人民\"唯一喜爱的语言\"，故排除。", "passage": "Music is a universal language. In every culture and country, you will find music. It is vitally important across the globe and has the potential to bring people together in peace and harmony. One of my favorite experiences in music is seeing musicians from different cultures come together and perform as a group. When each culture brings its own unique qualities to the group, an entirely new sound is achieved. This can create extraordinary results. People can communicate with each other using music as the common language.\n\nOne common musical thread between cultures is known as the pentatonic scale. It is astonishing how many cultures share this tool in approaching music. The pentatonic scale consists of five notes; “penta-\"' meaning 5, and “\"tonic\" meaning tone or note. This scale can be found in music everywhere including that of Native Americans, Africans, and Asians. It is also found in popular music, blues, jazz, and rock. Using this five-note scale, musicians from around the globe have a medium through which they can relate, communicate, perform, and ultimately understand each other.\n\nMusic is tied to specific perceptual (感知的), mental, and emotional faculties, including language (all societies put words to their songs), motor control (people, in all societies dance), sound analysis (all musical systems have a distinct pitch and quality), and aesthetics (美感) (their tunes and rhythms are balanced between repetitiveness and chaos).\n\nThere is no known society that existed without music. Although each culture has their own specific style and approach to music, you will find that the purposes behind music are very similar across the board. Music essentially serves to express or change emotions. It can cheer you up, sadden you, anger you or excite you. Music is often ceremonial: used in religion, ceremony, and celebration. Music can simply be recreational: entertaining, joyful and fun. Any way you have used music, it is almost certain that every other culture has used music in the very same way. And that is something that bonds all of us. We all share a deep love for music and it has the power to unite us."}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q52", "type": "reading", "no": 52, "q": "Why does the author like to see musicians from different cultures perform together?", "opts": ["Their musical experience can be compared", "A novel language can be created", "Their unique skills can be displayed", "Fantastic results can be achieved"], "answer": 3, "why": "|定位|根据题干中的关键词musicians from different cultures perform together可定位到第1段第4句。\n答案为(D)。第1段第4句提到，作者喜欢看到来自不同文化的音乐人同台演绎，接着在第5、6句中提到，一种全新的声音便由此产生，这能创造出非同凡响的效果。(D)项是对该句的同义转述。其中Fantastic results对应原文中的extraordinary results；be achieved对应原文中的create。\n|干扰项排除|(A)项是根据原文中experiences in music设置的干扰，但此处指的是与音乐相关的种种体验，而不是\"对比\"他们的音乐阅历，故排除；(B)项是根据原文中the common language设置的干扰，但原文指的是音乐本身就是通用语言，而不是创造出一种新奇的语言，故排除；(C)项是根据原文中their own unique qualities设置的干扰，但原文指的是每种文化都能为团队带来独有的特色，而不是展现他们独有的技艺，故排除。", "passage": "Music is a universal language. In every culture and country, you will find music. It is vitally important across the globe and has the potential to bring people together in peace and harmony. One of my favorite experiences in music is seeing musicians from different cultures come together and perform as a group. When each culture brings its own unique qualities to the group, an entirely new sound is achieved. This can create extraordinary results. People can communicate with each other using music as the common language.\n\nOne common musical thread between cultures is known as the pentatonic scale. It is astonishing how many cultures share this tool in approaching music. The pentatonic scale consists of five notes; “penta-\"' meaning 5, and “\"tonic\" meaning tone or note. This scale can be found in music everywhere including that of Native Americans, Africans, and Asians. It is also found in popular music, blues, jazz, and rock. Using this five-note scale, musicians from around the globe have a medium through which they can relate, communicate, perform, and ultimately understand each other.\n\nMusic is tied to specific perceptual (感知的), mental, and emotional faculties, including language (all societies put words to their songs), motor control (people, in all societies dance), sound analysis (all musical systems have a distinct pitch and quality), and aesthetics (美感) (their tunes and rhythms are balanced between repetitiveness and chaos).\n\nThere is no known society that existed without music. Although each culture has their own specific style and approach to music, you will find that the purposes behind music are very similar across the board. Music essentially serves to express or change emotions. It can cheer you up, sadden you, anger you or excite you. Music is often ceremonial: used in religion, ceremony, and celebration. Music can simply be recreational: entertaining, joyful and fun. Any way you have used music, it is almost certain that every other culture has used music in the very same way. And that is something that bonds all of us. We all share a deep love for music and it has the power to unite us."}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q53", "type": "reading", "no": 53, "q": "What does the passage say about the pentatonic scale?", "opts": ["It is present in music all around the world", "It is helpful to understanding music", "It is the basis for musicians' creation", "It is a medium for ordinary people to relate"], "answer": 0, "why": "|定位|根据题干中的关键词the pentatonic scale可定位到第2段第4句。\n答案为(A)。第2段第4句提到，这种音阶在各地的音乐中都能见到。(A)项是对该句的同义转述。其中It is present对应原文中的be found；in music all around the world对应原文中的in music everywhere。\n|干扰项排除|(B)项是根据原文中understand each other设置的干扰，但原文说的是音乐人最终实现彼此理解，而不是理解音乐，故排除；原文仅提到诸多文化在音乐创作中都会使用该音阶，并未将其定义为音乐人创作的基础，(C)项属于过度解读，故排除；(D)项是根据原文中a medium through which they can relate设置的干扰，但原文指的是这是音乐人建立联结的媒介，而不是普通民众建立联结的媒介，故排除。", "passage": "Music is a universal language. In every culture and country, you will find music. It is vitally important across the globe and has the potential to bring people together in peace and harmony. One of my favorite experiences in music is seeing musicians from different cultures come together and perform as a group. When each culture brings its own unique qualities to the group, an entirely new sound is achieved. This can create extraordinary results. People can communicate with each other using music as the common language.\n\nOne common musical thread between cultures is known as the pentatonic scale. It is astonishing how many cultures share this tool in approaching music. The pentatonic scale consists of five notes; “penta-\"' meaning 5, and “\"tonic\" meaning tone or note. This scale can be found in music everywhere including that of Native Americans, Africans, and Asians. It is also found in popular music, blues, jazz, and rock. Using this five-note scale, musicians from around the globe have a medium through which they can relate, communicate, perform, and ultimately understand each other.\n\nMusic is tied to specific perceptual (感知的), mental, and emotional faculties, including language (all societies put words to their songs), motor control (people, in all societies dance), sound analysis (all musical systems have a distinct pitch and quality), and aesthetics (美感) (their tunes and rhythms are balanced between repetitiveness and chaos).\n\nThere is no known society that existed without music. Although each culture has their own specific style and approach to music, you will find that the purposes behind music are very similar across the board. Music essentially serves to express or change emotions. It can cheer you up, sadden you, anger you or excite you. Music is often ceremonial: used in religion, ceremony, and celebration. Music can simply be recreational: entertaining, joyful and fun. Any way you have used music, it is almost certain that every other culture has used music in the very same way. And that is something that bonds all of us. We all share a deep love for music and it has the power to unite us."}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q54", "type": "reading", "no": 54, "q": "What is the fundamental role of music in people's life? 17", "opts": ["To bring them together", "To show their identity", "To express their emotions", "To keep them entertained"], "answer": 2, "why": "|定位|根据题干中的关键词fundamental role of music可定位到最后一段第3句。\n答案为(C)。最后一段第3句提到，音乐本质上用于抒发或改变情绪。(C)项是对该句的同义转述。其中express和emotions为原文信息再现。\n|干扰项排除|(A)项是根据原文中bring people together设置的干扰，但原文中的\"音乐也拥有团结所有人的力量\"只是音乐的附加作用，并非音乐的根本作用，故排除；(B)项在原文中无对应内容，文中并未提及音乐用来展现身份，故排除；(D)项是根据原文中It can cheer you up设置的干扰，但原文中提到的\"音乐让人心情愉悦\"只是音乐所能带来的情绪变化之一，表述片面，并非根本作用，故排除。", "passage": "Music is a universal language. In every culture and country, you will find music. It is vitally important across the globe and has the potential to bring people together in peace and harmony. One of my favorite experiences in music is seeing musicians from different cultures come together and perform as a group. When each culture brings its own unique qualities to the group, an entirely new sound is achieved. This can create extraordinary results. People can communicate with each other using music as the common language.\n\nOne common musical thread between cultures is known as the pentatonic scale. It is astonishing how many cultures share this tool in approaching music. The pentatonic scale consists of five notes; “penta-\"' meaning 5, and “\"tonic\" meaning tone or note. This scale can be found in music everywhere including that of Native Americans, Africans, and Asians. It is also found in popular music, blues, jazz, and rock. Using this five-note scale, musicians from around the globe have a medium through which they can relate, communicate, perform, and ultimately understand each other.\n\nMusic is tied to specific perceptual (感知的), mental, and emotional faculties, including language (all societies put words to their songs), motor control (people, in all societies dance), sound analysis (all musical systems have a distinct pitch and quality), and aesthetics (美感) (their tunes and rhythms are balanced between repetitiveness and chaos).\n\nThere is no known society that existed without music. Although each culture has their own specific style and approach to music, you will find that the purposes behind music are very similar across the board. Music essentially serves to express or change emotions. It can cheer you up, sadden you, anger you or excite you. Music is often ceremonial: used in religion, ceremony, and celebration. Music can simply be recreational: entertaining, joyful and fun. Any way you have used music, it is almost certain that every other culture has used music in the very same way. And that is something that bonds all of us. We all share a deep love for music and it has the power to unite us."}, {"id": "CET4-2-Q55", "type": "reading", "no": 55, "q": "In what aspect is music similar throughout the world?", "opts": ["The way people respond to it", "The role it plays in society", "The joy it brings to people", "The way it is made use of"], "answer": 3, "why": "|定位|根据题干中的关键词similar、music around the world可定位到最后一段第7句。\n答案为(D)。最后一段第7句提到，无论你以何种方式使用音乐，几乎可以肯定，其他所有文化也都以同样的方式运用音乐。(D)项是对该句的同义转述。其中The way it is made use of对应原文中的used music in the very same way。\n|干扰项排除|(A)项是根据原文中It can cheer you up, sadden you, anger you or excite you设置的干扰，但原文仅说明音乐能引发多种情绪，而非人们回应音乐的方式具有共性，故排除；(B)项是根据原文中used in religion, ceremony, and celebration设置的干扰，但原文指的是音乐的使用途径，而非统一的社会角色定位，故排除；(C)项是根据原文中entertaining, joyful and fun设置的干扰，但原文说明音乐同时能带来悲伤、愤怒等情绪，欢乐只是其中一种效果，无法概括全球音乐的共性，故排除。", "passage": "Music is a universal language. In every culture and country, you will find music. It is vitally important across the globe and has the potential to bring people together in peace and harmony. One of my favorite experiences in music is seeing musicians from different cultures come together and perform as a group. When each culture brings its own unique qualities to the group, an entirely new sound is achieved. This can create extraordinary results. People can communicate with each other using music as the common language.\n\nOne common musical thread between cultures is known as the pentatonic scale. It is astonishing how many cultures share this tool in approaching music. The pentatonic scale consists of five notes; “penta-\"' meaning 5, and “\"tonic\" meaning tone or note. This scale can be found in music everywhere including that of Native Americans, Africans, and Asians. It is also found in popular music, blues, jazz, and rock. Using this five-note scale, musicians from around the globe have a medium through which they can relate, communicate, perform, and ultimately understand each other.\n\nMusic is tied to specific perceptual (感知的), mental, and emotional faculties, including language (all societies put words to their songs), motor control (people, in all societies dance), sound analysis (all musical systems have a distinct pitch and quality), and aesthetics (美感) (their tunes and rhythms are balanced between repetitiveness and chaos).\n\nThere is no known society that existed without music. Although each culture has their own specific style and approach to music, you will find that the purposes behind music are very similar across the board. Music essentially serves to express or change emotions. It can cheer you up, sadden you, anger you or excite you. Music is often ceremonial: used in religion, ceremony, and celebration. Music can simply be recreational: entertaining, joyful and fun. Any way you have used music, it is almost certain that every other culture has used music in the very same way. And that is something that bonds all of us. We all share a deep love for music and it has the power to unite us."}]}, {"id": "CET4-3", "level": "CET4", "set": 3, "year": 2026, "month": 6, "title": "四级 2026年6月 第3套", "audio": "", "minutes": 15, "questions": [{"id": "CET4-3-Q46", "type": "reading", "no": 46, "q": "What does the passage tell us about physical disgust?", "opts": ["It is an emotion as contrasted with thoughts", "It is a concept beyond children's understanding", "It is a mechanism to keep us from being harmed", "It is an experience gained from any form of danger"], "answer": 2, "why": "|定位| 根据题干中的关键词physical disgust可定位到第1段第2句。\n|答案| 答案为(C)。第1段第2句提到，其中一类叫作生理厌恶，这种情绪能让我们避开食用或触碰生肉、昆虫等可能危害健康的东西。(C)项是对该句的同义转述。其中keep us from being harmed对应原文protects us from... that could be dangerous for us。\n|干扰项排除| (A)项在文中未提及，故排除；(B)项是根据原文do not achieve an understanding of the concept设置的干扰，但原文说的是孩子在四岁左右才能真正理解这一概念，并非理解不了，故排除；(D)项是根据原文things that could be dangerous for us设置的干扰，但原文是说生理厌恶是用来规避危险事物的保护机制，并非从各类危险中获取的体验，故排除。", "passage": "Disgust is a universal human emotion. One type of disgust is called physical disgust, which protects us from eating or touching things that could be dangerous for us, such as raw meat or insects. Children experience physical disgust from a very early age, but do not achieve an understanding of the concept until they are around four years old.\n\nSome researchers maintain that there is another type of disgust, called moral disgust, that protects us from engaging in activities that can be perceived as morally wrong. Examples of moral disgust are stealing, lying or cheating.\n\nHowever, not all academics think that moral disgust really exists. Some say that when we refer to disgust in relation to moral issues, we are using disgust as a metaphor (隐喻), and what we are feeling is in reality anger. If moral disgust appears quite late in development, it makes sense to think that there is actually a social component to it. Children learn about emotions in many different ways. Of special relevance are parents-children conversations about emotions. Indeed, children whose parents talk about emotions often have a better emotional understanding than children whose parents do not discuss emotions often.\n\nHowever, there are no studies that have examined how parents and children talk about disgust. This is what we set up to examine in our study.\n\nWe asked 68 English-speaking mothers and their four-, six- or eight-year-old children to discuss some stories about moral and physical disgust. Our preliminary findings show mothers and children do talk about disgust, but mainly about physical disgust and not so much about moral disgust.\n\nWhen we asked mothers and children to discuss moral issues, such as whether it is OK to play with a child who has stolen someone else's snack, they linked this type of moral wrongdoing with the emotion of anger and not disgust.\n\nDoes this mean that moral disgust does not exist? Some researchers suggest moral disgust exists in adults. Our findings suggest that moral disgust is either understood at a later age or is only used metaphorically, if at all, in children as old as eight years."}, {"id": "CET4-3-Q47", "type": "reading", "no": 47, "q": "How do some researchers perceive moral disgust?", "opts": ["It tends to appear in various forms of wrongdoing", "It is just as annoying to people as physical disgust", "It is a term used by common folk to describe anger", "It stops people from doing anything morally wrong"], "answer": 3, "why": "|定位| 根据题干中的关键词some researchers可定位到第2段第1句。\n|答案| 答案为(D)。第2段第1句提到，一些研究者认为，还存在另一种厌恶情绪，叫作道德厌恶，它能约束我们不去做那些会被视作违背道德的事。(D)项是对该句的同义转述，其中stops people from doing anything morally wrong对应原文protects us from engaging in activities that can be perceived as morally wrong。\n|干扰项排除| 原文中提到，错误行为（如偷窃、撒谎）是引发道德厌恶的原因，而不是道德厌恶这种情绪本身的表现形式，故排除(A)项；原文并未对比人们对生理厌恶与道德厌恶的反感程度，故排除(B)项；原文说的是一些学者认为人们所说的道德厌恶其实是愤怒，而不是普通人用该词描述愤怒，故排除(C)项。", "passage": "Disgust is a universal human emotion. One type of disgust is called physical disgust, which protects us from eating or touching things that could be dangerous for us, such as raw meat or insects. Children experience physical disgust from a very early age, but do not achieve an understanding of the concept until they are around four years old.\n\nSome researchers maintain that there is another type of disgust, called moral disgust, that protects us from engaging in activities that can be perceived as morally wrong. Examples of moral disgust are stealing, lying or cheating.\n\nHowever, not all academics think that moral disgust really exists. Some say that when we refer to disgust in relation to moral issues, we are using disgust as a metaphor (隐喻), and what we are feeling is in reality anger. If moral disgust appears quite late in development, it makes sense to think that there is actually a social component to it. Children learn about emotions in many different ways. Of special relevance are parents-children conversations about emotions. Indeed, children whose parents talk about emotions often have a better emotional understanding than children whose parents do not discuss emotions often.\n\nHowever, there are no studies that have examined how parents and children talk about disgust. This is what we set up to examine in our study.\n\nWe asked 68 English-speaking mothers and their four-, six- or eight-year-old children to discuss some stories about moral and physical disgust. Our preliminary findings show mothers and children do talk about disgust, but mainly about physical disgust and not so much about moral disgust.\n\nWhen we asked mothers and children to discuss moral issues, such as whether it is OK to play with a child who has stolen someone else's snack, they linked this type of moral wrongdoing with the emotion of anger and not disgust.\n\nDoes this mean that moral disgust does not exist? Some researchers suggest moral disgust exists in adults. Our findings suggest that moral disgust is either understood at a later age or is only used metaphorically, if at all, in children as old as eight years."}, {"id": "CET4-3-Q48", "type": "reading", "no": 48, "q": "Why does moral disgust appear quite late in children's development?", "opts": ["It needs to be learnt through social interactions", "They have little control over its learning process", "It is too complex for them to acquire", "Their parents seldom talk about emotions"], "answer": 0, "why": "|定位| 根据题干中的关键词appear quite late和children's development可定位到第4段第1-3句。\n|答案| 答案为(A)。第4段第1-3句提到，如果道德厌恶在人的成长过程中出现得很晚，那就说明这种情绪具备社会属性。孩童会通过多种途径认知各类情绪。其中亲子间关于情绪的沟通尤为关键。(A)项是对该信息的同义转述，其中learnt through social interactions对应原文social component和parent-children conversations。\n|干扰项排除| (B)项是根据原文Children learn about emotions设置的干扰，但原文说的是儿童习得情绪，而非无法掌控过程，故排除；原文仅点明道德厌恶具备社会属性，并未说明该情绪过于复杂、难以习得，故排除(C)项；(D)项是根据原文parents talk about emotions设置的干扰，但原文是指亲子情绪交流会影响情绪认知，并非原因，故排除。", "passage": "Disgust is a universal human emotion. One type of disgust is called physical disgust, which protects us from eating or touching things that could be dangerous for us, such as raw meat or insects. Children experience physical disgust from a very early age, but do not achieve an understanding of the concept until they are around four years old.\n\nSome researchers maintain that there is another type of disgust, called moral disgust, that protects us from engaging in activities that can be perceived as morally wrong. Examples of moral disgust are stealing, lying or cheating.\n\nHowever, not all academics think that moral disgust really exists. Some say that when we refer to disgust in relation to moral issues, we are using disgust as a metaphor (隐喻), and what we are feeling is in reality anger. If moral disgust appears quite late in development, it makes sense to think that there is actually a social component to it. Children learn about emotions in many different ways. Of special relevance are parents-children conversations about emotions. Indeed, children whose parents talk about emotions often have a better emotional understanding than children whose parents do not discuss emotions often.\n\nHowever, there are no studies that have examined how parents and children talk about disgust. This is what we set up to examine in our study.\n\nWe asked 68 English-speaking mothers and their four-, six- or eight-year-old children to discuss some stories about moral and physical disgust. Our preliminary findings show mothers and children do talk about disgust, but mainly about physical disgust and not so much about moral disgust.\n\nWhen we asked mothers and children to discuss moral issues, such as whether it is OK to play with a child who has stolen someone else's snack, they linked this type of moral wrongdoing with the emotion of anger and not disgust.\n\nDoes this mean that moral disgust does not exist? Some researchers suggest moral disgust exists in adults. Our findings suggest that moral disgust is either understood at a later age or is only used metaphorically, if at all, in children as old as eight years."}, {"id": "CET4-3-Q49", "type": "reading", "no": 49, "q": "What did the author and his colleagues want to examine in their experiment?", "opts": ["What parents tell children against moral wrongdoing", "What parents and children link moral wrongdoing to", "How moral disgust is seen by children aged 4, 6 and 8", "How disgust is discussed between parents and children"], "answer": 3, "why": "|定位| 根据题干中的关键词the author and his colleagues和examine可定位到第5段。\n|答案| 答案为(D)。第5段提到，但目前暂无研究调查过亲子之间如何谈论\"厌恶\"这种情绪。这也是我们本次研究想要探究的内容。(D)项是对该信息的同义转述，其中How disgust is discussed between parents and children对应原文how parents and children talk about disgust。\n|干扰项排除| (A)项是根据原文moral wrongdoing设置的干扰，但实验重点不是父母的告诫话语，故排除；(B)项是根据原文将不道德行为与愤怒情绪联系在一起设置的干扰，这并非实验研究初衷，故排除；原文仅研究关于厌恶的对话，并未专门调研低龄儿童对道德厌恶的看法，故排除(C)项。", "passage": "Disgust is a universal human emotion. One type of disgust is called physical disgust, which protects us from eating or touching things that could be dangerous for us, such as raw meat or insects. Children experience physical disgust from a very early age, but do not achieve an understanding of the concept until they are around four years old.\n\nSome researchers maintain that there is another type of disgust, called moral disgust, that protects us from engaging in activities that can be perceived as morally wrong. Examples of moral disgust are stealing, lying or cheating.\n\nHowever, not all academics think that moral disgust really exists. Some say that when we refer to disgust in relation to moral issues, we are using disgust as a metaphor (隐喻), and what we are feeling is in reality anger. If moral disgust appears quite late in development, it makes sense to think that there is actually a social component to it. Children learn about emotions in many different ways. Of special relevance are parents-children conversations about emotions. Indeed, children whose parents talk about emotions often have a better emotional understanding than children whose parents do not discuss emotions often.\n\nHowever, there are no studies that have examined how parents and children talk about disgust. This is what we set up to examine in our study.\n\nWe asked 68 English-speaking mothers and their four-, six- or eight-year-old children to discuss some stories about moral and physical disgust. Our preliminary findings show mothers and children do talk about disgust, but mainly about physical disgust and not so much about moral disgust.\n\nWhen we asked mothers and children to discuss moral issues, such as whether it is OK to play with a child who has stolen someone else's snack, they linked this type of moral wrongdoing with the emotion of anger and not disgust.\n\nDoes this mean that moral disgust does not exist? Some researchers suggest moral disgust exists in adults. Our findings suggest that moral disgust is either understood at a later age or is only used metaphorically, if at all, in children as old as eight years."}, {"id": "CET4-3-Q50", "type": "reading", "no": 50, "q": "What can we infer about moral disgust from the author and his colleagues' findings?", "opts": ["It is a notion hardly comprehensible to children", "It rarely exists in children younger than eight", "It is a term most parents use when talking to children", "It exists in adults with a strong sense of justice"], "answer": 0, "why": "|定位| 根据题干中的关键词moral disgust和findings可定位到最后一段最后一句。\n|答案| 答案为(A)。最后一段最后一句提到，要么年龄更大些道德厌恶才能被理解，要么即便到了8岁，也只会以隐喻的方式来使用，如果他们能理解的话。(A)项是对该句的同义转述，其中hardly comprehensible to children对应原文is either understood at a later age or is only used metaphorically...。\n|干扰项排除| (B)项是依据文中儿童理解道德厌恶年龄偏晚设置的干扰，但原文并未表明8岁以下儿童几乎不存在该情绪，表述过于绝对，故排除；原文明确提到亲子间极少谈及道德厌恶，(C)项与原文相悖，故排除；原文仅说明成年人身上存在道德厌恶，并未限定正义感强的成年人，(D)项属于过度解读，故排除。", "passage": "Disgust is a universal human emotion. One type of disgust is called physical disgust, which protects us from eating or touching things that could be dangerous for us, such as raw meat or insects. Children experience physical disgust from a very early age, but do not achieve an understanding of the concept until they are around four years old.\n\nSome researchers maintain that there is another type of disgust, called moral disgust, that protects us from engaging in activities that can be perceived as morally wrong. Examples of moral disgust are stealing, lying or cheating.\n\nHowever, not all academics think that moral disgust really exists. Some say that when we refer to disgust in relation to moral issues, we are using disgust as a metaphor (隐喻), and what we are feeling is in reality anger. If moral disgust appears quite late in development, it makes sense to think that there is actually a social component to it. Children learn about emotions in many different ways. Of special relevance are parents-children conversations about emotions. Indeed, children whose parents talk about emotions often have a better emotional understanding than children whose parents do not discuss emotions often.\n\nHowever, there are no studies that have examined how parents and children talk about disgust. This is what we set up to examine in our study.\n\nWe asked 68 English-speaking mothers and their four-, six- or eight-year-old children to discuss some stories about moral and physical disgust. Our preliminary findings show mothers and children do talk about disgust, but mainly about physical disgust and not so much about moral disgust.\n\nWhen we asked mothers and children to discuss moral issues, such as whether it is OK to play with a child who has stolen someone else's snack, they linked this type of moral wrongdoing with the emotion of anger and not disgust.\n\nDoes this mean that moral disgust does not exist? Some researchers suggest moral disgust exists in adults. Our findings suggest that moral disgust is either understood at a later age or is only used metaphorically, if at all, in children as old as eight years."}, {"id": "CET4-3-Q51", "type": "reading", "no": 51, "q": "What is the finding of a recent study in Nature?", "opts": ["People are more honest with those who know them than with total strangers", "People we want to have detailed information about are keen to know about us", "People who get to know about a person wrongly assume the person knows them too", "People who are familiar with each other tend to behave honestly in their everyday dealings"], "answer": 2, "why": "|定位| 根据题干中的关键词a recent study in Nature可定位到第1段。\n|答案| 答案为(C)。第1段第1句提到，发表于《自然》杂志的一项近期研究发现，当我们了解到一个陌生人的详细信息后，会误以为对方也了解我们。(C)项是对该句的同义转述，其中get to know about a person对应原文中的finding out details about a stranger；wrongly assume the person knows them too对应原文中的mistakenly think that they also know about us。\n|干扰项排除| (A)项是依据文中的we act more honestly around them设置的干扰，但文中说的是由于\"误以为对方了解自己\"，因此会表现得更加诚实；(B)项是依据文中的After finding out details about a stranger设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"我们了解到一个陌生人的详细信息后\"，而不是\"我们想深入了解的人也渴望了解我们\"，故排除；(D)项是依据文中we act more honestly around them设置的干扰，但文中说的是人们会\"在这类人面前表现得更加诚实\"，而不是\"彼此熟悉的人在日常交往中往往表现得更诚实\"，故排除。", "passage": "After finding out details about a stranger, we mistakenly think that they also know about us. As a result, we act more honestly around them, according to a recent study in Nature.\n\nPast research has found that we tend to assume social relationships are reciprocal (相互的).Most of the time, this assumption is accurate: someone you think of as a friend will usually consider you a friend too. But sometimes our social ties are more one-sided: for example, you might learn something about a stranger who doesn't know you at all. Researchers Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest wondered whether our tendency to believe that social ties are reciprocal could lead us to mistakenly feel that a stranger does actually know us. In a series of lab-based studies, this is exactly what the researchers found. When we know about strangers, we erroneously think they also know about us. But the really interesting finding came when the researchers stepped out of the lab, to conduct a field experiment. The team developed leaflets (传单) containing fairly ordinary information about local community police officers, such as their favourite food, hobbies, or reasons for joining the police force. They then sent out these leaflets to every apartment in a number of housing developments in disadvantaged areas. The officers themselves also dropped off cards to local residents containing similar information.\n\nTwo months later, the researchers surveyed residents at these housing developments as well as control areas that did not receive leaflets or outreach cards. Residents were asked to imagine that they had committed a crime and how likely it would be that local officers would find out about it. They also rated how well the officers in the area knew them.\n\nThe team found that residents of the developments that had received the leaflets believed it more likely that local officers would find out about illegal activity than residents of the control areas. Even more strikingly, the team found that immediately after the leaflet distribution, there were fewer criminal complaints and arrests in the areas around the developments that had received the leaflets than around the control developments."}, {"id": "CET4-3-Q52", "type": "reading", "no": 52, "q": "What does the passage say about the assumption that social relationships are reciprocal?", "opts": ["It is more the exception than the rule", "It does not hold true in every situation", "It does not apply to complex relationships", "It is more accurate in one-sided social ties"], "answer": 1, "why": "|定位| 根据题干中的关键词assumption、social relationships和reciprocal可定位到第2段。\n|答案| 答案为(B)。第2段第1-3句提到，以往研究发现，人们倾向于认为社会关系是相互的。大多数情况下，这种假设是准确的：你把某人当作朋友，对方通常也会把你当作朋友。然而，有时我们的社会关系却更为单向化：例如，你可能了解到一个陌生人的情况，而对方却对你一无所知。(B)项是对以上信息的概括转述，其中does not hold true in every situation对应原文中的Most of the time, this assumption is accurate和sometimes our social ties are more one-sided。\n|干扰项排除| (A)项是依据文中this assumption is accurate设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"大多数情况下，这种假设是准确的\"，而不是\"它更多是一种例外，而不是普遍规律\"，故排除；文中并未讨论这种假设是否适用于复杂的关系，故排除(C)项；(D)项是依据文中our social ties are more one-sided设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"有时社会关系更为单向化\"，因此这一假设就不成立了，而不是\"在单向社会关系中，它更加准确\"，故排除。", "passage": "After finding out details about a stranger, we mistakenly think that they also know about us. As a result, we act more honestly around them, according to a recent study in Nature.\n\nPast research has found that we tend to assume social relationships are reciprocal (相互的).Most of the time, this assumption is accurate: someone you think of as a friend will usually consider you a friend too. But sometimes our social ties are more one-sided: for example, you might learn something about a stranger who doesn't know you at all. Researchers Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest wondered whether our tendency to believe that social ties are reciprocal could lead us to mistakenly feel that a stranger does actually know us. In a series of lab-based studies, this is exactly what the researchers found. When we know about strangers, we erroneously think they also know about us. But the really interesting finding came when the researchers stepped out of the lab, to conduct a field experiment. The team developed leaflets (传单) containing fairly ordinary information about local community police officers, such as their favourite food, hobbies, or reasons for joining the police force. They then sent out these leaflets to every apartment in a number of housing developments in disadvantaged areas. The officers themselves also dropped off cards to local residents containing similar information.\n\nTwo months later, the researchers surveyed residents at these housing developments as well as control areas that did not receive leaflets or outreach cards. Residents were asked to imagine that they had committed a crime and how likely it would be that local officers would find out about it. They also rated how well the officers in the area knew them.\n\nThe team found that residents of the developments that had received the leaflets believed it more likely that local officers would find out about illegal activity than residents of the control areas. Even more strikingly, the team found that immediately after the leaflet distribution, there were fewer criminal complaints and arrests in the areas around the developments that had received the leaflets than around the control developments."}, {"id": "CET4-3-Q53", "type": "reading", "no": 53, "q": "What did Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest want to find out about the belief that social relationships are reciprocal?", "opts": ["Whether it may resolve misunderstandings about social ties", "Whether it could help develop one's relationships with strangers", "Whether it could help strengthen interpersonal relationships", "Whether it may lead to false assumptions about a stranger"], "answer": 3, "why": "|定位| 根据题干中的关键词Anuj Shah和Michael LaForest可定位到第2段最后一句。\n|答案| 答案为(D)。第2段最后一句提到，研究人员阿努杰·沙阿和迈克尔·拉福雷斯特想弄清楚：我们倾向于相信社会关系是相互的这一观念，是否会导致我们错误地觉得一个陌生人实际上也了解自己。(D)项是对该句的概括转述，其中false assumptions about a stranger对应原文中的mistakenly feel that a stranger does actually know us。\n|干扰项排除| (A)项属于无中生有，文中没有提到这种观念是否能够化解社会关系中的误解，故排除；(B)项属于无中生有，文中没有讨论这种观念是否有助于人与陌生人建立关系，故排除；文中没有提到这种观念是否有助于加强人际关系，故排除(C)项。", "passage": "After finding out details about a stranger, we mistakenly think that they also know about us. As a result, we act more honestly around them, according to a recent study in Nature.\n\nPast research has found that we tend to assume social relationships are reciprocal (相互的).Most of the time, this assumption is accurate: someone you think of as a friend will usually consider you a friend too. But sometimes our social ties are more one-sided: for example, you might learn something about a stranger who doesn't know you at all. Researchers Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest wondered whether our tendency to believe that social ties are reciprocal could lead us to mistakenly feel that a stranger does actually know us. In a series of lab-based studies, this is exactly what the researchers found. When we know about strangers, we erroneously think they also know about us. But the really interesting finding came when the researchers stepped out of the lab, to conduct a field experiment. The team developed leaflets (传单) containing fairly ordinary information about local community police officers, such as their favourite food, hobbies, or reasons for joining the police force. They then sent out these leaflets to every apartment in a number of housing developments in disadvantaged areas. The officers themselves also dropped off cards to local residents containing similar information.\n\nTwo months later, the researchers surveyed residents at these housing developments as well as control areas that did not receive leaflets or outreach cards. Residents were asked to imagine that they had committed a crime and how likely it would be that local officers would find out about it. They also rated how well the officers in the area knew them.\n\nThe team found that residents of the developments that had received the leaflets believed it more likely that local officers would find out about illegal activity than residents of the control areas. Even more strikingly, the team found that immediately after the leaflet distribution, there were fewer criminal complaints and arrests in the areas around the developments that had received the leaflets than around the control developments."}, {"id": "CET4-3-Q54", "type": "reading", "no": 54, "q": "When did Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest find something really interesting in their research?", "opts": ["During lab-based studies", "During a field experiment", "While investigating local residents' crime", "While talking to police officers nearby"], "answer": 1, "why": "|定位| 根据题干中的关键词really interesting可定位到第3段第3句。\n|答案| 答案为(B)。第3段第3句提到，不过，真正有趣的发现出现在研究人员走出实验室、开展实地实验之时。(B)项是对该句的同义转述，其中during a field experiment对应原文中的when... to conduct a field experiment。\n|干扰项排除| (A)项是依据文中In a series of lab-based studies, this is exactly what the researchers found设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"实验室研究验证了研究假设\"，而真正有趣的发现出现在后面的实地实验中，故排除；(C)项是依据文中Residents were asked to imagine that they had committed a crime设置的干扰，但文中只是让居民设想自己犯下罪行，而不是调查当地居民的犯罪情况，故排除；(D)项属于无中生有，文中没有提到研究人员与附近警察交谈，故排除。", "passage": "After finding out details about a stranger, we mistakenly think that they also know about us. As a result, we act more honestly around them, according to a recent study in Nature.\n\nPast research has found that we tend to assume social relationships are reciprocal (相互的).Most of the time, this assumption is accurate: someone you think of as a friend will usually consider you a friend too. But sometimes our social ties are more one-sided: for example, you might learn something about a stranger who doesn't know you at all. Researchers Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest wondered whether our tendency to believe that social ties are reciprocal could lead us to mistakenly feel that a stranger does actually know us. In a series of lab-based studies, this is exactly what the researchers found. When we know about strangers, we erroneously think they also know about us. But the really interesting finding came when the researchers stepped out of the lab, to conduct a field experiment. The team developed leaflets (传单) containing fairly ordinary information about local community police officers, such as their favourite food, hobbies, or reasons for joining the police force. They then sent out these leaflets to every apartment in a number of housing developments in disadvantaged areas. The officers themselves also dropped off cards to local residents containing similar information.\n\nTwo months later, the researchers surveyed residents at these housing developments as well as control areas that did not receive leaflets or outreach cards. Residents were asked to imagine that they had committed a crime and how likely it would be that local officers would find out about it. They also rated how well the officers in the area knew them.\n\nThe team found that residents of the developments that had received the leaflets believed it more likely that local officers would find out about illegal activity than residents of the control areas. Even more strikingly, the team found that immediately after the leaflet distribution, there were fewer criminal complaints and arrests in the areas around the developments that had received the leaflets than around the control developments."}, {"id": "CET4-3-Q55", "type": "reading", "no": 55, "q": "What happened in the areas around the housing developments subjected to the studies?", "opts": ["They saw fewer crimes than the control developments", "They witnessed a swifter elimination of illegal activities", "Local police responded more quickly to criminal complaints than before", "Local police related better to residents there than to those in other areas"], "answer": 0, "why": "|定位| 根据题干中的关键词areas around the housing development可定位到最后一段最后一句。\n|答案| 答案为(A)。最后一段最后一句提到，更值得注意的是，研究团队还发现，在传单发放后不久，那些收到传单的社区周边地区的犯罪投诉和逮捕案件数量，都少于对照社区周边地区。(A)项是对该句的概括转述，其中fewer crimes对应原文中的fewer criminal complaints and arrests。\n|干扰项排除| (B)项是依据文中的fewer criminal complaints and arrests设置的干扰，但文中说的是\"犯罪投诉和逮捕案件数量更少\"，而不是\"违法活动被更迅速地消除\"，故排除；文中没有提到当地警方对犯罪投诉的响应速度比以前更快，故排除(C)项；(D)项是依据文中的They also rated how well the officers in the area knew them设置的干扰，但文中只是调查居民认为警方对自己了解的程度，并未说明当地警方与居民之间的关系比其他地区更加融洽，故排除。", "passage": "After finding out details about a stranger, we mistakenly think that they also know about us. As a result, we act more honestly around them, according to a recent study in Nature.\n\nPast research has found that we tend to assume social relationships are reciprocal (相互的).Most of the time, this assumption is accurate: someone you think of as a friend will usually consider you a friend too. But sometimes our social ties are more one-sided: for example, you might learn something about a stranger who doesn't know you at all. Researchers Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest wondered whether our tendency to believe that social ties are reciprocal could lead us to mistakenly feel that a stranger does actually know us. In a series of lab-based studies, this is exactly what the researchers found. When we know about strangers, we erroneously think they also know about us. But the really interesting finding came when the researchers stepped out of the lab, to conduct a field experiment. The team developed leaflets (传单) containing fairly ordinary information about local community police officers, such as their favourite food, hobbies, or reasons for joining the police force. They then sent out these leaflets to every apartment in a number of housing developments in disadvantaged areas. The officers themselves also dropped off cards to local residents containing similar information.\n\nTwo months later, the researchers surveyed residents at these housing developments as well as control areas that did not receive leaflets or outreach cards. Residents were asked to imagine that they had committed a crime and how likely it would be that local officers would find out about it. They also rated how well the officers in the area knew them.\n\nThe team found that residents of the developments that had received the leaflets believed it more likely that local officers would find out about illegal activity than residents of the control areas. Even more strikingly, the team found that immediately after the leaflet distribution, there were fewer criminal complaints and arrests in the areas around the developments that had received the leaflets than around the control developments."}]}];
